\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar set for fifth winning week on Fed bets, China worries\b0\par\par\uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2023\par 08:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The dollar was set for a fifth winning week versus major peers, making it longest winning streak for 15 months, buoyed by expectations U.S. interest rates will stay high for longer, and by a shift to safer assets due to worries over China's economy.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, however, the dollar trimmed those gains slightly as its rally against the yen kept traders on edge against the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities.\par \par \uc2 A quickening in the depreciation for China's yuan also appeared to be a concern for authorities in Beijing, as the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  set a much-stronger-than-expected daily fixing, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  some early support after it struck 9-month lows a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index - which measures the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against six developed-market rivals, including the yen and euro - eased 0.02% to 103.38 in the Asian day, after touching a two-month high at 103.59 overnight.\par \par \uc2 For the week, it is set to gain 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, minutes from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's last meeting showed most members of the rate-setting committee continued to see "significant upside risks to inflation," suggesting a bias toward further rate increases.\par \par \uc2 Strong economic data this week, particularly retail sales, had already bolstered the case for additional tightening.\par \par \uc2 That all helped push 10-year Treasury yields to the highest since October at 4.328% on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The market wants the Fed to go on hold, but the data just isn't supporting that," said Tony Sycamore, a markets analyst at IG.\par \par \uc2 "The risk aversion, the higher yields, the resilient economic data... all of those things have played out to perfection for the U.S. dollar."\par \par \uc2 Some selling to lock in profit from the dollar's rally makes sense going into the weekend, Sycamore added, but a break above 103.70 for the index next week looks likely, opening the way for tests of the May peak at 104.70, and then 105.88.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar eased 0.32% to 145.365 on Friday, after reaching a nine-month peak of 146.40 overnight.\par \par \uc2 In autumn of last year, the dollar's surge beyond 145 triggered the first yen buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities in a generation.\par \par \uc2 However, at that time a steep drop in Treasury yields had also helped make that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a success, which is not the case currently, \uc2 Ray Attrill\uc2 , head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 , wrote in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 "No such JPY tailwind this time around," he said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the euro edged up 0.06% to $1.0878, rebounding from Thursday's six-week low of $1.08565.\par \par \uc2 Against the yuan, the dollar was about flat at 7.3045 in offshore trading, recovering a 0.24% loss from earlier, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the official mid-point at 7.2006, more than 1,000 pips stronger than Reuters estimate.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plumbed a nine-month trough of 7.3490 on Thursday in offshore markets.\par \par \uc2 China's economic troubles have deepened, with property developer China \uc2 Evergrande\uc2  seeking Chapter 15 protection in a U.S. bankruptcy court, and concerns also growing over default risks in its shadow banking sector.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has so far disappointed with stimulus, even as each recent data release has painted an increasingly grim picture of the economic outlook, though the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  cut rates earlier this week in a surprise move that widened the yield gap against the U.S., rendering the yuan even more vulnerable to decline.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar, which often trades as a proxy for China and has tended to track the yuan in recent days, gave up early gains to slip 0.05% to $0.6399. It slumped to a nine-month low of $0.6365 on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the world's biggest cryptocurrency, bitcoin , dipped to a fresh two-month low at $26,172 on Friday, after plunging more than 7% in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 "There comes a point when it just couldn't ignore the rise in (\uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 ) yields any longer," said IG's Sycamore, who sees the potential for a decline to $24,500.\par \par \uc2 "The question becomes whether you want your assets in a speculative section of the market when you're in the middle of a bond market rout."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Buckland; Editing by Jacqueline Wong & Simon Cameron-Moore) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-18T07:18:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230818ej8i00j7p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
