\par\par \b \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  Nears End of Tightening With One More Hike Likely -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2023\par 10:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0841 GMT - \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  is very close to the end of its tightening cycle after Thursday's 25 basis-point hike took the key rate to 4%, with \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  deputy chief eurozone economist Jack Allen-Reynolds says in a note. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  expects one final 25 basis-point increase in September to 4.25%. This latest increase was in line with previous signalling and guidance is also broadly as expected, though the commitment to another increase in September seems slightly less firm than previously, he says. "Next year, we think that the krone will strengthen as global investor risk appetite improves. This will pull inflation down more quickly than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forecasting, allowing policymakers to cut interest rates more rapidly too." (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 GMT - The Norwegian krone rises after \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  raised its key rate by 25 basis points to 4.0%, as expected, and warned of further possible increases as inflation "remains high and markedly above the target." If the krone proves weaker than previously projected or if pressures in the economy persist, a higher policy rate than signaled in June may be needed to bring down inflation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its policy statement. "If the economy evolves as currently anticipated, the policy rate will be raised further in September," Governor \uc2 Ida Wolden Bache\uc2  is quoted as saying. EUR/NOK falls 0.3% to 11.4975, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 , from around 11.5285 just before the decision. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT - USD/JPY's bullish bias resumes, based on technical charts, says Kelvin Wong, senior market analyst at Oanda, in an email. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair has broken above the 145.50-146.10 resistance area, where \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place on Sept. 22, Wong says. The main driver of USD/JPY's rally has been a weak Chinese yuan and the rising yield premium of 10-year U.S. Treasuries over 10-year JGBs, Wong says. The next medium-term resistance to watch will be at the 148.20-148.85 area, Wong adds. USD/JPY is little changed at 146.33, after earlier touching 146.57, its highest intraday level since November, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT - \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  is widely expected to raise its key rate by 25 basis points to 4.0% in a decision due at 0800 GMT and ING expects the Norwegian krone to rise further as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could leave open the possibility of more increases. "Markets...are underestimating the chances of another hike in September," says ING \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Francesco Pesole\uc2 . "We cannot exclude \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  will hint at openness to more tightening, and we see upside risks for NOK anyway, given the rather flat market pricing," he says. EUR/NOK falls 0.1% to 11.5346. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0719 GMT - The dollar is likely to rise further after minutes of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's July monetary policy meeting showed policymakers continued to see significant upside risks to inflation, KBC Bank Economics write in a note. This could cause the euro to drop imminently below its July low against the dollar--at $1.0833, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 --, as the dollar holds its momentum, they say. "We remain flabbergasted by the fact that U.S. money markets only attach a 1/3 probability for a final Fed rate hike by November despite this clear Fed guidance." EUR/USD trades steady at 1.0879, having earlier hit a six-week low of 1.0862, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT - Japan is set to buck the regional trend and deliver faster growth in 2023 after the economy grew an annualized 6.0% in 2Q, DBS says in a note. Among the factors bolstering the impressive recovery is "the synergy of a weaker yen and Japan's enticing tourism resources," senior economist Ma Tieying says. Full-year growth could accelerate about 1 percentage point, with on-year growth likely to be stable at around 2% in 3Q-4Q, she says. DBS expects the Japanese economy to expand 2.0% this year, up from 1.2% growth forecast previously.(farah.elias@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0625 GMT - Eurozone government bond yields are trading higher, tracking \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, as investors bank on high-for-longer monetary policy rates in developed markets, analysts say. "Markets are taking the prospect of another hike from the Fed increasingly seriously, with futures now pricing in a 45% chance of a further hike by the November meeting," Deutsche Bank Research analysts write in a note. "But as well as the upcoming decisions, it's clear that investors are adjusting to the fact that rates could remain at a higher level for some time," they say. Eurozone 10-year government bond yields are up by 6-8 basis points across the board, with the 10-year German Bund yield up 6bps at 2.704%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2 . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT - Japanese stocks end lower, dragged by falls in retailers and trading houses, as concerns resurface about the Fed's further policy tightening. MatsukiyoCocokara drops 3.2% and \uc2 Mitsui & Co\uc2 . declines 2.3%. The Nikkei Stock Average falls 0.4% to 31626.00. The 10-year Japanese government bond yield rises 2.5 basis points to 0.650%. Investors are focusing on U.S. weekly jobless claims for any policy implications. USD/JPY is at 146.38, compared with 146.35 as of Wednesday 5 p.m. Eastern Time. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT - Yuan weakens against USD in onshore and offshore markets amid a dim economic outlook. After China's disappointing July economic data, market participants are tempted to lower their 2023 growth forecasts, flagging increasing risks of missing Beijing's annual GDP growth target of around 5%, says Ken Cheung, chief Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2 , in an email. The country's deepening property crisis and the spillover to wealth management products have added to concerns, Cheung says. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will probably keep more \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy flexibility and the Covid-19 low of 7.3274 is unlikely to offer much support, Cheung adds. USD/CNY is up 0.3% at 7.3169 after earlier touching 7.3174, highest intraday level since November, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . USD/CNH is up 0.1% at 7.3424. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT - USD/SGD rises in the afternoon Asian session on prospects for more Fed tightening. The FOMC meeting's minutes released overnight showed most Fed officials saw "significant" upside risks to inflation, which could require further tightening by the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Maybank analysts say in an \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research & Strategy note. Also, recent rises in Treasury yields have boosted the greenback against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the analysts add. USD/SGD is up 0.3% at 1.3620 after earlier touching 1.3625, the highest intraday level since December, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0321 GMT - Thailand's political outlook may become clearer, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analysts say in a research report. The House Assembly will probably convene on Aug. 22 to vote for a prime minister, after the Constitutional Court on Wednesday dismissed a petition to review a parliamentary decision that blocked the Move Forward Party's prime ministerial candidate from being renominated, the analysts note. If a PM is successfully selected, this is likely to improve clarity on the political outlook and should be positive for THB risk assets, the analysts add. With formation of a new government probably drawing closer, \uc2 GS\uc2  remains bullish THB against non-Japan Asian peers and maintains its 12-month USD/THB forecast of 32.00. USD/THB is up 0.2% at 35.58. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT - After two robust prints, Australian employment fell 14,600 in July, with the unemployment rate rising from 3.5% to 3.7%. While weaker than expected, the softness in the data fits neatly with the sentiment expressed in the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's most recent set of minutes, which noted twice that the labor market might be at a turning point, says Adam Boyton, head of Australian economics at ANZ. That said, given the volatility in the monthly labor force survey, it will take more than a month's data to prove that point, he adds. Unemployment rate printed at 3.7% in January and April, only to fall back again in subsequent months, Boyton noted. (james.glynn@wsj.com;@JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 17, 2023 04:41 ET (08:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230817ej8h000sw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
