\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar edges higher vs  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 -zone yen, China fears weigh on yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 897 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2023\par 16:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Recasts to lead with Japanese yen, updates prices, adds \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  table, adds WASHINGTON dateline)\par     By Hannah Lang and Samuel Indyk\par        WASHINGTON/LONDON, Aug 16 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen further weakened against the dollar on\par Wednesday, hovering in a zone that last year triggered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while the yuan slipped to a nine-month\par trough as concerns mounted about the extent of China's slowdown. \par     The yen has hit the key 145 per dollar level for four sessions now, a zone that triggered heavy dollar\par selling by Japanese authorities in September and October last year. It last weakened 0.13% versus the\par greenback to 145.75 per dollar, after hitting 145.940 earlier in the session, a level not seen since\par November. \par     Finance Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shunichi Suzuki\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on Tuesday authorities were not targeting absolute \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels\par for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "Markets are concerned whether the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or whether dollar-yen needs to go all\par the way up to 150," said Niels Christensen, chief analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par     "They have not been so loud in the last week, but the threat of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is why the market has been\par a little bit hesitant to push dollar-yen up."\par     Elsewhere in Asia, the yuan touched its lowest level since November in both the onshore and offshore\par markets, falling as low as 7.3379.\par     That extended Tuesday's decline following Chinese data that missed forecasts and prompted Beijing to\par deliver unexpected cuts to its key policy rates as authorities there sought to shore up an economy that has\par rapidly lost steam in recent months.\par     The Australian dollar, often used as a liquid proxy for the yuan, plumbed nine-month lows in\par response to the Chinese data.\par     "I really don't think that the backdrop is conducive for a sustained risk rally at this point," said\par Bipan Rai, global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at CIBC Capital Markets. "Not only the China data, it's also the fact\par that we're dealing with tighter monetary conditions globally, and that's going to crimp demand at the\par margin."\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WATCH\par     The pound was up after data showed British core inflation stayed strong in July, hitting a high\par of $1.2768 and was last up around 0.45% at $1.2759.\par     Core inflation in Britain, which strips out volatile energy and food prices, remained at 6.9% in July,\par flat versus the June reading, and higher than expectations in a Reuters poll for a reading of 6.8%.\par     The dollar index was off 0.087% at 103.100, though it was not far from an over one-month peak hit\par on Monday, driven by higher bond yields following upbeat U.S. data. The euro gained 0.12% to\par $1.0917.\par     The New Zealand dollar, which had fallen to a nine-month low of $0.5932 in early Asian trade,\par rebounded after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  policy meeting, to trade higher by 0.11% versus the\par greenback at $0.596.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held its cash rate steady as expected on Wednesday, but slightly pushed out when it\par expects to start cutting borrowing costs to 2025.\par     "The statement gave a clearly more hawkish tone, likely defying any dovish expectations," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par research analyst Shreya Sodhani. \par     "We think today's more hawkish statement and the governor's press conference suggest that the timing of\par rate cuts will likely be pushed back a bit."\par     ========================================================\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 10:30AM (1430 GMT)\par  Description      RIC         Last           U.S. Close  Pct Change     YTD Pct       High Bid    Low Bid\par                                               Previous                   Change                   \par                                               Session                                             \par  Dollar index                 103.1000       103.2100    -0.09%         -0.377%       +103.2800   +102.9400\par  Euro/Dollar                  $1.0915        $1.0904     +0.12%         +1.89%        +$1.0934    +$1.0896\par  Dollar/Yen                   145.7600       145.5700    +0.12%         +11.16%       +145.9100   +145.3100\par  Euro/Yen                     159.11         158.74      +0.23%         +13.41%       +159.2000   +158.6400\par  Dollar/Swiss                 0.8791         0.8786      +0.06%         -4.92%        +0.8814     +0.8775\par  Sterling/Dollar              $1.2759        $1.2702     +0.45%         +5.50%        +$1.2766    +$1.2689\par  Dollar/Canadian              1.3515         1.3497      +0.16%         -0.23%        +1.3518     +1.3476\par  Aussie/Dollar                $0.6452        $0.6456     -0.04%         -5.33%        +$0.6481    +$0.6429\par  Euro/Swiss                   0.9596         0.9579      +0.18%         -3.02%        +0.9611