\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits highest in more than a month on China economy concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 912 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2023\par 21:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Updates prices, adds analyst comment in paragraph 10, adds\par paragraph on Argentine peso in paragraph 15)\par     By Hannah Lang\par        WASHINGTON, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar hit more\par than a one-month high on Monday as investors sought a safe haven\par due to concerns about China's economy, and traders braced for\par possible Japanese government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the yen hit its\par lowest level since November.\par     The dollar index, which tracks the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against\par its major peers, was up 0.301% at 103.170, hitting its highest\par level in more than a month. \par     Analysts said investors bought the dollar as shelter from\par concerns about the health of the global economy, particularly\par China. \par     A source told Reuters that Country Garden, China's largest\par private developer, is seeking to delay payment on a private\par onshore bond for the first time, in a new sign of stress in the\par sector.\par     Meanwhile, two Chinese listed companies said at the weekend\par they had not received payment on maturing investment products\par from asset manager Zhongrong International Trust Co.\par     "A lot of traders are focusing again on China," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Edward\par Moya\f28 \uc2 \f2 , senior market analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OANDA\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "I think there's so much\par concern with just their growth outlook, with their current\par property crisis, and I think one of the biggest wealth managers\par not being able to make debt obligations is a big red\par flag."\par         Japan's yen was trading at its lowest level\par since November at 145.50 per dollar, with the dollar up 0.36%\par against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     The Bank of Japan has stuck to its ultra-loose monetary\par policy as other global central banks hiked interest rates,\par making returns in other countries look more attractive and\par weighing heavily on the yen.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in September when the\par dollar rose past 145 yen, prompting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par (MOF) to buy the yen and push the pair back to around 140 yen.\par The yen is down nearly 10% against the dollar for the year.\par     "I wouldn't be too surprised if we see some news out of the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in the next week or two especially if yen starts\par to move towards, say, 147, that they're going to start to make\par some moves to strengthen the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Helen Given, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par trader, at Monex USA in Washington.\par     The Australian dollar slid to its lowest level\par since May and was last down 0.28% versus the U.S. dollar at\par $0.648. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is often seen as a proxy for investor\par sentiment on China.\par     Sterling was last down 0.16% to $1.2676, while the\par euro was 0.38% lower at $1.09045.\par     Russia's rouble reversed course after falling\par past 100 per U.S. dollar on Monday, driven in large part by the\par Russian current account surplus shrinking as energy export\par revenue dropped and government spending on the Ukraine war\par remained high.\par     That reversal came after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sparked\par expectations of another hefty rate hike in announcing an\par extraordinary policy meeting for Tuesday.\par     Argentina's peso fell on Monday after a far-right\par libertarian who wants to axe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and dollarize the\par economy unexpectedly won a primary election. That prompted the\par Argentine government to devalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by nearly 18%. \par     Economic data could move \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  later in the week, as\par investors will scrutinize Chinese industrial output and consumer\par spending data on Tuesday before minutes from the latest \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S.\par Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  meeting on Wednesday. British inflation figures\par are also due on Wednesday.\par     Japanese GDP data is due on Tuesday, ahead of inflation\par figures on Friday.\par     ========================================================\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 3:00PM (1900 GMT)\par  Description      RIC         Last           U.S. Close  Pct Change     YTD Pct       High Bid    Low Bid\par                                               Previous                   Change                   \par                                               Session                                             \par  Dollar index                 103.1700       102.8700    +0.30%         -0.309%       +103.4600   +102.7600\par  Euro/Dollar                  $1.0904        $1.0947     -0.38%         +1.77%        +$1.0965    +$1.0874\par  Dollar/Yen                   145.5050       144.9550    +0.38%         +10.98%       +145.5700   +144.6500\par  Euro/Yen                     158.66         158.65      +0.01%         +13.09%       +158.8200   +158.2000\par  Dollar/Swiss                 0.8790         0.8764      +0