\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar hits highest in more than a month on China economy concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 891 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2023\par 21:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar hit more than a one-month high on Monday as investors sought a safe haven due to concerns about China's economy, and traders braced for possible Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the yen hit its lowest level since November.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which tracks the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against its major peers, was up 0.301% at 103.170, hitting its highest level in more than a month.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said investors bought the dollar as shelter from concerns about the health of the global economy, particularly China.\par \par \uc2 A source told Reuters that Country Garden, China's largest private developer, is seeking to delay payment on a private onshore bond for the first time, in a new sign of stress in the sector.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, two Chinese listed companies said at the weekend they had not received payment on maturing investment products from asset manager Zhongrong International Trust Co.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of traders are focusing again on China," said \uc2 Edward Moya\uc2 , senior market analyst at \uc2 OANDA\uc2 . "I think there's so much concern with just their growth outlook, with their current property crisis, and I think one of the biggest wealth managers not being able to make [their] debt obligations is a big red flag."\par \par \uc2 Japan's yen was trading at its lowest level since November at 145.50 per dollar, with the dollar up 0.36% against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has stuck to its ultra-loose monetary policy as other global central banks hiked interest rates, making returns in other countries look more attractive and weighing heavily on the yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in September when the dollar rose past 145 yen, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) to buy the yen and push the pair back to around 140 yen. The yen is down nearly 10% against the dollar for the year.\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't be too surprised if we see some news out of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  in the next week or two especially if yen starts to move towards, say, 147, that they're going to start to make some moves to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Helen Given, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader, at Monex USA in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar slid to its lowest level since May and was last down 0.28% versus the U.S. dollar at $0.648. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is often seen as a proxy for investor sentiment on China.\par \par \uc2 Sterling was last down 0.16% to $1.2676, while the euro was 0.38% lower at $1.09045.\par \par \uc2 Russia's rouble reversed course after falling past 100 per U.S. dollar on Monday, driven in large part by the Russian current account surplus shrinking as energy export revenue dropped and government spending on the Ukraine war remained high.\par \par \uc2 That reversal came after \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked expectations of another hefty rate hike in announcing an extraordinary policy meeting for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's peso fell on Monday after a far-right libertarian who wants to axe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and dollarize the economy unexpectedly won a primary election. That prompted the Argentine government to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by nearly 18%.\par \par \uc2 Economic data could move \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the week, as investors will scrutinize Chinese industrial output and consumer spending data on Tuesday before minutes from the latest \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting on Wednesday. British inflation figures are also due on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Japanese GDP data is due on Tuesday, ahead of inflation figures on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ========================================================\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 3:00PM (1900 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Description RIC Last U.S. Close Pct Change YTD Pct High Bid Low Bid\par \par \uc2 Previous Change\par \par \uc2 Session\par \par \uc2 Dollar index 103.1700 102.8700 +0.30% -0.309% +103.4600 +102.7600\par \par \uc2 Euro/Dollar $1.0904 $1.0947 -0.38% +1.77% +$1.0965 +$1.0874\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Yen 145.5050 144.9550 +0.38% +10.98% +145.5700 +144.6500\par \par \uc2 Euro/Yen 158.66 158.65 +0.01% +13.09% +158.8200 +158.2000\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Swiss 0.8790 0.8764 +0.30% -4.94% +0.8828 +0.8756\par \par \uc2 Sterling/Dollar $1.2674 $1.2697 -0.16% +4.82% +$1.2713 +$1.2617\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Canadian 1.3468 1.3446 +0.19% -0.57% +1.3478 +1.3437\par \par \uc2 Aussie/Dollar $0.6479 $0.6498 -0.28% -4.94% +$0.6503 +$0.6454\par \par \uc2 Euro/Swiss 0.9585 0.9595 -0.10% -3.13% +0.9613 +0.9576\par \par \uc2 Euro/Sterling 0.8601 0.8620 -0.22% -2.75% +0.8632 +0.8601\par \par \uc2 NZ $0.5966 $0.5985 -0.29% -6.02% +$0.5991 +$0.5944\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Dollar\par \par \uc2 Euro/Norway 11.4239 11.4084 +0.14% +8.86% +11.4769 +11.4069\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Sweden 10.8100 10.8246 -0.52% +3.86% +10.8866 +10.7707\par \par \uc2 Euro/Sweden 11.7893 11.8505 -0.52% +5.73% +11.8886 +11.7734\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Hannah Lang in Washington; Additional reporting by Harry Robertson in London and Ankur Banerjee in Singapore; Editing by Susan Fenton, Kirsten Donovan, Paul Simao and Cynthia Osterman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-14T19:15: