\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits more than one-month high on China fears, rising yields\b0\par\par\uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2023\par 15:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, updates prices at 1245 GMT)\par \par \uc2 By Harry Robertson and Ankur Banerjee\par \par \uc2 LONDON/SINGAPORE, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar hit more than a one-month high on Monday as investors sought a safe haven due to concerns about China's economy, while traders were on the lookout for Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the yen fell to its lowest level since November.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which tracks the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against its major peers, was up 0.26% to 103.15 on Monday, the highest level since July 7.\par \par \uc2 Sterling was last down 0.26% to $1.266, while the euro was 0.28% lower at $1.092.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said investors were buying the safe-haven dollar over concerns about the health of the global economy, particularly China.\par \par \uc2 They also pointed to a recent rise in U.S. bond yields on the back of persistent strength in the country's economy, which has supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. 10-year Treasury yield was trading at around 4.18% on Monday, just shy of the nine-month high touched last week.\par \par \uc2 A source told Reuters that Country Garden, China's largest private developer, is seeking to delay payment on a private onshore bond for the first time, in a fresh sign of stress in the sector.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, two Chinese listed companies said over the weekend that they had not received payment on maturing investment products from asset manager Zhongrong International Trust Co.\par \par \uc2 "High U.S. bond yields and what seems to be a deteriorating environment in the Chinese financial sector are weighing on risk assets," \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2 , head of markets at ING, said in emailed comments.\par \par \uc2 He said the dollar is likely to stay strong "unless Chinese authorities can surprise with some powerful stimulus measures".\par \par \uc2 Japan's yen was trading at its lowest level since Nov. 10 at 145.36 per dollar, with the dollar up 0.29% against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A move to around 145.2 in the Asian session was quickly reversed.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has stuck to its ultra-loose monetary policy as other global central banks hiked interest rates, making returns in other countries look more attractive and weighing heavily on the yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last September when the dollar rose past 145 yen, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) to buy the yen and push the pair back to around 140 yen. The yen is down nearly 10% against the dollar for the year.\par \par \uc2 "Lack of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far suggests that the patience level of Japanese authorities may have gone up after the latest tweak to monetary policy and the disinflation trends in the United States," said Charu Chanana, a market strategist at Saxo Markets.\par \par \uc2 "Still, traders are potentially cautious of that 145 handle."\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar slid to its lowest level since May at $0.6456 and was last down 0.33% at $0.648. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is often seen as a proxy for investor sentiment about China.\par \par \uc2 Russia's rouble fell past 100 per U.S. dollar on Monday, driven in large part by the Russian current account surplus shrinking sharply as energy export revenue dropped and government spending on the Ukraine war remained high.\par \par \uc2 Economic data could move \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the week.\par \par \uc2 Investors will scrutinise Chinese industrial output and consumer spending data on Tuesday, before minutes from the latest \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting on Wednesday. British inflation figures are also due on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Japanese GDP data is due on Tuesday, ahead of inflation figures on Friday.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Harry Robertson in London and Ankur Banerjee in Singapore Editing by Susan Fenton, Kirsten Donovan and Paul Simao) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-14T14:07:13.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230814ej8e01xln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
