\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 - \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  markets steady after yen breaches 2023 low\b0\par\par\uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2023\par 12:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices at 1020 GMT)\par \par \uc2 By Harry Robertson and Ankur Banerjee\par \par \uc2 LONDON/SINGAPORE, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets steadied on Monday after the yen hit its lowest level since November, leaving traders on the look out for Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a one-month high against a basket of other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors sought a safe haven on concerns about China's economy, but then eased.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened to as low as 145.22 per dollar in early Asian hours, its lowest since Nov. 10, before quickly reversing course in a volatile start to the week. The dollar last fetched 144.96 yen, flat on the day.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has stuck to its ultra-loose monetary policy as other global central banks hiked interest rates, making returns in other countries look more attractive and weighing heavily on the yen.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last September when the dollar rose past 145 yen, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) to buy the yen and push the pair back to around 140 yen. The yen is down nearly 10% against the dollar for the year.\par \par \uc2 "Lack of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far suggests that the patience level of Japanese authorities may have gone up after the latest tweak to monetary policy and the disinflation trends in the United States," said Charu Chanana, a market strategist at Saxo Markets.\par \par \uc2 "Still, traders are potentially cautious of that 145 handle."\par \par \uc2 Much of the movement in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  came late in the Asian session, before the market fell into a summer lull.\par \par \uc2 The euro slipped before reversing losses to stand flat against the dollar at $1.095.\par \par \uc2 The early move helped the dollar index rise to 103.02, its highest since early July. It was last very slightly lower at 102.82.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said investors have been buying the safe-haven dollar over concerns about the health of the global economy, particularly China. They also pointed to a recent rise in U.S. bond yields on the back of persistent strength in the country's economy.\par \par \uc2 Chinese property giant Country Garden's onshore bonds were suspended, and two Chinese listed companies said over the weekend that they had not received payment on maturing investment products from asset manager Zhongrong International Trust Co.\par \par \uc2 "High U.S. bond yields and what seems to be a deteriorating environment in the Chinese financial sector are weighing on risk assets," said \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2 , head of markets at ING, in emailed comments.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar slid to its lowest since May at $0.6456 but was last little changed at $0.649. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is often seen as a proxy for investor sentiment about China.\par \par \uc2 Russia's rouble fell past 100 per U.S. dollar on Monday, driven in large part by the Russian current account surplus shrinking sharply as energy export revenue dropped and government spending on the Ukraine war remained high.\par \par \uc2 Sterling edged up to $1.271.\par \par \uc2 Economic data could move \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the week.\par \par \uc2 Investors will scrutinise Chinese industrial output and consumer spending data on Tuesday, before minutes from the latest \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting on Wednesday. British inflation figures are also due on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Japanese GDP data is due on Tuesday, ahead of inflation figures on Friday.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Harry Robertson in London and Ankur Banerjee in Singapore Editing by Susan Fenton, Kirsten Donovan) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-14T11:41:43.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230814ej8e01fmd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
