\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen breaks above 145/dlr level in choppy trading, dollar firm\b0\par\par\uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2023\par 04:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The yen was volatile on Monday after slipping to its lowest for the year against the dollar, breaching the key 145 level as traders warily looked for clues on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the dollar rose to touch a more than one month high.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen weakened to as low as 145.22 per dollar in early Asian hours, its lowest since Nov. 10 2022 before quickly reversing course. It was last at 144.96 per dollar, flat on the day.\par \par \uc2 Japan's low yields have made the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  an easy target for short-sellers and funding trades, with the widening gap in the interest rates between Japan and the United States leading to persistent weakness in the yen.\par \par \uc2 The yen has fallen around 20% since the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  began rapidly raising rates to combat soaring inflation in March 2022, while the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains stuck in an extremely accommodating stance.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last September when the dollar rose past 145 yen, which prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the yen and push the pair back to around 140 yen. The yen is down over 9% against the dollar for the year.\par \par \uc2 With the yen loitering around that level again, traders expect Japanese officials to start warning of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon.\par \par \uc2 Saxo Markets strategists said this week's GDP and CPI data in Japan could be key, as well as U.S. data which could continue to push Treasury yields higher.\par \par \uc2 "Traders are also continuing to watch whether Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but lack of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far suggests potential patience from them."\par \par \uc2 Treasury yields have been elevated and got another boost on Friday after data showed U.S. producer prices increased slightly in July, more than expected, as the cost of services rebounded at the fastest pace in nearly a year.\par \par \uc2 That comes after news on Thursday that consumer prices rose moderately in July. The PPI data cast some doubt on whether the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is done with its rate hike cycle.\par \par \uc2 Markets anticipate nearly 89% chance of the Federal Reserve standing pat on interest rates at its meeting next month, CME FedWatch tool showed, with traders anticipating no more hikes for the rest of the year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, though, have maintained that it is too soon to make that call.\par \par \uc2 The inflation data along with recent labour market data point to the Fed leaving rates unchanged at its September meeting, ANZ analysts said, adding that there will be one more set of nonfarm payroll and CPI data for the Fed to consider before the meeting.\par \par \uc2 They said the resilience of the U.S. consumer will be in the spotlight with the release of July retail sales data, with rising fuel prices and tighter credit conditions expected to bite.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which measures the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against six peers, tacked on 0.078% at 102.94, its highest since July 7. The index is up 1% for the month.\par \par \uc2 The euro was down 0.09% to $1.0934, while sterling was last at $1.2676, down 0.14% on the day\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar fell 0.48% to $0.647, while the kiwi slipped 0.38% to $0.596. Both Antipodean \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been undermined by disappointing trade and inflation data from China, the biggest buyer of their resource exports.\par \par \uc2 While sentiment towards China is down, this week's high-frequency China data may only need a small beat to cause a strong upside reaction in China proxies, said Pepperstone's Head of Research Chris Weston.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ankur Banerjee in Singapore Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-14T03:02:33.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230814ej8e006bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
