\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar set for fourth week of gains, climbs back towards 145 yen\b0\par\par\uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2023\par 13:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Refreshes prices adds quote)\par \par \uc2 By Amanda Cooper\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 11 (Reuters) - The dollar headed for a fourth weekly gain on Friday even after data showed U.S. inflation did not pick up as strongly as expected in July, reinforcing the existing view among investors that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is unlikely to raise rates much more.\par \par \uc2 The stronger dollar put the Japanese yen on course to test a key support level, though liquidity was thin with Japan on holiday on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was last at 144.58 yen having earlier climbed as high as 144.89 yen, its highest since June 30 when it briefly breached 145, a level at which investors think the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might start warning of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "You should expect the rhetoric once yen gets to 145," said \uc2 Bank of Singapore\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Moh Siong Sim. "I think the market will get a lot more careful as we get to that level."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last September when the dollar rose past 145 yen, which prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the yen and push the pair back to around 140 yen. The yen is down over 9% against the dollar for the year.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, sterling rose for the first time in four days after data showed the British economy grew more than expected in June, allaying some concern about the impact of high inflation and high rates on activity.\par \par \uc2 The pound was last up 0.34% at $1.2719, but was still heading for a fourth weekly drop.\par \par \uc2 Friday's moves were happening in the shadow of the previous day's U.S. data which showed consumer inflation rose 0.2% last month, matching the gain in June, and by 3.2% in the 12 months through July.\par \par \uc2 Futures traders now place a near-90% chance of the Fed leaving its benchmark interest rate in its current range of 5.25-5.5% when it meets in September. Prior to the inflation data, that chance was already above 85%.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, which measures the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against six others, eased 0.1% to 102.50 on Friday, but was still set for a fourth weekly gain.\par \par \uc2 "From a dollar point of view, we think the recent price action denotes a reluctance to rotate away from the greenback given the emergence of concerning stories in other parts of the world," said \uc2 Francesco Pesole\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING in a note.\par \par \uc2 "If economic slowdown alarms are flashing yellow in Washington, they are flashing amber in Frankfurt and Beijing."\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere on Friday the euro was up 0.1% at $1.0992, and the Swiss franc was a touch firmer at 0.8757 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar rose 0.12% to $0.652.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Ankur Banerjee in Singapore and Alun John in London Editing by David Evans, Kirsten Donovan) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-11T12:18:33.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230811ej8b01e2t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
