\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -US dollar recovers from inflation-related losses; yen slumps\b0\par\par\uc2 963 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2023\par 22:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Recasts; adds analyst comment, Fed's Daly's remarks; updates prices)\par \par         * \par       US CPI rises, but largely in line with expectations\par     \par \par         * \par       US jobless claims increase in latest week\par     \par \par         * \par       Fed funds futures price in no hike, then cut in May 2024\par     \par \par         * \par       Yen falls, hits five-week low vs dollar, 15-year trough vs euro\par     \par \par   \par     By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss\par        NEW YORK, Aug 10 (Reuters) - \par     The dollar reversed losses on Thursday, led by gains against the yen, as investors digested U.S.\par inflation numbers that showed a moderate increase last month, but are still way above the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par 2% inflation target.\par   \par         The greenback climbed to five-week peaks against the yen of 144.735, and last traded up\par 0.7% at 144.71 yen. So far this year, the dollar has gained 10.4% versus the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par   \par         The U.S. unit also pared gains against the euro, which last changed hands at $1.0985, up just 0.1%\par . The recovery of the dollar against both the euro and yen pushed the dollar index up 0.1% to\par 102.56.\par   \par     Earlier in the session, the dollar dropped after data showed the consumer price index (CPI) rose 0.2%\par last month, matching the gain in June. The CPI climbed 3.2% in the 12 months through July, up from a 3.0%\par rise in June, which was the smallest year-on-year gain since March 2021.\par     Excluding the volatile food and energy categories, the CPI gained 0.2% in July, the same as the June\par increase. In the 12 months through July, core CPI grew 4.7% after rising 4.8% in June.\par         "The story from this morning's CPI release took a little while to settle into markets. While yes,\par year-over-year CPI did come in slightly below expectation, that 3.2% figure is still higher than it was last\par month," said Helen Given, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at Monex USA in Washington.\par   \par         "It's also still a good bit higher than the Fed's 2% inflation target, so it's still very much in\par the realm of possibility that there will be a further 25 basis point hike this year. Even if the Fed chooses\par not to hike interest rates again, a cut is not coming any time soon as inflation above target remains\par entrenched in the U.S. economy."\par   \par         \f28 \uc2 \f2 San Francisco Federal Reserve Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 Mary Daly\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Thursday also said \par     more progress is needed\par      to tame inflation, even though it is moving in the right direction. She is a voter on the Federal Open\par Market Committee in 2024.\par   \par         She said the July CPI numbers do not mean that the Fed can declare victory over inflation, adding\par that the labor market is not yet balanced.\par   \par     Futures on the benchmark fed funds rate have priced in a pause in rate hikes at the next meeting and for\par the rest of the year. The next possible move by the Fed is a rate cut in May 2024, rate futures\par showed. \par     A separate report from the Labor Department on Thursday showed initial claims for state unemployment\par benefits increased 21,000 to a seasonally adjusted 248,000 for the week ended Aug. 5. Economists had\par forecast 230,000 claims for the latest week.\par     In other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar slipped 0.1% to 0.8765 francs.\par     The euro gained against the yen, soaring to a 15-year high of 159.20. It was last up 0.8% at 158.96 yen.\par     Analysts had partly attributed the yen's weak trend to higher oil prices, given that Japan is a major\par oil importer.\par         Investors are also on the lookout for possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lift the yen. In\par September, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when the dollar rose above 145 yen, pushing the pair to around 140 yen as the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought the yen to weaken the dollar.\par   \par         \par   \par         ========================================================\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 3:53PM (1953 GMT)\par  Description      RIC         Last           U.S. Close  Pct Change     YTD Pct       High Bid    Low Bid\par                                               Previous                   Change                   \par                                               Session                                             \par  Dollar index                 102.6000       102.4800    +0.14%         -0.860%       +102.6400   +101.7600\par  Euro/Dollar                  $1.0980        $1.0975     +0.06%         +2.49%        +$1.1065    +$1.0968\par  Dollar/Yen                   144.7600       143.7500    +0.70%         +10.41%       +144.7850   +143.2550\par  Euro/Yen                     158.96         157.73      +0.78%         +13.30%       +159.2000   +157.6700\par  Dollar/Swiss                 0.8767         0.8773      -0.08%         -5.20%        +0.8776     +0.8690\par  Sterling/Dollar              $1.2676        $1.2721     -0.35%         +4.82%        +$1.2818    +$1.2670\par  Dollar/Canadian              1.3439         1.3420      +0.14%         -0.81%        +1.3441     +1.3373\par  Aussie/Dollar    