\par\par \b \uc2 US dollar recovers from inflation-related losses; yen slumps\b0\par\par\uc2 904 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2023\par 22:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Reuters) - The dollar reversed losses on Thursday, led by gains against the yen, as investors digested U.S. inflation numbers that showed a moderate increase last month, but are still way above the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's 2% inflation target.\par \par \uc2 The greenback climbed to five-week peaks against the yen of 144.735, and last traded up 0.7% at 144.71 yen. So far this year, the dollar has gained 10.4% versus the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit also pared gains against the euro, which last changed hands at $1.0985, up just 0.1%. The recovery of the dollar against both the euro and yen pushed the dollar index up 0.1% to 102.56.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session, the dollar dropped after data showed the consumer price index (CPI) rose 0.2% last month, matching the gain in June. The CPI climbed 3.2% in the 12 months through July, up from a 3.0% rise in June, which was the smallest year-on-year gain since March 2021.\par \par \uc2 Excluding the volatile food and energy categories, the CPI gained 0.2% in July, the same as the June increase. In the 12 months through July, core CPI grew 4.7% after rising 4.8% in June.\par \par \uc2 "The story from this morning's CPI release took a little while to settle into markets. While yes, year-over-year CPI did come in slightly below expectation, that 3.2% figure is still higher than it was last month," said Helen Given, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Monex USA in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "It's also still a good bit higher than the Fed's 2% inflation target, so it's still very much in the realm of possibility that there will be a further 25 basis point hike this year. Even if the Fed chooses not to hike interest rates again, a cut is not coming any time soon as inflation above target remains entrenched in the U.S. economy."\par \par \uc2 San Francisco Federal Reserve Bank\uc2 Mary Daly\uc2  on Thursday also said more progress is needed to tame inflation, even though it is moving in the right direction. She is a voter on the Federal Open Market Committee in 2024.\par \par \uc2 She said the July CPI numbers do not mean that the Fed can declare victory over inflation, adding that the labor market is not yet balanced.\par \par \uc2 Futures on the benchmark fed funds rate have priced in a pause in rate hikes at the next meeting and for the rest of the year. The next possible move by the Fed is a rate cut in May 2024, rate futures showed.\par \par \uc2 A separate report from the Labor Department on Thursday showed initial claims for state unemployment benefits increased 21,000 to a seasonally adjusted 248,000 for the week ended Aug. 5. Economists had forecast 230,000 claims for the latest week.\par \par \uc2 In other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar slipped 0.1% to 0.8765 francs.\par \par \uc2 The euro gained against the yen, soaring to a 15-year high of 159.20. It was last up 0.8% at 158.96 yen.\par \par \uc2 Analysts had partly attributed the yen's weak trend to higher oil prices, given that Japan is a major oil importer.\par \par \uc2 Investors are also on the lookout for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the yen. In September, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar rose above 145 yen, pushing the pair to around 140 yen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought the yen to weaken the dollar.\par \par \uc2 ========================================================\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 3:53PM (1953 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Description RIC Last U.S. Close Pct Change YTD Pct High Bid Low Bid\par \par \uc2 Previous Change\par \par \uc2 Session\par \par \uc2 Dollar index 102.6000 102.4800 +0.14% -0.860% +102.6400 +101.7600\par \par \uc2 Euro/Dollar $1.0980 $1.0975 +0.06% +2.49% +$1.1065 +$1.0968\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Yen 144.7600 143.7500 +0.70% +10.41% +144.7850 +143.2550\par \par \uc2 Euro/Yen 158.96 157.73 +0.78% +13.30% +159.2000 +157.6700\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Swiss 0.8767 0.8773 -0.08% -5.20% +0.8776 +0.8690\par \par \uc2 Sterling/Dollar $1.2676 $1.2721 -0.35% +4.82% +$1.2818 +$1.2670\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Canadian 1.3439 1.3420 +0.14% -0.81% +1.3441 +1.3373\par \par \uc2 Aussie/Dollar $0.6524 $0.6529 -0.08% -4.30% +$0.6617 +$0.6521\par \par \uc2 Euro/Swiss 0.9626 0.9625 +0.01% -2.72% +0.9637 +0.9615\par \par \uc2 Euro/Sterling 0.8661 0.8625 +0.42% -2.07% +0.8665 +0.8626\par \par \uc2 NZ $0.6033 $0.6052 -0.31% -4.98% +$0.6117 +$0.6028\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Dollar\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Norway 10.2960 10.2080 +0.84% +4.89% +10.3040 +10.0900\par \par \uc2 Euro/Norway 11.3087 11.1970 +1.00% +7.77% +11.3210 +11.1587\par \par \uc2 Dollar/Sweden 10.6939 10.6654 +0.30% +2.75% +10.7006 +10.5459\par \par \uc2 Euro/Sweden 11.7419 11.7063 +0.30% +5.31% +11.7505 +11.6679\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss; Additional reporting Alun John in London; Editing by Mark Potter, Andrea Ricci and Richard Chang) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-10T20:10:44.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230810ej8a006bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
