\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's 'Mr.Yen' Sakakibara expects no yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2023\par 07:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Reuters) - Japanese authorities are unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the yen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already found some support and will head much higher as U.S. interest rates peak, former finance official Eisuke Sakakibara said.\par \par \uc2 Sakakibara gained a reputation as a market mover in the 1990s after devising several \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  during his time as vice finance minister, earning him the nickname "Mr Yen". He remains a closely watched figure by markets for his insight on the yen.\par \par \uc2 The yen is poised to reach 130 against the U.S. dollar by the year end as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  ends its aggressive monetary tightening and as Japan's economic outlook brightens, Sakakibara told Reuters on Friday.\par \par \uc2 \u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0 "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  are reasonably satisfied with what has been going on, so I don't foresee any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to warn or to change the course of the exchange rate," he said.\par \par \uc2 \u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0 Sakakibara's comments come as the yen hovers around 142 to a dollar, having steadied in the past week after a decline of 7.5% this year as Japan's low yields made the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  an easy target for short-sellers and funding trades.\par \par \uc2 The yen has fallen around 14% since the Fed began rapidly raising rates to combat soaring inflation in March 2022, while the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  remains stuck in an extremely accommodating stance.\par \par \uc2 \u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0 What the data-dependent Fed ultimately decides at upcoming meetings will be key to the yen's position, but if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows through on market expectations, the yen will edge up to 130 by the end of 2023 and gradually to 120 "within the next few years", Sakakibara said.\par \par \uc2 \u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0 After the last FOMC meeting in July, a majority of market participants expect the Fed will hold rates at 5.25-5.50% as inflation eases and the economy loses momentum.\par \par \uc2 Sakakibara takes \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  head \uc2 Kazuo Ueda\uc2  at his word that easy policy will be retained for the time being. He reckons Japan has at long last escaped the deflationary trends that have haunted the economy since his time at the MOF.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October when the yen slipped to 149.70 against the dollar, and speculation grew that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could tumble further.\par \par \uc2 \u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0 While the chance that the yen weakens further against the dollar can't be completely dismissed, Sakakibara believes the "tide has changed" for dollar-yen.\par \par \uc2 \u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0 If the yen can reach that 120-130 range, the Japanese government should be feeling pretty comfortable, he said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Brigid Riley and Hiroko Hamada; Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-7T05:38:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230807ej87008c1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
