------------------------------\par --  \par     ** indicates closing price \par     All prices as of 20:15 GMT\par     \par     EQUITIES\par \par     GLOBAL - U.S. shares were flat after a day of choppy trading as U.S. bond yields\par hit nine-month peaks and the dollar dipped following a U.S. credit downgrade.\par     In Asia, MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan\par fell 0.2%, extending losses after a drop of 2.3% a day earlier.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     NEW YORK - U.S. stocks closed nearly flat on Thursday after a choppy trading\par session, as investors weighed another rise in Treasury yields with the latest batch of\par economic data and earnings.\par     According to preliminary data, the S&P 500 lost 12.37 points, or 0.27%, to\par end at 4,501.02 points, while the Nasdaq Composite lost 13.73 points, or 0.11%,\par to 13,957.99. The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 75.07 points, or 0.21%, to\par 35,207.45.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - European stocks hit a three-week low on Thursday, hurt by disappointing\par earnings reports and elevated U.S. bond yields, though British stocks regained some\par ground after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised rates in line with expectations.\par     The pan-European STOXX 600 index fell 0.6%, ending its third straight\par session in the red.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japanese shares fell for a second straight day on Thursday, tracking the\par overnight drop on Wall Street, as investor sentiment was hurt by rising yields in the\par United States and Japan.\par     The Nikkei index lost 1.68% to close at 32,159.28, after losing 2.3% in its\par biggest one-day drop in the previous session.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - China stocks rose on Thursday, led by financial shares, after state\par media said wealth effect via stock investing gains would help boost household\par consumption, while China's services activity expanded at a faster pace in July.\par     China's blue-chip CSI300 Index closed up 0.9%, while the Shanghai\par Composite Index was up 0.6%. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - Australian shares are set to open marginally higher on Friday, likely\par boosted by energy stocks on strong oil prices, while investors still remain on edge\par after rating agency \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2 's credit rating. \par     The local share price index futures rose 0.1%, a 60.7-point discount to\par the underlying S&P/ASX 200 index close. The benchmark fell 0.05% on Thursday.  \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - South Korean shares ended lower on Thursday, weighed by caution ahead of\par U.S. employment data due this week and lingering anxiety over a U.S. credit rating\par downgrade.\par     The benchmark KOSPI closed down 11.08 points, or 0.42%, at 2,605.39, in its\par second straight session of loss after a 1.9% drop the day before.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par     NEW YORK - The dollar hit a four-week top against its major peers on Thursday,\par shrugging off a U.S. credit rating downgrade that cast doubt on the country's fiscal\par outlook, and instead got a leg up from strong private payrolls data.\par     The euro fell 0.09% to $1.0928, while the U.S. dollar index scaled\par a four-week peak of 102.82, extending the previous session's 0.5% gain.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     CHINA - China's yuan inched up on Thursday against the dollar in a tight range,\par buoyed by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's continual support through a stronger-than-expected daily\par fixing and by a survey showing services activity quickened slightly in July.\par     Spot yuan opened at 7.1888 per dollar and was changing hands at 7.1916\par at midday, 4 pips stronger than the previous late session close and 0.59% weaker than\par the midpoint. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - The Australian and New Zealand dollars were on the defensive on\par Thursday as markets further scaled back expectations of rate hikes at home, leaving\par them with a yield disadvantage against their U.S counterpart.\par     The Aussie huddled at $0.6533, having shed 1.1% overnight in a second\par straight session of steep losses. The break of support at $0.6595 has turned the\par technical outlook bearish for a test of the May trough at $0.6459.\par     The kiwi dollar eased to $0.6070, after also sliding 1.1% overnight.\par Support lies around $0.6050, ahead of its May low of $0.5986.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - The Korean won weakened a little, while the benchmark bond yield climbed.\par     The won ended onshore trade 0.05% lower at 1,299.1 per dollar, after\par falling as much as 0.31% to the lowest level since July 10.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TREASURIES\par     \par     NEW YORK - U.S. long-term Treasury yields hit nine-month highs on Thursday after\par employment and other economic data pointed to easing inflation, maintaining their high\par levels in the afternoon.\par     The benchmark U.S. 10-year yields were last up 11.3 basis points (bps)\par at 4.191%, after initially hitting 4.195%, the highest since November.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - Long-dated euro zone borrowing costs rose, and the German yield curve\par reached the least inverted level since mid-June on fading recession fears after U.S.\par data and expectations for a higher for longer rate scenario.\par     Germany's 10-year bond yield, the benchmark of the euro area, was by\par 1520 GMT up 4.5 basis points (bps) at 2.55%, after hitting 2.566%, its highest level\par since July 12.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - The benchmark 10-year Japanese government bond yield hit a fresh nine-year\par peak on Thursday before pulling back after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cool\par the speed of the rise.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  offered to buy 300 billion yen ($2.09 billion) of bonds with 5-10\par years left to maturity and 100 billion yen of paper maturing in 3-5 years at 0400 GMT,\par after the 10-year yield rose to 0.655% for the first time since April\par 2014.\par     For a full report, click on\par  \par     \par     COMMODITIES\par     \par     GOLD\par \par      Gold was near a more than three-week low on Thursday, dragged by a robust dollar\par and elevated bond yields, while investors remained cautious ahead of July U.S. nonfarm\par payrolls data.\par     Spot gold was flat at $1,933.80 per ounce by 2:34 p.m. EDT (1834 GMT), after\par hitting its lowest since July 11. U.S. gold futures settled 0.3% lower at\par $1,968.80.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     IRON ORE\par     \par     Iron ore futures slumped on Thursday on fresh concerns about China's steel\par production curbs and recovery prospects for the struggling domestic property sector,\par with sentiment further dampened by flooding in the top steelmaking province of Hebei.\par     The most-traded September iron ore on China's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dalian Commodity Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par ended daytime trading 3% lower at 810.50 yuan ($112.70) per metric ton, after earlier\par hitting 808.50 yuan, its weakest since July 12.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     BASE METALS\par     \par     Tin prices rebounded on Thursday following a mining ban in part of the world's\par third largest tin miner, Myanmar, and other industrial metals also rose on the back of\par U.S. jobs data.\par     Three-month tin surged as much as 3.8% on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Metal Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  during\par Asian trading and was up 2.5% to $28,100 per metric ton by 1630 GMT.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     OIL\par     \par     Oil prices gained about 2% on Thursday as Saudi Arabia and Russia took steps to\par keep supplies tight into September and possibly beyond.\par     Brent futures rose $1.94, or 2.3%, to settle at $85.14 a barrel, while U.S.\par West Texas Intermediate crude rose $2.06, or 2.6%, to settle at $81.55.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     PALM OIL\par     \par      Malaysian palm oil futures extended early losses on Thursday, hitting its lowest\par closing since the end of June as it tracked losses in rival edible oils and concerns\par over sluggish demand.\par     The benchmark palm oil contract for October delivery on the Bursa Malaysia\par Derivatives Exchange fell 91 ringgit, or 2.32%, to 3,824 ringgit ($839.52) per metric\par ton.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     RUBBER\par     \par     Japanese rubber futures tracked Shanghai market lower on Thursday, as traders\par sought more concrete and substantial stimulus measures from China, although a weaker\par yen limited losses.\par     Osaka Exchange's rubber contract for January delivery, finished\par 0.1 yen, or 0.1%, lower at 197.9 yen ($1.38) per kg.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par \par  (Bengaluru Bureau; +91 80 6749 1130)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-3T21:30:04.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230803ej8303fx6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
