\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  for the second time this week to curb yield rise\b0\par\par\uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan entered the market for the second time this week to stem gains in benchmark sovereign bond yields, underscoring its determination to curb sharp moves in rates even as it leaves room for them to rise.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  also highlighted the challenge investors face in interpreting a rate regime that relies on gray lines to allow the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to be flexible rather than provide clarity to markets. The impact was also immediately felt in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, with the yen weakening. It triggered mild turmoil in the Tokyo stock market, which remained down.\par \par \uc2 "Two observations don't make a pattern, but for now five basis point increases could be the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's tolerance for higher moves in Japan's 10-year government bond yields," said David Forrester, a strategist at Credit Agricole in Singapore. "Such a slow rise would help limit the yen's decline against the dollar in an environment of rising \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields."\par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightened policy on Friday to allow 10-year yields to rise to 1%,its actions this week make it clear that it will not tolerate a rapid move to that level .\par \par \uc2 Thursday's unscheduled trade announcement came after the benchmark 10-year note touched a new nine-year high of 0.65%. It fell back slightly below that after the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  said it would buy 400 billion yen (US$2.8 billion) of securities in various maturities.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of rising JGB yields is reverberating through global bond markets. Japanese investors, with ultra-low interest rates at home, are the largest foreign holders of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  debt and own everything from European bonds to Brazilian bills. Every incremental yield gain at home can increase the incentive for them to repatriate funds.\par \par \uc2 One indicator that tracks banks, including \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial\uc2 , swung from an intraday gain to a loss after the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's latest move. Banks are seen as beneficiaries of higher interest rates, which widen the margins they can earn on loans after decades of struggling with Japan's ultra-low interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "It's too early to tell whether they are focusing on the level or speed at which yields rise," Shoki Omori, chief desk strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2 , said of the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 . "The 10-year yield may hit 0.7% in a week if the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's message remains unclear."\par \par \uc2 The yen swung from a gain to a loss that made it the biggest laggard against the dollar among the Group of 10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This is likely to limit the prospects of a lower USD/JPY in the near term amid a backdrop of rising US yields," he said. Moh Siong Sim, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Bank of Singapore\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230803ej8300745\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
