\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen extends losses, euro firms after GDP, inflation data\b0\par\par\uc2 1,022 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 2023\par 16:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Adds comment in paragraph 4, updates price quotes at 10:29 ET)\par     By Herbert Lash and Alun John\par        NEW YORK/LONDON, July 31 (Reuters) - The yen extended\par losses on Monday from a volatile session at the end of last week\par after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BoJ) loosened its grip on interest\par rates, but it remained on track for its first monthly gain\par against the dollar since March.\par     The dollar was little changed against a basket of major\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before key U.S. unemployment data at week's end,\par while the euro strengthened after data showed economic growth\par nudged higher and inflation ticked lower. \par     U.S. non-farm payrolls on Friday will be the first of\par several data points that will shape a Federal Reserve interest\par rate decision in late September. Before then, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par leaders will attend the Fed's Aug. 24-26 symposium in Jackson\par Hole, Wyoming, where structural shifts in the global economy\par will be in focus.\par     "We'll have to see if the data from the U.S. continues to\par paint a resilient picture of the U.S. economy, and if it does,\par that can help the dollar at least tread water between now and\par Jackson Hole," said Joe Manimbo, senior market analyst at\par Convera in Washington.\par     The dollar index fell 0.03% when measured against six\par major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But against the yen, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose\par 0.74% at 142.200 after fresh \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the BoJ on Monday.\par     The yen went into a tailspin on Friday as traders tried to\par determine the implications of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BoJ\f28 \uc2 \f2 's move to maintain\par ultra-low rates while making its bond yield curve control (YCC)\par policy more flexible and loosening its defense of a long-term\par rate cap. \par     The BoJ's policy of keeping yields pinned down has weighed\par heavily on the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the past year, and fresh\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday showed it could continue to do so. \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's benchmark 10-year government bond yield surged to a\par nine-year high, spurring the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to conduct additional\par purchase \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow its rise. The move "has likely\par surprised some market participants and encouraged yen selling\par overnight", \f28 \uc2 \f2 MUFG\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts said in a Monday note.  \par     Elsewhere in Asia, data on Monday showed China's\par manufacturing activity fell for a fourth straight month in July,\par though the China-exposed Australian dollar and Chinese shares\par were buoyed by news of further measures to spur the country's\par sputtering economic recovery.    \par     The Aussie was last up 1.2% at $0.6728, and the\par offshore yuan slipped 0.02% at 7.1474 per dollar,\par drawing some support from an announcement from \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's State\par Council\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Monday on measures to restore and expand consumption\par in the automobile, real estate and services sector.\par     The dollar was headed for a monthly loss on the prospect\par that the Fed's aggressive rate-hike cycle - a key driver of the\par dollar's strength - could have come to an end with last week's\par 25-basis-point increase.\par     The dollar is heading for its first monthly loss against the\par yen since March, and its second successive monthly loss against\par the euro and pound. \par     Data on Friday showed that the annual U.S. inflation rate\par rose in June at its slowest pace in more than two years, with\par underlying price pressure receding, easing pressure on the\par Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) to continue raising rates.\par     The euro rose 0.23% to $1.104 after data showed euro\par zone inflation fell further in July, while the bloc returned to\par growth in the second quarter of 2023 with a\par greater-than-expected expansion. \par     The euro is eyeing a monthly gain of about 1%. Last week's\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy meeting raised the possibility of a\par rate pause in September, though \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts said Monday's\par data "allow the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  to both argue for a longer hold as well as\par for another hike".\par     Sterling rose 0.04% at $1.2855, on track for a 1.3%\par monthly gain, ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2 's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 BoE\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) policy\par meeting on Thursday, with market pricing finely balanced between\par a 25 and 50 basis point increase. \par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 10:29 a.m. (1429 GMT)\par  Description      RIC         Last       U.S. Close  Pct Change     YTD Pct       High Bid    Low Bid\par                                           Previous                   Change                   \par                                           Session                                             \par  Dollar index                 101.5600   101.6100    -0.03%         -1.865%       +101.8500   +101.5400\par  Euro/Dollar                  $1.1040    $1.1015     +0.23%         +3.03%        +$1.1046    +$1.1006\par  Dollar/Yen                   142.2000   141.1800    +0.75%         +8.49%        +142.6700   +140.6900\par  Euro/Yen                     157.00     155.48      +0.98%         +11.90%       +157.2800   +155.1200\par  Dollar/Swiss                 0.8674     0.8709      -0.37%         -6.16%        +0.8729     +0.8677\par  Sterling/Dollar              $1.2856    $1.2849     +0.06%         +6.31%        +$1.2872    +$1.2829\par  Dollar/Canadian              1.3167     1.3252      -0.62%         -2.80%        +1.3261     +1.3167\par  Aussie/Dollar                $0.6728    $0.6648     +1.20%         -1.31%        +$0.6728    +$0.6650\par  Euro/Swiss                   0.9577     0.9589      -0.13%         -3.21%        +0.9623     +0.9577\par  Euro/Sterling                0.8587     0.8574      +0.15%         -2.93%        +0.8590     +0.8562\par  NZ                           $0.6221    $0.6153     +1.10%         -2.04%        +$0.6221    +$0.6156\par  Dollar/Dollar                                                                                \par  Dollar/Norway                10.1100    10.2020     -0.82%         +3.10%        +10.2130    +10.1100\par  Euro/Norway                  11.1641    11.2262     -0.55%         +6.39%        +11.2520    +11.1491\par  Dollar/Sweden                10.4959    10.5402     -0.27%         +0.84%        +10.5595    +10.4767\par  Euro/Sweden                  11.5870    11.6180     -0.27%         +3.92%        +11.6255    +11.5579\par  \par \par \par     \par  (Reporting by Herbert Lash; Additional reporting by Alun John\par in London and Rae Wee in Singapore; Editing by Himani Sarkar,\par Kim Coghill, Christina Fincher and Mark Heinrich)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-7-31T15:42:52.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230731ej7v02fch\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
