\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Choppy yen at three-week low, euro firms after GDP, inflation data\b0\par\par\uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 2023\par 13:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 31 (Reuters) - The yen softened on Monday, extending losses from a volatile session at the end of last week after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (BoJ) loosened its grip on interest rates, but it remained on track for its first monthly gain against the dollar since March.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, the euro firmed after data showed economic growth nudged higher and inflation ticked lower.\par \par \uc2 There is plenty on the agenda in the coming week, with the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  meeting on Thursday, and U.S. payrolls on Friday, the first of several data points that will shape the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's September interest rate decision.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose on Monday to 142.5 yen, its highest in three weeks in European trading, and was last up 0.87% at 142.4.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  went into a tailspin on Friday as traders tried to determine the implications of the \uc2 BoJ\uc2 's move to maintain ultra-low rates while making its bond yield curve control (YCC) policy more flexible and loosening its defence of a long-term rate cap.\par \par \uc2 The dollar eventually ended the Friday session with a 1.2% gain against the yen, though that was after it had slid 1% to a session-low of 138.05 yen.\par \par \uc2 The BoJ's policy of keeping yields pinned down has weighed heavily on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the past year, and fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday showed it could continue to do so.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark 10-year government bond yield surged to a nine-year high, spurring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct additional purchase \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow its rise. The move "has likely surprised some market participants and encouraged yen selling overnight", \uc2 MUFG\uc2  analysts said in a Monday note.\par \par \uc2 "At the same time, the yen\u8217\'20\'19s failure to sustain gains following the \uc2 BoJ\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s policy announcement last week could also reflect the message from the BoJ that it is not in a hurry to begin raising interest rates," they added.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Asia, data on Monday showed China's manufacturing activity fell for a fourth straight month in July, though the China-exposed Australian dollar and Chinese shares were buoyed by news of further measures to spur the country's sputtering economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie was last up 0.9% at $0.6707, and the offshore yuan was steady at 7.1531 per dollar, drawing some support from an announcement from \uc2 China's State Council\uc2  on Monday on measures to restore and expand consumption in the automobile, real estate and services sector.\par \par \uc2 FED HIKES DONE?\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was headed for a monthly loss on the prospect that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's aggressive rate-hike cycle - a key driver of the dollar's strength - could have concluded with last week's 25-basis-point increase.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index was last flat at 101.69, but was eyeing a monthly decline of roughly 1%, extending its loss to a second straight month.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is heading for its first monthly loss against the yen since March, and its second successive monthly loss against the euro and pound.\par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed that the annual U.S. inflation rate rose in June at its slowest pace in more than two years, with underlying price pressure receding, easing pressure on the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) to continue raising rates.\par \par \uc2 With two more monthly CPI and payrolls reports before the Fed's next meeting in September, markets have plenty of time to change their mind, and so, said \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  strategist Jim Reid, "all roads this week lead to U.S. payrolls on Friday".\par \par \uc2 The euro rose 0.21% to $1.1039 on Monday after the data showed euro zone inflation fell further in July while the bloc returned to growth in the second quarter of 2023, with a greater than expected expansion.\par \par \uc2 The euro is eyeing a monthly gain of about 1%. Last week's \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meeting raised the possibility of a rate pause in September, though \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  analysts said Monday's data "allow the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to both argue for a longer hold as well as for another hike".\par \par \uc2 Sterling gained 0.13% to $1.2850 ahead of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 's (\uc2 BoE\uc2 ) policy meeting this week, with market pricing finely balanced between a 25 and 50 basis point increase. It is set for a 1.3% monthly gain.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Rae Wee; editing by Himani Sarkar, Kim Coghill, Christina Fincher and Mark Heinrich) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-7-31T12:06:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230731ej7v01gxl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
