\par\par \b \uc2 Weaker  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Might Help China and Japan -- Barron's\b0\par\par\uc2 By Craig Mellow \par \uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2023\par 03:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 44\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 When the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  talks, markets listen. Even when it kind of mumbles.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaked guidance a few weeks ago that it was unleashing state-owned banks to sell dollars to defend the yuan. That stabilized the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a 5% slide over the previous three months.\par \par \uc2 Beijing's throat-clearing got investors thinking about Japan following suit. The yen has bounced 4% against the dollar after a 9% slide. This week's soft inflation report from the U.S. could add to this momentum, hinting at an end to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's interest-rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 So are the reserves-rich Asian giants marshaling a counterattack against the almighty dollar? Not exactly. The Bank of Japan's prime interest rate is 0.1% and China's 3.55%, compared to the Fed's 5.25%.\par \par \uc2 No amount of dollar sales will drive a sustained rally against that basic arithmetic. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can end the one-way bets against the yuan and yen, though, and maintain a measured decline rather than a rout. "Speed always kills with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves," says Edward al-Hussainy, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Columbia Threadneedle Investments\uc2 . "They can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so that the moves are less disruptive."\par \par \uc2 China and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  both have reasons to welcome a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It could support their world-beating export industries, and neither is much worried about importing inflation. Too weak a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the other hand, risks popular discontent as consumer imports become too expensive. "Last year we got a big jump in import prices, and people hated it," says Masamichi Adachi, chief Japan economist at \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Similarities between the No. 2 and No. 3 economies end there. China is grappling with an underwhelming recovery from last year's zero-Covid lockdowns. It needs a growth accelerant to whittle down enormous youth unemployment, among other pressing problems.\par \par \uc2 The classic means to achieve that would be by cutting interest rates, which would further weaken the yuan. "Their key point of tension is between measures to support the economy and increasing pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says \uc2 Michael Hirson\uc2 , head of China research at 22V Research.\par \par \uc2 Japan is enjoying its best economic mojo in quite some time. Inflation is rearing its head, which is a good thing over there; wages and home prices are both rising for the first time in decades; foreign investors are pouring in. The iShares MSCI Japan exchange-traded fund (ticker: EWJ) is up 14% this year despite the slumping yen. The Chinese equivalent, iShares MSCI China (MCHI), has lost 5%.\par \par \uc2 Investors expect the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to support the yen a bit at its next meeting July 28 -- lifting its cap on 10-year government bond yields, the so-called yield curve control, from 0.5% to perhaps 1% annually. That should suck domestic buyers back in, as the return becomes competitive with hedging costs on dollar investments, Adachi predicts.\par \par \uc2 The moderate measure could keep the yen above the 150-to-the-dollar level where the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  (successfully) last autumn. Current value is around 138.\par \par \uc2 Actually raising interest rates is less likely, says Ayako Fujita, chief economist at JPMorgan Securities Japan. More than 70% of Japanese mortgages are written with floating rates, and small businesses borrow at less than 1% annually on average. "Policy makers are very much afraid" to risk this equilibrium, she says.\par \par \uc2 With inflation tentatively entrenching itself around 2% and credit still nearly free, "real rates have never been so negative" in Japan, she says. Throw in expansionist fiscal policy and you get "perfect conditions for asset price inflation," \uc2 Fujita\uc2  concludes.\par \par \uc2 Investors might want to grab that while they can in Japan. China still has some tensions to work through.\par \par \uc2 To subscribe to Barron's, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/subscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/subscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 14, 2023 21:30 ET (01:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230715ej7f00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
