\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Tumbling US dollar a boon to risk assets across the globe\b0\par\par\uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2023\par 07:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, July 14 (Reuters) - Cooling U.S. inflation is accelerating a decline in the dollar, and risk assets around the world stand to benefit.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is down nearly 13% against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from last year\u8217\'20\'19s two-decade high and stands at its lowest level in 15 months. Its decline quickened after the U.S. reported softer-than-expected inflation data on Wednesday, supporting views that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is nearing the end of its interest rate-hiking cycle.\par \par \uc2 Because the dollar is a linchpin of the global financial system, a wide range of assets stand to benefit if it continues falling.\par \par \uc2 Weakness in the dollar can be a boon to some U.S. companies, as a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes exports more competitive abroad and makes it cheaper for multinationals to convert foreign profits back into dollars.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. technology sector, which includes some of the big growth companies that have led markets higher this year, generates just over 50% of its revenues overseas, an analysis of Russell 1000 companies by Bespoke Investment Group showed.\par \par \uc2 Raw materials, which are priced in dollars, become more affordable to foreign buyers when the dollar declines. The S&P/Goldman Sachs Commodity Index is up 4.6% this month, on pace for its best month since October.\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets benefit as well, because a falling U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes debt denominated in dollars easier to service. The MSCI International Emerging Market \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Index is up 2.4% this year.\par \par \uc2 "For markets, the weaker dollar and its underlying driver, weaker inflation, is a balm for everything, especially for assets outside the U.S.," said Alvise Marino, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . The greenback's tumble has come as \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields eased in recent days, dulling the dollar's allure while boosting a wide range of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from the Japanese yen to the Mexican peso.\par \par \uc2 "That sound you hear is the breaking of technical levels across the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," said \uc2 Karl Schamotta\uc2 , chief market strategist at Corpay. "The dollar is plunging toward levels that prevailed before the Fed started hiking, and we're seeing risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  melt up on a global basis."\par \par \uc2 A continued fall in the dollar could boost profits for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategies such as the dollar-funded carry trade, which involves the sale of dollars to buy a higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing the investor to pocket the difference.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's decline has already made the strategy a profitable one this year: An investor selling dollars and buying the Colombian peso would have collected 25% year-to-date, while the Polish zloty has yielded 13%, data from Corpay showed.\par \par \uc2 Paresh Upadhyaya, director of fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Amundi US\uc2 , is bearish on the dollar while betting on gains in the Kazakhstan tenge, Uruguayan peso and Indian rupee.\par \par \uc2 "When you look at what's going on right now, the outlook for the dollar remains pretty bleak," said Upadhyaya, who expects carry trades to thrive if the dollar keeps falling.\par \par \uc2 In the world of monetary policy, the dollar's decline may be a relief to some countries, as it removes the urgency for them to support their falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Among them is Japan. The greenback has tumbled 3% against the yen this week and is set for its biggest weekly fall against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since January. Yen weakness has been problematic for Japan's import-reliant economy and raised expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after doing so for the first time since 1998 last year.\par \par \uc2 Traders have also been watchful for potential action from \uc2 Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  given weakness in the Swedish krona . But this week, the dollar is down almost 6% against the krona and set for its biggest weekly drop since November.\par \par \uc2 Continued strength in the yen could see investors unwind the large bearish positions that have built up against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, pushing it higher, said \uc2 Societe Generale\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Kenneth Broux\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of course, being bearish the dollar has its own risks. One is a potential rebound in U.S. inflation, which could stoke bets on more Fed hawkishness and unwind many of the anti-dollar trades that have prospered this year.\par \par \uc2 Though inflation has cooled, the U.S. economy has remained resilient compared with other countries and few believe the Fed will cut rates anytime soon, which could potentially limit the dollar's near-term downside.\par \par \uc2 Still, Helen Given, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Monex USA, believes the Fed will wrap up its rate-hiking cycle before most other central banks, sapping the dollar\u8217\'20\'19s long-term momentum.\par \par \uc2 While the dollar may pare some of its recent losses, "looking six months out it's likely the dollar will be even weaker than it is today," she said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Saqib Iqbal Ahmed; Additional reporting by Dhara Ranasinghe and Ira Iosebashvili; Writing by Ira Iosebashvili; Editing by Leslie Adler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-7-14T06:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230714ej7e00e9x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
