\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EXPLAINER-What would Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  to boost the weak yen look like?\b0\par\par\uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2023\par 01:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story first published on June 29 with no changes to text)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 29 (Reuters) - Japanese authorities are facing renewed pressure to combat a continued yen fall driven by market expectations that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will keep interest rates ultra-low, even as other central banks tighten monetary policy to curb inflation.\par \par \uc2 Aside from verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government has several options to stem what it considers excessive yen falls. Among them is to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying large amounts of yen, usually selling dollars for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Below are details on how yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could work, the likelihood of this happening and challenges of such a move:\par \par \uc2 LAST YEN-BUYING \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought yen in September, its first foray in the market to boost its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998, after a \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) decision to maintain ultra-loose policy drove the yen as low as 145 per dollar. It \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in October after the yen plunged to a 32-year low of 151.94.\par \par \uc2 WHY STEP IN?\par \par \uc2 Yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is rare. Far more often the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold yen to prevent its rise from hurting the export-reliant economy by making Japanese goods less competitive overseas.\par \par \uc2 But yen weakness is now seen as problematic, with Japanese firms having shifted production overseas and the economy heavily reliant on imports for goods ranging from fuel and raw materials to machinery parts.\par \par \uc2 WHAT HAPPENS FIRST?\par \par \uc2 When Japanese authorities escalate their verbal warnings to say they "stand ready to act decisively" against speculative moves, that is a sign \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be imminent.\par \par \uc2 A rate check by the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 , a practice in which \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials call dealers and ask for the price of buying or selling yen, is seen by traders as a possible precursor to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LINE IN THE SAND?\par \par \uc2 Authorities say they look at the speed of yen falls, rather than levels, and whether the moves are driven by speculators, in deciding whether to step in.\par \par \uc2 Market players, however, see the first threshold at 145 yen to the dollar, where \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the dollar breaks above that, 150 yen could be the next line in the sand, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 WHAT TRIGGER?\par \par \uc2 The decision is highly political. When public anger over the weak yen and a subsequent rise in the cost of living is high, that puts pressure on the administration to respond. This was the case when Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 But while inflation remains above the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's 2% target, public pressure has declined as fuel and global commodity prices have fallen from last year's peaks.\par \par \uc2 If the pace of yen declines accelerates and draws the ire of media and public, the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would rise again.\par \par \uc2 The decision would not be easy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is costly and could easily fail, given that even a large burst of yen buying would pale next to the $7.5 trillion that change hands daily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 HOW WOULD IT WORK?\par \par \uc2 When \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen rises, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues short-term bills, raising yen it then sells to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To support the yen, however, the authorities must tap Japan's foreign reserves for dollars to sell for yen.\par \par \uc2 In either case, the finance minister issues the order to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  executes the order as the ministry's agent.\par \par \uc2 CHALLENGES?\par \par \uc2 Yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more difficult than yen-selling.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds nearly $1.3 trillion in foreign reserves, they could be substantially eroded if Tokyo repeatedly spent huge for yen.\par \par \uc2 That means there are limits to how long \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could keep defending the yen, unlike for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - where \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can essentially print yen by issuing bills.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities also consider it important to seek the support of \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  partners, notably the United States if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  involves the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Washington gave tacit approval when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year, reflecting recent close bilateral relations.\par \par \uc2 But stepping in repeatedly would be difficult, as Washington traditionally opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in cases of extreme market volatility. (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Additional reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto and Kentaro Sugiyama; Editing by William Mallard)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-7-6T23:46:29.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230703ej73031yd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
