\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar softer after PCE data, yen briefly breaks 145 mark\b0\par\par\uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2023\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Writes through with updated prices, byline, previous dateline LONDON)\par \par \uc2 By Chuck Mikolajczak\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, June 30 (Reuters) - The dollar index was lower on Friday following two straight days of gains, after economic data showed a cooling in consumer spending, raising some doubt about the potential aggressiveness of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Commerce Department said consumer spending ticked up 0.1% in May while data for the prior month was revised to show spending accelerated by 0.6% versus the previously reported 0.8%. The personal consumption expenditures (PCE) gained 0.1% for the month after a 0.4% rise in April while advancing 3.8% on an annual basis, slowing from a revised 4.3% the prior month.\par \par \uc2 The Fed tracks PCE gauges for its 2% inflation target.\par \par \uc2 "Spending was weak, especially in inflation adjusted terms. Goods spending fell and even services spending looks to be sputtering," said \uc2 Brian Jacobsen\uc2 , chief economist at Annex Wealth Management in Menomonee Falls, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation is drifting lower. The off-ramp to 2% inflation is a long one, though."\par \par \uc2 The dollar index fell 0.377% at 102.920.\par \par \uc2 The index had risen 0.82% over the prior two sessions after comments from Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  and solid economic data heightened market expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise rates two more times this year, while cooling the belief a rate cut could be in the cards by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Expectations for a 25 basis point hike at the Fed's July meeting dipped slightly, with markets now pricing in an 84.3% chance of a hike, down slightly from the 89.3% on Thursday, according to \uc2 CME\uc2 's FedWatch Tool.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index is up 0.4% for the quarter and is poised to snap a streak of back-to-back quarterly declines. For the first half, the greenback is off 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen strengthened 0.23% was on track to snap a three-day run of weakening against the greenback at 144.41 per dollar, after briefly topping the 145 mark with a fresh 7-month high of 145.07.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been watching to see if the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, which last happened at around the 145 mark as U.S. and \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy plans are likely to remain counter to each other.\par \par \uc2 The greenback is up nearly 9% for the quarter against the yen, which would mark its strongest in a year.\par \par \uc2 The pound is one of the best performing developed market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the second quarter up, 2.5%, while the dollar index, which tracks the unit against six major peers, is up 0.8% on the quarter, set for its first quarterly gain since the third quarter of 2022.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's gains also helped it to as high as 145.07 yen in Asia trade Friday, its highest in seven months, and into territory at which Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last autumn.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  on Friday warned the country will take the appropriate steps should the yen continue to weaken, and against investors selling the yen too far, echoing similar comments from other government ministers and officials this week.\par \par \uc2 Earlier data showed core inflation in Tokyo ticked higher in June and remained above the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's 2% target for the 13th month, keeping pressure on \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  policymakers to scale back their ultra-easy monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, euro zone inflation data fell for a third consecutive month, but showed a small drop in underlying inflation and was unlikely to keep the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from hiking rates at its July meeting.\par \par \uc2 The euro was up 0.42% to $1.091 while Sterling was last trading at $1.2692, up 0.63% on the day.\par \par \uc2 Data showed Britain's economy grew by just 0.1% in the first quarter, as inflation sapped disposable income in households.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Chuck Mikolajczak; editing by Philippa Fletcher) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-30T15:19:26.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230630ej6u020rj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
