\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia shares steady as weak China data fuel stimulus hopes, yen frail\b0\par\par\uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2023\par 07:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 30 (Reuters) - Asian stocks inched higher on Friday as weak factory activity data from China stoked expectations of fresh stimulus, even as strong U.S. economic data bolstered the view that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will stay hawkish for longer.\par \par \uc2 The yen remained fragile after hitting the psychologically important barrier of 145 per dollar , fuelling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  issued a another warning against excessive weakening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Futures indicated European stocks were set for a higher open, with Eurostoxx 50 futures up 0.18%, German DAX futures up 0.24% and FTSE futures 0.28% higher.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was 0.3% higher in choppy trading, on course to eke out a gain of just over 1% in the first half of the year.\par \par \uc2 Investors' focus on Friday will be on the U.S. Personal Consumption Expenditures (PCE) index reading, the Fed's favoured inflation gauge.\par \par \uc2 But before that euro zone June inflation data will likely provide cues to the broader picture in the region, after data on Thursday showed German inflation rose more than expected in June.\par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters expect euro zone inflation to fall to 5.6% in June from 6.1% in May.\par \par \uc2 While German consumer prices, harmonised to compare with other \uc2 European Union\uc2  countries, rose by a higher-than-anticipated 6.8% on the year in June, inflation fell sharply in Spain and Italy.\par \par \uc2 "There is a growing divergence in the path of inflation across the region, which is leading to some disagreement about the right path for policy," said \uc2 Rob Carnell\uc2 , ING's regional head of research, Asia-Pacific.\par \par \uc2 "Though one suspects that the response will be, if in doubt, hike."\par \par \uc2 Data through the week has painted a picture of a resilient U.S. economy that has eased some of the worries of an impending recession but also stoked expectations that the Fed will stay on its hawkish path.\par \par \uc2 The number of Americans filing new claims for unemployment benefits unexpectedly fell last week, pointing to continued labour market strength, while first quarter GDP was sharply revised upward.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  signalled on Thursday that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to resume its monetary tightening campaign after a break earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 The strong economic data sent Treasury yields higher, with the yield on 10-year Treasury notes touching a three-month high of 3.868% on Thursday. It was last at 3.839%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, China's factory activity declined for a third straight month in June and weakness in other sectors deepened, official surveys showed on Friday, adding pressure for authorities to do more to shore up growth as demand falters at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 China's blue-chip CSI300 Index and the Shanghai Composite Index rose nearly 1%, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was up 0.24% on stimulus hopes.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the yen remained in spotlight, briefly trading at 145.07 per dollar - a fresh seven month low and breaking through the 145 barrier that analysts have been watching out for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When the yen breached the 145 level last September, authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in 24 years. The yen was last at 144.67 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The government is watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves with a great sense of urgency," \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  said. "We will respond appropriately if the moves become excessive."\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities are under pressure to combat a continued yen fall driven by market expectations that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will keep interest rates ultra-low, even as other central banks tighten monetary policy to curb inflation.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude was flat at $69.87 per barrel and Brent was at $74.49, up 0.2% on the day.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Ankur Banerjee; Editing by Stephen Coates and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-30T06:56:38.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230630ej6u00el1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
