\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks in the summer swing, yen bows to dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 1,036 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2023\par 15:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Europe shares rise, U.S. futures up slightly\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Yuan eases despite \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's firmer-than-expected guidance\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Yen pressured after Ueda contrasted with Powell, Lagarde\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Gold slips to 3-month low\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 29 (Reuters) - World shares and the dollar inched higher while gold hit a three-month low on Thursday as focus flipped between the battle to lower inflation, the health of the U.S. economy and banks, and possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in China and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wall Street was set to open higher after a pre-market open upward revision to Q1 GDP data and Wednesday's news that the biggest U.S. banks such as \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  had sailed through annual health checks.\par \par \uc2 'Big Tech' added to its rampant 70% rally this year overnight and Europe's STOXX 600 index was making gains too as bumper profits from fashion giant \uc2 H&M\uc2 's summer collection lifted its shares 16.5%.\par \par \uc2 Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kicked the European day off with another interest rate hike, but the fact the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  didn't go for a bigger than 25 basis point move pushed the Swedish crown to a record low.\par \par \uc2 It all tied in with the multi-trillion dollar question economists are struggling with. Where is stubbornly high inflation heading?\par \par \uc2 Spain reported its annual inflation rate had dropped to 1.9% in June, its lowest since March 2021. Equivalent numbers from Europe's biggest economy, Germany, though were stronger again, coming too as the world\u8217\'20\'19s top central bankers decamped from an \uc2 ECB\uc2 -hosted get-together near Lisbon.\par \par \uc2 "We are entering a delicate phase for monetary policy given the lags," S&P's Global Chief Economist \uc2 Paul Gruenwald\uc2  said as the firm predicted a further rise in default rates in many parts of the world.\par \par \uc2 "If inflation remains sticky, rates will need to go higher. But if central banks have overtightened, growth will slow sharply."\par \par \uc2 In Asia, MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan had fallen 0.5% with holidays in Singapore, India and Malaysia making for thinner trading.\par \par \uc2 Chinese blue chips fell 0.5% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng index slumped 1.2%. Japan's Nikkei, gave up most of its early gains to end up a modest 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Most of the focus though remained on the region's two biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japan's yen and China's yuan, which have both been under intense pressure in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 The yuan eased to 7.2491 per dollar, just a whisker away from its eight-month trough hit a day ago. That was despite another stronger-than-expected official rate from the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , which investors read as Beijing trying to steady the yuan.\par \par \uc2 The yen, meanwhile, touched a more than seven-month low versus the dollar. The dollar's surge of more than 11% against the yen since late March has seen it reach 144.71 yen and prompted increased warnings from Japanese government officials this week about the speed of the move.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last autumn when the dollar strengthened beyond 145 yen. It was at 144.24 in European trading.\par \par \uc2 "The playbook of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is consistent with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  happening soon and if it gets above 145 we could quite easily get to see them \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," said ING global head of markets \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Shane Oliver\uc2 , chief economist at AMP in Sydney, said though that China might not mind its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling a bit further because it helps support its giant export sector.\par \par \uc2 "But they probably don't want it to fall too rapidly because then it looks a bit like a panic," he added.\par \par \uc2 GERMAN ANGST\par \par \uc2 Overnight, U.S. share markets had ended broadly flat although the high-flying Nasdaq had managed another small gain as \uc2 Apple\uc2  closed at a fresh record high.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  had said in Portugal that U.S. interest rates are likely to rise further and did not rule out a July hike. Notably, he said he did not see inflation abating to the 2% target until 2025.\par \par \uc2 In the bond markets, U.S. and European yields - a proxy for borrowing costs - were driving higher again.\par \par \uc2 In contrast to Spain's and Italy's softer data, German consumer prices, harmonised to compare with other \uc2 European Union\uc2  countries, rose by a more-than-anticipated 6.8% year-on-year.\par \par \uc2 Alongside the upward revision in U.S. Q1 GDP, Thursday's U.S. data also saw an unexpected drop in the number of Americans filing new claims for unemployment benefits, a sign of continued strength in the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 Germany's 10 year bond yield, the benchmark for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc, was 6.7 basis points (bps) higher at 2.38% and two-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields were up at 4.8% surpassing the highs hit on Wednesday after Powell comments.\par \par \uc2 Futures see about an 80% chance the Fed will raise interest rates by 25 basis points in July, before holding rates steady for the remainder of the year.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  had cemented expectations for a ninth consecutive rise in euro zone rates in July on Wednesday and markets have all but priced in two more rate hikes from the \uc2 ECB\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) Governor \uc2 Kazuo Ueda\uc2  had reiterated that "there's still some distance to go" in sustainably achieving 2% inflation, the conditions the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  has set for considering an exit from ultra-easy stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Investors are now awaiting the U.S. PCE index on Friday, the Fed's favoured inflation gauge. Analysts polled by Reuters expect the core rate to be 4.7% on a year-over-year basis, still well above the Fed's 2% target.\par \par \uc2 "Markets seem stuck in a holding pattern, watching in awe the inconsistencies between risk sentiment, yield curves, data surprises and inflation," said \uc2 Mark McCormick\uc2 , global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and EM Strategy at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Stella Qiu in Sydney; Editing by Christina Fincher and Susan Fenton) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-29T14:24:22.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230629ej6t01yal\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
