\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks shrug off inflation angst, yen bows to dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 971 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2023\par 11:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Europe shares opens flat, U.S. futures up slightly\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Yuan eases despite \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's firmer-than-expected guidance\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Yen pressured after Ueda contrasted with Powell, Lagarde\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Gold slips to 3-month low\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 29 (Reuters) - World shares and the dollar inched higher and gold was at a three-month low on Thursday as traders' attention continued to swing between the battle to lower inflation and speculation about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in China and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Europe's regional STOXX 600 index barely budged in early trading after what had been its biggest rise in almost a month the previous day, while futures markets were pointing to a fractionally higher start on Wall Street later.\par \par \uc2 Sweden had already kicked the day off with another interest rate hike, while one of its biggest firms and one of Europe's largest fashion retailers \uc2 H&M\uc2  saw its shares hit a 16-month high after forecast-beating results.\par \par \uc2 It all tied in with the multi-trillion dollar question economists are struggling with. Where is stubbornly high inflation heading?\par \par \uc2 Spain reported its annual inflation rate had dropped to 1.9% in June, its lowest since March 2021. Equivalent numbers from Europe's biggest economy, Germany, are due out too while the world\u8217\'20\'19s top central bankers were decamping from an \uc2 ECB\uc2 -hosted get-together near Lisbon.\par \par \uc2 "We are entering a delicate phase for monetary policy given the lags," S&P's Global Chief Economist \uc2 Paul Gruenwald\uc2  said as the firm predicted a further rise in default rates in many parts of the world.\par \par \uc2 "If inflation remains sticky, rates will need to go higher. But if central banks have overtightened, growth will slow sharply."\par \par \uc2 Overnight in Asia, MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan had fallen 0.5% with holidays in Singapore, India and Malaysia making for thinner trading.\par \par \uc2 Chinese blue chips fell 0.3% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng index slumped 1.3%. Japan's Nikkei, however, gave up earlier gains to be up 0.1%\par \par \uc2 Most of the focus remained on the region's two biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japan's yen and China's yuan, which have both been under intense pressure in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 The yuan eased to 7.2491 per dollar, just a whisker away from its eight-month trough hit a day ago. That was despite another stronger-than-expected official rate from the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , which investors read as Beijing trying to steady the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Japan's yen, meanwhile, touched a more than seven-month low versus the dollar. The dollar's surge of more than 11% against the yen since late March has seen it reach 144.71 yen and prompted increased warnings from Japanese government officials this week about the speed of the move.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last autumn when the dollar strengthened beyond 145 yen. It was at 144.24 in European trading.\par \par \uc2 "The playbook of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is consistent with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  happening soon and if it gets above 145 we could quite easily get to see them \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," said ING global head of markets \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Shane Oliver\uc2 , chief economist at AMP in Sydney said though that China might not mind its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling a bit further because it helps support its giant export sector\par \par \uc2 "But they probably don't want it to fall too rapidly because then it looks a bit like a panic," he added.\par \par \uc2 GERMAN ANGST\par \par \uc2 Overnight, U.S. share markets had ended broadly flat although the high-flying Nasdaq had managed another small gain as \uc2 Apple\uc2  closed at a fresh record high.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  had said in Portugal that U.S. interest rates are likely to rise further and did not rule out a July hike. Notably, he said he did not see inflation abating to the 2% target until 2025.\par \par \uc2 In the bond markets, European yields - a proxy for borrowing costs - were inching up again.\par \par \uc2 In contrast to Spain's data, news that Nordrhein-Westfalen's inflation rate had ticked up again bolstered expectations for something similar from the German-wide figure later given that NW is the country's most populous state.\par \par \uc2 Germany's 10 year bond yield, the benchmark for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc, was 4.5 basis points (bps) higher at 2.36%, while the two-year yield was up 4 bps at 3.21%.\par \par \uc2 Two-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields were up at 4.759% too although still below the 4.778% that had touched on Wednesday after Powell comments.\par \par \uc2 Futures see about an 80% chance the Fed will raise interest rates by 25 basis points in July, before holding rates steady for the remainder of the year.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 , on the other hand, further cemented expectations for a ninth consecutive rise in euro zone rates in July. Markets have all but priced in two more rate hikes from the \uc2 ECB\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) Governor \uc2 Kazuo Ueda\uc2  reiterated that "there's still some distance to go" in sustainably achieving 2% inflation, the conditions the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  has set for considering an exit from ultra-easy stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Investors are now awaiting the U.S. PCE index on Friday, the Fed's favoured inflation gauge. Analysts polled by Reuters expect the core rate to be 4.7% on a year-over-year basis, still well above the Fed's 2% target.\par \par \uc2 "Markets seem stuck in a holding pattern, watching in awe the inconsistencies between risk sentiment, yield curves, data surprises and inflation," said \uc2 Mark McCormick\uc2 , global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and EM Strategy at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Stella Qiu in Sydney; Editing by Christina Fincher) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-29T10:20:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230629ej6t014cu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
