\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares subdued, yen and yuan hover near 8-mth troughs\b0\par\par\uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2023\par 04:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, June 29 (Reuters) - Asian shares were sudued on Thursday after global central banks reaffirmed their inflation-fighting resolve, warning rates may need to rise further, while the yen and the Chinese yuan struggled to lift from lows amid fears of official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was flat, while markets in Singapore, India and Malaysia are closed for holidays. Chinese blue chips slid 0.3% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng index fell 0.7%.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei, however, gained 1% and was headed for a monthly rise of 8.5% and a quarterly jump of 19%.\par \par \uc2 The offshore yuan hovered near an eight-month trough at 7.24 per dollar on Thursday, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the daily guidance at the weakest level since November.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, U.S. shares were mixed. The Nasdaq managed a small gain with support from tech stocks, with \uc2 Apple\uc2  registered a record closing high, while the Dow closed slightly lower.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2  rose 3% after the bell. The company's forecast of third-quarter results beat estimates, powered by demand from the booming artificial intelligence and an easing supply glut.\par \par \uc2 At a \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forum on Wednesday, Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said the Fed will likely raise rates further and did not rule out a a hike for July. Notably, he said he did not see inflation abating to the 2% target until 2025.\par \par \uc2 "The messaging was broadly a continuation of views signposted in previous comments and market reaction was relatively modest," said Stephen Wu, an economist at the \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Indeed, two-year Treasury yields closed at 4.722% after briefly spiking to 4.778%, as bond market continued to cast doubt on Fed's hawkishness of two more hikes. They were little changed on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Futures see about an 80% chance the Fed will raise interest rates by 25 basis points in July, before holding rates steady for the remainder of the year.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 , on the other hand, cemented expectations for a ninth consecutive rise in euro zone rates in July. Markets have all but priced in two more rate hikes from the \uc2 ECB\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) Governor Kazuo Ueda said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would see good reason to shift monetary policy if it became "reasonably sure" that inflation would accelerate into 2024 after a period of moderation.\par \par \uc2 Investors are now waiting for the U.S. Personal Consumption Expenditures (PCE) index reading on Friday, the Fed's favored inflation gauge. Analysts polled by Reuters expect the core rate to be 4.7% on a year-over-year basis, still well above the Fed's 2% target.\par \par \uc2 "Markets seem stuck in a holding patterns, watching in awe the inconsistencies between risk sentiment, yield curves, data surprises and inflation," said \uc2 Mark McCormick\uc2 , global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and EM Strategy at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "For U.S., disinflation is the main driver and sending the strongest directional H2 cue for the USD: choppy but lower."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was little changed against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, after rising 0.5% overnight, aided by hawkish comments from Powell and quarter-end rebalancing flows.\par \par \uc2 The greenback is down 0.5% in the first half of the year after hitting a decade high last year.\par \par \uc2 The yen regained some composure on Thursday, rising 0.2% to 144.26, but still just a touch below an eight-month low of 144.62 hit overnight, with markets on egde for signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese officials.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices were flat on Thursday. U.S. crude futures were little changed at 69.55 per barrel, and Brent crude was down 0.1% at $74.00 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices were 0.1% higher at $1,909.59 per ounce.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Stella Qiu; Editing by Lincoln Feast.) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-29T03:13:12.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230629ej6t005xx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
