\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-US stock futures tip lower, yen stumbles and euro shines\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2023\par 14:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Yen near 15-yr low on rising euro, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a risk\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Gold touches 4-month low\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Upbeat US data, hawkish \uc2 ECB\uc2  fuels rate speculation\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Tech stocks hit by report US to limit AI chips for China\par \par \uc2 (Adds quote in paragraph 4, refocuses paragraph 1,2 to US and updates pricing throughout)\par \par \uc2 By Nell Mackenzie\par \par \uc2 LONDON June 28 (Reuters) -\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks were poised to open lower on Wednesday pressured by reports of a possible curb on U.S. tech exports to China, while investors awaited comments from Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  to gauge the path for interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Shares of chip companies \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  fell as much as 4.6% in premarket trading, while \uc2 Advanced Micro Devices\uc2  dipped 3.5% after the Wall Street Journal reported the Commerce Department was mulling stopping shipments of artificial intelligence (AI) chips made by these companies to China as early as July.\par \par \uc2 Treasury yields inched higher on the strength of U.S. data, combined with hawkish commentary emerging from Portugal at a gathering of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavyweights that include Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Governor Kazuo Ueda.\par \par \uc2 "In Europe, the data has to evolve to avoid a hike in September rather than the other way around," said James Rossiter, head of global macro strategy at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 Rossiter noted how \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaker \uc2 Martins Kazaks\uc2  said on Tuesday that in order for the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to pause rate hikes in September, the region would need to see a sustained fall in inflation over the coming months.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe we'll see some successes on inflation here and there, but it just won't be enough," said Rossiter.\par \par \uc2 European inflation and rate hike hints from the Portugal meeting were also a focus for Tim Graf, head of EMEA macro strategy at \uc2 State Street Global Advisors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Unless you get some big exogenous shock between now and the next \uc2 ECB\uc2  meeting, they're going to hike rates again," he said.\par \par \uc2 Across the Atlantic, the U.S. economy continues to show resilience in the face of tighter monetary conditions, he added.\par \par \uc2 "Recession probability models in the U.S. project a 55-70% to 65-70% probability we'll get a recession in the next 12 months. But we've been hearing this for the last 12 months, and it's not here," said Graf.\par \par \uc2 A broader bullish sentiment helped MSCI's broadest index of global shares tick up 0.2%. at 1134 GMT.\par \par \uc2 U.S. money market futures now imply around a 77% chance of a hike to 5.25-5.5%, and slightly more risk of a further move to 5.5-5.75%, which nudged short-term Treasury yields higher.\par \par \uc2 EURO ON THE RISE\par \par \uc2 Bond yields also moved sharply higher in Europe after a bevy of central bankers sounded hawkish on inflation and warned rates would likely have to stay higher for longer.\par \par \uc2 Markets imply a 90% probability of an \uc2 ECB\uc2  rate hike to 3.75% in July and a peak around 4.0%.\par \par \uc2 That underpinned the euro against the dollar at $1.095 , while keeping it near a 15-year peak of 157.98 yen .\par \par \uc2 The dollar had hit a near eight-month top of 144.26 yen , before easing back to 144.15 as Japanese officials again protested against the yen's weakness.\par \par \uc2 Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Masato Kanda on Wednesday warned against further falls in the yen, saying authorities would take an appropriate response if moves became excessive.\par \par \uc2 Markets are wary in case \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the yen as it did last October, which knocked the dollar down from 151.94 to as low as 144.50 in a matter of hours.\par \par \uc2 In commodities, gold slipped past a four-month low to $1,905.35 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices edged up after data showed a larger-than-expected draw in U.S. crude and gasoline inventories, but they remain uncomfortably close to lows for the year so far.\par \par \uc2 Brent lay flat at $72.24 a barrel after dropping a dollar from earlier highs, while U.S. crude was also largely flat at $67.67.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Nell Mackenize; Editing by Amanda Cooper, Kim Coghill, Conor Humphries and Chizu Nomiyama) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-28T13:12:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230628ej6s01nvl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
