\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Oil climbs, rouble falls as markets take mutiny in stride\b0\par\par\uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2023\par 07:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Oil bounces as much as 1.3%; rouble hits 15-month low\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 MSCI AxJ index touches three-week low\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market broadly steady; yen firm\par \par \uc2 By Tom Westbrook\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 26 (Reuters) - Oil was slightly higher on Monday and the rouble lower as an abortive weekend mutiny by Russian mercenaries raised questions about Russian stability and crude supply, but left investors hesitant to draw any further conclusions.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures were last up 0.2% at $74.02 a barrel having earlier fetched as much as $74.80. The rouble dropped to a 15-month low early in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan slipped to a three-week low, as small falls in China, Taiwan and Australia offset minor gains in South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei eased 0.1%. The battered yen rose marginally on hints at looming government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it and after a summary showing a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board called for an early revision of yield curve control.\par \par \uc2 European futures gained 0.3%, S&P 500 futures rose 0.2% and FTSE futures added 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Russian mercenaries made a short-lived rebellion on Saturday, seizing the southern city of Rostov and advancing on Moscow demanding the removal of Russian military commanders in charge of the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The private Wagner army then withdrew after striking a deal guaranteeing their safety and the passage of their leader, \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 , to Belarus.\par \par \uc2 The consequences for the Ukraine war were not clear, though the challenge to Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's authority was the starkest in decades of his leadership.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the market can get its head around working out if there are implications," said \uc2 Ray Attrill\uc2 , head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  said one concern was the possibility of martial law in Russia and its effect on the workforce at ports and oil production facilities.\par \par \uc2 Gold, which had hit a three-month low on Friday, rose 0.2% to $1,925 an ounce. U.S. Treasuries were firm with yields, which fall when prices rise, marginally lower.\par \par \uc2 Two-year yields fell 2 basis points to 4.731%. Ten-year yields fell 1.8 bps to 3.721%.\par \par \uc2 "This putsch... has revealed cracks and fragilities that now cannot be unseen," said \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  economist Vishnu Varathan.\par \par \uc2 "It undeniably amplifies global geopolitical risks."\par \par \uc2 CHINA FOCUS\par \par \uc2 With the mutiny being on the watchlist rather than driving action in Asia, investors were left to pore over the latest signs of China's recovery stalling, which on Monday was softer-than-hoped-for travel figures for last week's holiday.\par \par \uc2 S&P Global also followed most Wall Street banks and cut its 2023 GDP growth forecast for China on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Blue chip stocks fell 0.7% in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 The yuan slid to catch up offshore falls during the break on Thursday and Friday, but the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  fixed the midpoint of the its trading band surprisingly strong, suggesting it might not be so tolerant of further weakness.\par \par \uc2 The yuan was last at a seven-month low of 7.2199 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The risk-sensitive Australian dollar was steady at $0.6683. The euro nursed last week's modest drop at $1.0903 and sterling held at $1.2730.\par \par \uc2 The yen, down nearly 9% this year as global interest rate expectations rise and Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stays dovish, bounced as much as 0.3% to 143.27 per dollar, partly thanks to speculation around \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or a policy shift.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Masato Kanda toughened his tone on Monday, describing recent moves as "rapid and one-sided" in a possible prelude to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy yen.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaker also called for revision to its yield curve control policy, a summary of opinions at the June meeting showed on Monday, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ultra-loose monetary settings may be at a crossroads.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Stephen Coates and Muralikumar Anantharaman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-26T06:39:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230626ej6q00i21\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
