\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Oil firm, stocks wobbly after short-lived Russian mutiny\b0\par\par\uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2023\par 04:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *               \par \par \uc2 Oil bounces as much as 1.3%                              \par \par \uc2 *               \par \par \uc2 MSCI AxJ index touches three-week low                              \par \par \uc2 *               \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market broadly steady; yen firm                              \par \par \uc2 By Tom Westbrook               \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 26 (Reuters) - Oil was slightly higher on Monday as an abortive weekend mutiny by Russian mercenaries raised questions about crude supply, while stocks lacked direction as investors waited for more clarity around the situation.               \par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures were last up 0.4% at $74.14 having earlier drifted as high as $74.80. MSCI's index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan dipped to a three-week low before floating around either side of flat.               \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei bounced around, too, and was last trading flat. The safe-haven yen rose marginally, helped by hints at possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it and by a summary showing a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member pushed in June for a debate on its yield curve control policy.               \par \par \uc2 S&P 500 and European futures rose 0.3%.               \par \par \uc2 Russian mercenaries made a short-lived rebellion on Saturday, seizing the southern city of Rostov and advancing on Moscow demanding the removal of Russian military commanders in charge of the war in Ukraine.               \par \par \uc2 The private Wagner army then withdrew after striking a deal guaranteeing their safety and the exile of their leader, \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 , to Belarus. The consequences for the Ukraine war were not clear, though the challenge to Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's authority was the starkest in decades of his leadership.               \par \par \uc2 "Geopolitical risk amid internal instability in Russia has increased," said \uc2 Rystad Energy\uc2  analysts Jorge Leon. "As such, we are likely to see a marginal uptick in oil prices in the coming days, if the situation does not deteriorate."               \par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  said one concern was the possibility of martial law in Russia and its effect on the workforce at ports and production facilities.               \par \par \uc2 U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  said the turmoil in Russia could take months to play out, while Italy's foreign minister said it had shattered the "myth" of Russian unity.                              \par \par \uc2 CHINA FOCUS               \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere markets were already on edge about a darkening growth outlook, as China's post-pandemic recovery stalls and global interest rates remain high, and traders were reluctant to take any new positions on the basis of Russian events.               \par \par \uc2 The risk-sensitive Australian dollar was steady at $0.6679. The euro nursed last week's modest drop at $1.0906 and sterling held at $1.2728.               \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the market can get its head around working out if there are implications," said \uc2 Ray Attrill\uc2 , head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  in Sydney.               \par \par \uc2 "People may think that ultimately Putin's grip on power is weakened here. Maybe the Ukrainians may be emboldened to be upping their counteroffensives," he said, but without obvious progress traders in Asia would be focused on China.               \par \par \uc2 On that front, S&P Global followed most Wall Street banks and cut its 2023 GDP growth forecast for China on Sunday.               \par \par \uc2 The yuan opened sharply lower onshore, catching up with offshore falls on Thursday and Friday, but the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  fixed the midpoint of the its trading band slightly firmer than some expectations, which perhaps dampened further falls.               \par \par \uc2 The yuan was last at 7.2094 per dollar.               \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen, which has been on a slide as global interest rate expectations rise and Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stays steadfastly dovish, bounced as much as 0.3% to 143.27 per dollar, before steadying back to 143.51.               \par \par \uc2 Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Masato Kanda said on Monday authorities will respond to any excessive moves and did not rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as happened last year. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should also discuss revising its yield curve control policy at an early stage, a board member was quoted as saying at a June policy meeting, a summary of opinions released on Monday showed.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-26T03:29:09.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230626ej6q00795\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
