\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Japan will stop yen decline beyond USD/JPY 145, most economists say\b0\par\par\uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2023\par 06:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/econ-polls?RIC=JPGDPQP GDP poll data\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/econ-polls?RIC=JPCPIQP core CPI poll data\par \par \uc2 By Satoshi Sugiyama\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, June 15 (Reuters) - Japan's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act to stop the yen's decline if it depreciates to the 145 per U.S. dollar level, more than half of economists polled by Reuters said.\par \par \uc2 Market participants closely watch how the government and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) respond to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, following their meeting last month when the yen neared a six-month low and ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate review concluding on Friday.\par \par \uc2 While 96% of the poll's respondents expect the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to maintain its policy this week, around half see a rollback of easing, including a tweak to the yield curve control (YCC) scheme, in either July or September.\par \par \uc2 Fifteen of 28 economists (54%) said the government and the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  will take steps such as issuing a warning or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market once the yen weakens beyond 145 per greenback, the June 8-13 poll found. Another 12 said 150 yen is the trigger.\par \par \uc2 "Domestic firms' weak-yen tolerance has improved thanks to strong tourist demand, but a sharp yen decline would torment manufacturers because soft overseas demand has made the benefit of a weak yen hardly visible", said S&P Global Market Intelligence's Principal Economist Harumi Taguchi.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the policymakers may weigh in if the yen weakens quickly, or if the depreciation is feared to prolong domestic inflation and squeeze households' purchasing power.\par \par \uc2 In a separate question on the weak yen's impact on \uc2 BOJ\uc2  policy, nine economists (31%) said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decisions could be swayed by a yen depreciation beyond 145 per dollar. Ten said 150 is the threshold, three opted for 155 and two chose 160 or further.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 , the finance ministry and the \uc2 Financial Services Agency\uc2  held a three-party talk on May 30, similar to another one last year that served as a prelude to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 24 years in September.\par \par \uc2 The yen hit a 32-year low near 152 to the dollar in October but then reversed course, as the government carried out additional \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  jolted the market with a surprise YCC tweak in December. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at 140.885 per dollar around midday on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 STAY ON COURSE, FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  is expected to maintain ultra-loose monetary policy at the latest two-day meeting from Thursday, sources familiar with the bank's thinking have told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 In the poll, all but one - \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  - out of 28 economists corroborated the view, citing an improved bond market functionality and Governor Kazuo Ueda's accommodative remarks so far.\par \par \uc2 Still, nearly two-thirds of respondents predicted the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  would scale back the current policy this year, although the margin has shrunk from 71% in last month's survey. The ratio of economists expecting an unwinding in July was largely unchanged at around 43%.\par \par \uc2 "July could be the best timing to tweak YCC, at the time of the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's quarterly inflation forecast release and before the likely U.S. recession later this year", said \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe\uc2 , senior economist at \uc2 Sony Financial Group\uc2 , who expects the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to double the cap around the 10-year yield target to 1.0%.\par \par \uc2 Also, more than 70% of the economists polled said Japan's 2024 wage growth will likely remain at a level that is sufficient for the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to consider ending or tweaking YCC.\par \par \uc2 Cautious Japanese firms offered annual wage hikes of more than 3% this year, a three-decade-high, given the need to lure workers amid high inflation and a labour crunch. \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's Ueda has said an end to easy policy would depend on the economy achieving 2% inflation coupled with pay growth.\par \par \uc2 (For other stories from the Reuters global long-term economic outlook polls package:) (Reporting by Satoshi Sugiyama; Additional reporting by Kantaro Komiya; Polling by Veronica Khongwir and Anant Chandak; Editing by Christian Schmollinger)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-18T04:05:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230615ej6f00gij\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
