\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Japan's top  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  diplomat: Ready to respond to yen moves 'appropriately'\b0\par\par\uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2023\par 13:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Yen near six-month low beyond 140 yen to dollar\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Kanda: won't rule out any option, when asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Government to keep in close contact with new \uc2 BOJ\uc2  leadership\par \par \uc2 (Recasts with Kanda, details, analyst comment)\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 30 (Reuters) - Japan will closely watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and won't rule out any options, its top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat said on Tuesday, after financial authorities met in the wake of the yen's slide to a six-month low against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should move stably, reflecting fundamentals, and excessive volatility is undesirable, Masato Kanda, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters.\par \par \uc2 Kanda was speaking after a meeting with counterparts from the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) and the country's financial watchdog.\par \par \uc2 "We will closely watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves and respond appropriately as needed," he said. "If necessary we won't rule out any options available," he said, when asked about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The three-party talks were last held in March and tend to take place when the yen swings sharply, either up or down.\par \par \uc2 Referring to new \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor Kazuo Ueda, Kanda said the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed their intention to keep in close contact in responding to various risks to markets.\par \par \uc2 "There are risk factors in financial markets such as the U.S. debt ceiling and its financial sector. The government and the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  shared the need to closely watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and financial market moves and their impact on Japan's economy," he said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts largely took the three-party talks in their stride.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe it's the start of some potential increasing concerns" about the yen, said \uc2 Derek Halpenny\uc2 , \uc2 MUFG\uc2 's head of research global markets EMEA.\par \par \uc2 "Obviously we've had the debt ceiling agreement, and may be looking at the markets seeing a reasonable chance the Fed (rate) hikes in June and it's feasible we get dollar/yen in mid 140s."\par \par \uc2 YEN WEAKNESS\par \par \uc2 The three-way talks were also held last year to warn against excessive weakening of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which drives up import costs and hits private consumption that makes up more than half the economy, the world's third largest.\par \par \uc2 The meeting last year served as a prelude to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first dollar-selling and yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 24 years, as the yen depreciated to a 32-year low near 152 to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Usually, a strong yen is a matter of concern for policymakers because it undermines the competitiveness of Japan's export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 Recently, yen weakness has been a greater concern. It has driven up import bills, sending Japan's trade balance into persistent deficit and undermining Japan's purchasing power.\par \par \uc2 The yen has been hovering close to a six-month low beyond 140 to the dollar since Friday.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the greenback dropped 0.03% to 140.4 yen, below this year's high of 140.91 hit on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Helping boost the dollar, longer-dated U.S. Treasuries rallied on Tuesday as bond traders welcomed a deal to suspend Washington's borrowing limit until January 2025 in exchange for caps on spending and cuts in government programmes. (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Additional reporting by Alun John; Editing by Jan Harvey, David Holmes and Mark Potter) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-5-30T12:25:58.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230530ej5u00r0f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
