 \par \uc2 \f2 3. Investment of General Account Assets for the Six Months Ended March 31, 2023\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Investment Status of Assets in Fiscal 2022 \u9312\'24\'60 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  environment In FY2022, While the recovery from the Novel COVID-19 crisis continued, the pace of recovery remained moderate due to the effects by soaring global energy and food prices and global economic slowdown. Overseas economies slowed significantly as central banks accelerated monetary tightening due to drastic global inflation. Towards the end of the fiscal year, financial instability expanded globally; it changes in the financial market environment, such as rapidly rising interest\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rates, caused bankruptcies and business instability in the banking sector in the United States and Europe. Amid these economic conditions, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  environment was as follows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on 10-year JGBs rose as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 BOJ\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) announced to target the fluctuation range of its yield curve control (YCC) from "about \u177\'00\'B10.25%" to "about \u177\'00\'B10.5%". On the other hand, since the beginning of the year, JGB yields declined as financial instability in the US and Europe spread, driven by significant monetary tightening by central banks abroad.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yield on ten-year government bonds: April 1, 2022  0.210%\par                                     March 31, 2023 0.320%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei 225 Stock Average held firm on the back of the economic recovery following the COVID-19 and expectations of improved performance by exporters due to the weak yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nikkei 225 Stock Average: April 1, 2022  27,821\par                           March 31, 2023 28,041\par TOPIX:                    April 1, 2022  1,946\par                           March 31, 2023 2,003\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the case of the JPY/USD exchange rate, the yen depreciated rapidly as the gap between Japanese and US interest rates widened against the backdrop of monetary tightening by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In the second half of the fiscal year, the yen remained strong as a result of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Government of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and changes in YCC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The JPY/EUR exchange rate rose after the summer as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (\f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) began to tighten monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPY /USD: April 1, 2022  \u165\'00\'A5122.39\par           March 31 2023  \u165\'00\'A5133.53\par JPY/EUR:  April 1, 2022  \u165\'00\'A5136.70\par           March 31, 2023 \u165\'00\'A5145.72\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u9313\'24\'61Investment Performance Overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With regard to asset management in FY2022, the Company maintained its investment strategy of mainly fixed income assets, such as government and corporate bonds, based on our risk-taking policy and medium-tolong-term asset management policy. In particular, from the point of view of financial soundness against fluctuations in financial markets and improving capital efficiency, it sold stocks and increased the amount of bonds matching its policy reserves, mainly super-long-term government bonds to reduce market risk such as interest rates and stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With regard to risk weighted assets such as foreign securities, the Company flexibly and quickly allocated funds taking into account market development. Furthermore, it promoted investments and loans in infrastructure, alternative assets and real assets to improve our portfolio profitability and diversify investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - 3 -\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dai-ichi Life Insurance Company, Limited\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is an excerpt of the original content. To continue reading it, access the original document \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.publicnow.com/CC77FCA09568D26BF476B6FB905D5B3D1D4BFB8B"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.release.tdnet.info/inbs/ek/140120230508559708.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 This content was originally posted here}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Disclaimer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dai-ichi Life Holdings Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . published this content on 15 May 2023 and is solely responsible for the information contained therein. Distributed by \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.publicnow.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Public}}}\uc2 \f2 , unedited and unaltered, on 15 May 2023 05:43:16 UTC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LCDVP00020230515ej5f0066e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
