estment Fund Managers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's Investment Adviser is Rising Sun Management Limited.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Apex Listed Companies Services (UK) Limited (formerly Sanne Fund Services UK Limited), the Company's appointed Administrator, (the "Administrator") provides administrative and company secretarial services to the Company under the terms of an administration agreement between the Company and the Administrator.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's registered office is: 6th Floor, 125 London Wall, London EC2Y 5AS. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    2.   BASIS OF PREPARATION AND SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES \par    a)   Basis of preparation \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Statement of compliance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The financial statements have been prepared in accordance with UK adopted international accounting standards. The financial statements have also been prepared as far as is relevant and applicable to the Company in accordance with the Statement of Recommended Practice ("SORP") issued in July 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Going Concern\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Directors have adopted the going concern basis in preparing the financial statements. The following is a summary of the Directors' assessment of the going concern status of the Company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's ability to continue as a going concern for the period assessed by the Directors, being at least 12 months from the date the financial statements were authorised for issue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This assessment took account of Russia's invasion into Ukraine and the recovery of the Covid-19. These uncertainties have created supply chain disruption and exacerbated inflationary pressures worldwide. The Directors do not foresee any immediate material risk to the Company's investment portfolio. A prolonged and deep market decline could lead to falling values of the underlying business or interruptions to cashflow, however the Company currently has more than sufficient liquidity available to meet any future obligations. An explanation of the market, liquidity and credit risks and how they are managed is contained in note 15 to the financial statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Directors have a reasonable expectation that the Company has adequate resources to continue in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  existence for at least twelve months from the date of this report. In reaching this conclusion, the Directors have considered the liquidity of the Company's portfolio of investments as well as its cash position, income and expense flows. The Company's net assets at 31 December 2022 were GBP158,745,000 (31 December 2021: GBP155,854,000). As at 31 December 2022, the Company held GBP31,738,000 (31 December 2021: GBP15,815,000) in cash. The total expenses for the year ended 31 December 2022 were GBP2,055,000 (31 December 2021: GBP1,792,000), which represented approximately 1.41% (31 December 2021: 1.37%) of average net assets during the year. At the date of approval of this document, based on the aggregate of investments and cash held, the Company has substantial operating expenses cover.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Use of estimates and judgements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The preparation of the financial statements and the manner in which they are presented requires management to make judgements, estimates and assumptions that affect the application of accounting policies and the reported amounts of assets, liabilities, income and expenses. Actual results may differ from these estimates. See below paragraph for judgement around determination of the functional and presentation \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Estimates and underlying assumptions are reviewed on an on-going basis. Revisions to accounting estimates are recognised in the year in which the estimates are revised and in any future periods affected. There have been no estimates, judgements or assumptions which have had a significant impact on the financial statements for the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Basis of measurement\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The financial statements have been prepared on the historical cost basis except for financial instruments at fair value through profit or loss, which are measured at fair value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Functional and presentation \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The financial statements are presented in sterling, which is the Company's functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Company's investments are denominated in Japanese yen. However, the Company's Shares are issued in sterling. In addition, a substantial majority of the Company's expenses are paid in sterling. It is also expected that the Company's dividend shall be declared and paid in sterling. All financial information presented in sterling has been rounded to the nearest thousand pounds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company is required to identify its functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , being the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the primary economic environment in which the Company operates. The Board, having regard to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the Company's share capital and the predominant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in which its shareholders operate, has determined that sterling is the functional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Sterling is also the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in which the financial statements are presented.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Standards, Interpretations and Amendments adopted from 1 January 2022\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nippon Active Value Fund PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Final Results for -9-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A number of new standards, amendments to standards are effective for the annual periods beginning after 1 January 2022. None of these has a significant effect on the measurement of the amounts recognised in the financial statements of the Company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Standards and Amendments issued but not yet Effective\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The relevant new and amended standards and interpretations that are issued, but not yet effective, up to the date of issuance of the Company's financial statements are disclosed below. These standards are not expected to have a material impact on the entity in future reporting periods and on foreseeable future transactions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amendments to IAS 1: Classification of Liabilities as Current or Non-current\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In January 2020, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IASB\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued amendments to paragraphs 69 to 76 of IAS 1 to specify the requirements for classifying liabilities as current or non-current. The amendments are effective for annual reporting periods beginning on or after 1 January 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Definition of Accounting Estimates - Amendments to IAS 8\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In February 2021, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IASB\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued amendments to IAS 8, in which it introduces a definition of 'accounting estimates'. The amendments are effective for annual reporting periods beginning on or after 1 January 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Disclosure of Accounting Policies - Amendments to IAS 1 and IFRS Practice Statement 2\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In February 2021, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IASB\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued amendments to IAS 1 and IFRS Practice Statement 2 Making Materiality Judgements. The amendments to IAS 1 are applicable for annual periods beginning on or after 1 January 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 b) Significant accounting policies\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following accounting policies have been applied consistently throughout the reporting year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investments\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Upon initial recognition investments are classified by the Company "at fair value through profit or loss". They are accounted for on the date they are traded and are included initially at fair value which is taken to be their cost. Subsequently quoted investments are valued at fair value, which is the bid market price, or if bid price is unavailable, last traded price on the relevant exchange. Subsequently investments are revalued at fair value, which is the bid market price for listed investments over the time until they are sold, any unrealised gains/losses are included in the fair value of the investments. Investments are derecognised on the trade date of their disposal, which is the point where the Company transfers substantially all the risks and rewards of the ownership of the financial asset.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Changes in the fair value of investments held at fair value through profit or loss and gains or losses on disposal are included in the capital column of the Statement of Comprehensive Income within "gains on investments".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taxation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment trusts which have approval under Section 1158 of the Corporation Tax Act 2010 are not liable for taxation on capital gains. The Company has been granted approval as an Investment Trust by HMRC.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Irrecoverable withholding tax is recognised on any overseas dividends on an accruals basis using the applicable rate for the country of origin.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Segmental reporting\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chief Operating Decision Maker, which is the Board, is of the opinion that the Company is engaged in a single segment of business. The financial information used by the Chief Operating Decision Maker to manage the Company presents the business as a single segment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dividends payable\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dividends to shareholders are recognised in the year of the ex-dividend date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Income\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Income includes investment income from financial assets at fair value through profit or loss and finance income. Investment income from financial assets at fair value through profit or loss is recognised in the Statement of Comprehensive Income within investment income when the Company's right to receive payments is established. Dividend income is presented gross of non-recoverable withholding taxes, which are disclosed separately in the Statement of Comprehensive Income.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dividend receivable arising from companies with the United Kingdom (UK) are classified as UK dividend income and all other income is classified as overseas dividend income.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Special dividends are assessed on their individual merits and may be credited to the Statement of Comprehensive Income as a capital item if considered to be closely linked to reconstructions of the investee company or other capital transactions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other income comprises interest earned on cash held on deposit. Other income is recognised on a receipt basis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expenses\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All expenses are accounted for on an accrual basis. In respect of the analysis between revenue and capital items presented within the Statement of Comprehensive Income, the Investment Adviser's fees are split 20% to revenue and 80% to capital. All other expenses are recognised as revenue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Transactions denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are translated into sterling at the exchange rates as at the date of the transaction. Monetary assets and liabilities denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the period end are reported at the rates of exchange prevailing at the period end. Any gain or loss arising from a change in exchange rates subsequent to the date of the transaction is included as an exchange gain or loss to capital or revenue in the Income Statement as appropriate. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements on investments are included in the Income Statement within gains on investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash and cash equivalents\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash and cash equivalents include deposits held at call with banks and other short-term deposits with original maturities of three months or less.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade and other payables\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade and other payables are initially recognised at fair value, and subsequently re-measured at amortised cost using the effective interest method where necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nature and purpose of equity and reserves:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Share capital and share premium\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Share capital represents the 1p nominal value of the issued share capital. Ordinary shares are classified as equity. Costs directly attributable to the issue of new shares (that would have been avoided if there had not been a new issue of new shares) are recognised against the value of the ordinary share premium.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The share premium account arose from the net proceeds of new shares and from the excess proceeds received on the sale of shares from treasury over the repurchase cost.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital reserve\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Profits and losses achieved by selling investments, changes in fair value arising upon the revaluation of investments that remain in the portfolio and other capital expenditure are all charged to the capital column of the Statement of Comprehensive Income and allocated to the capital reserve.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The capital reserve reflects any: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --    gains or losses on the disposal of investments; \par    --    exchange movements of a capital nature; \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- the increases and decreases in the fair value of investments which have been recognised in the capital column of the \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --    income statement; and \par    --    expenses which are capital in nature. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any gains in the fair value of investments that are not readily convertible to cash are treated as unrealised gains in the capital reserve.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Revenue reserve\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The revenue reserve reflects all income and expenditure recognised in the revenue column of the income statement and is distributable by way of dividends.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's distributable reserve consists of the capital reserve attributable to realised profit and the revenue reserve. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 3. INVESTMENTS \par  \par a) Investment at fair value through profit \par  or loss \par ------------------------------------------ \par  \par  \par                                               As at 31 December    As at 31 December \par                                                            2022                 2021 \par                                                        GBP' 000              GBP'000 \par ------------------------------------------  -------------------  ------------------- \par  Listed on a recognised overseas exchange               126,284              138,626 \par ------------------------------------------  -------------------  ------------------- \par  Total                                                  126,284              138,626 \par ------------------------------------------  -------------------  ------------------- \par  \par    b)      Movements during year \par  \par                                                      Year ended      Year ended \par                                                     31 December     31 December \par                                                            2022            2021 \par -------------