\par\par \b \uc2 CC Japan Income & Growth Trust PLC\uc2  Final Results\b0\par\par\uc2 17,600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2023\par 08:00\par \uc2 Regulatory News Service\par \uc2 RNS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2023 \par \par \uc2 TIDMCCJI\par \par \uc2 RNS Number : 6153N\par \par \uc2 CC Japan Income & Growth Trust PLC\par \par \uc2 24 January 2023\par \par \uc2 CC JAPAN INCOME & GROWTH TRUST PLC\par \par \uc2 LEI: 549300FZANMYIORK1K98\par \par \uc2 ANNUAL FINANCIAL REPORT ANNOUNCEMENT\par \par \uc2 INVESTMENT OBJECTIVE\par \par \uc2 The investment objective of the \uc2 CC Japan Income & Growth Trust Plc\uc2  (the "Company") is to provide Shareholders with dividend income combined with capital growth, mainly through investment in equities listed or quoted in Japan. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  FINANCIAL INFORMATION \par                                                              As at           As at \par                                                         31 October      31 October \par                                                               2022            2021 \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Net assets (millions)                                    GBP203.6        GBP222.9 \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Net asset value ("NAV") per Ordinary Share \par   ("Share")(1)                                              151.1p          165.4p \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Share price                                                138.8p          154.0p \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Share price discount to NAV(2)                               8.1%            6.9% \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Transferable Subscription Share price                       0.53p           3.50p \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Ongoing charges(2)                                          1.06%           1.05% \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Gearing (net)(2)                                            20.9%           21.1% \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  (1) Measured on a cum income basis. \par  (2) This is an Alternative Performance Measure ('APM'). Definitions \par   of APMs used in this report, together with how these measures have been \par   calculated are disclosed in the Annual Report. \par  \par  PERFORMANCE SUMMARY \par                                                       For the year    For the year \par                                                                 to              to \par                                                         31 October      31 October \par                                                               2022            2021 \par                                                        % change(1)     % change(1) \par  NAV ex-income total return per Share(2)                     -6.3%          +25.1% \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  NAV cum-income total return per Share(2)                    -5.9%          +24.3% \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Share price total return(2)                                 -7.1%          +32.7% \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Tokyo Stock Exchange Price Index ("TOPIX") \par   total return                                               -9.5%          +11.9% \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Revenue return per Share (Undiluted)                        5.14p           4.75p \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Dividends per Share: \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  First Interim dividend                                      1.40p           1.40p \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Second interim dividend                                     3.50p           3.35p \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  Total dividends per Share for the year                      4.90p           4.75p \par -------------------------------------------------  ---------------  -------------- \par  \par  (1) Total returns are stated in GBP sterling, including dividend reinvested. \par   (2) These are APMs. \par  Source: Coupland Cardiff Asset Management LLP - The Company's Factsheet \par   October 2022 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PERFORMANCE SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                            Year       Year       Year       Year       Year       Year \par                            Launch to         to         to         to         to         to         to \par                            Oct 2016*   Oct 2017   Oct 2018   Oct 2019   Oct 2020   Oct 2021   Oct 2022 \par                           ----------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  --------- \par  Share price                 122.40p    152.00p    153.00p    150.00p    119.50p    154.00p    138.75p \par                           ----------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  --------- \par  Share price total \par   return                      +23.5%     +27.2%      +2.8%      +0.7%     -17.3%     +32.7%       -7.1 \par                           ----------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  --------- \par  NAV per Share               123.90p    146.00p    148.60p    158.90p    136.80p    165.40p    151.09p \par                           ----------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  --------- \par  NAV (cum-income) \par   total return per \par   Share                       +24.9%     +20.7%      +4.1%      +9.9%     -11.1%     +24.3%      -5.9% \par                           ----------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  --------- \par  TOPIX Index total \par   return in GBP \par   sterling                    +32.7%     +10.1%      -0.4%      +7.2%      +0.3%     +11.9%      -9.5% \par                           ----------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  --------- \par  Revenue return \par   per Share (Undiluted)        3.60p      4.06p      4.55p      5.26p      5.04p      4.75p      5.14p \par                           ----------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  --------- \par  Dividends per \par   Share                        3.00p      3.45p      3.75p      4.50p      4.60p      4.75p    4.90p** \par                           ----------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  ---------  --------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Period from the Company's launch on 15 December 2015 to 31 October 2016.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ** Includes second interim dividend of 3.50 p for the year ended 31 October 2022.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHAIRMAN'S STATEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Performance\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the year to 31 October 2022, in sterling terms, the Net Asset Value of the Company ("NAV") declined by\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5.9% as measured by total return including income. The Ordinary Share price, again measured by total return\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 fell by 7.1%. This is disappointing, particularly after the strong set of results in the year to 31 October 2021,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 where NAV increased by 24.3% and the share price rose 32.7%. However, I can report that in the last year, we\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 outperformed the TOPIX Total Return Index which fell 9.5% in sterling terms during the period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our long-term track record and relative performance against the AIC Japan investment trust peer group\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 remains robust and underscores the validity of our investment mandate which seeks dividend income\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 combined with capital growth. Since launch in December 2015 until the recent financial year end, the Company's\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NAV total return, including dividend distributions, recorded an 82.8% increase, considerably outperforming\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the sterling adjusted TOPIX total return index, which rose 58.0%. Over the same period, the share price has\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 risen 65.7% again measured by total return in sterling, including an aggregate distribution of 25.45p per\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ordinary Share of dividends paid to Shareholders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It has been another difficult year given the challenges presented by Russia's invasion of Ukraine, while\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan continued to be impacted by successive waves of Covid-19 infections and a cautious policy towards\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 reopening the economy and its borders. As the world's Central Banks led by the US Federal Reserve have\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 increased interest rates and pursued a more hawkish monetary policy to combat inflation, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ("BOJ") has been an exception in continuing to provide liquidity to the markets and remains wedded\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to Yield Curve Control. This drove the Japanese Yen down at one time trading below Y150 to the US$. Our\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment Manager, Richard Aston, has handled the market volatility with great discipline by retaining focus\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 within the scope of the investment mandate which seeks total return. Richard has looked through market\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 volatility retaining confidence in portfolio holdings with solid prospects while continuing to find companies\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 which have growth trajectories, improving cash flow and dividends; some of which have already benefitted\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 from the prospect of a full reopening of the domestic Japanese economy. Over a 12-month and 5-year view,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 our investment performance is a leader in the AIC Japanese investment trust peer group as Richard's\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 confidence in the intrinsic value of portfolio holdings has been rewarded. However, our premium share price\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rating has not recovered with the NAV closing the year at an 8.1% discount compared to a 6.9% discount at the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 previous year end. At the time of writing the discount stands at 8.3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growing the Company\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Ordinary Share price discount means that until we can restore our premium to NAV, we are not able to issue any new shares.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Furthermore, world events and their impact on markets have conspired against any real rerating of the Ordinary\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares which might have allowed for the successful exercise of the Transferable Subscription Shares ("TSS")\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 issued as a 1 for 5 free bonus to Ordinary Shareholders in February 2021. Unless we see a Lester Piggott\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 type late run in NAV performance, the TSS will expire worthless on the last business day of February 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is also the last quarterly exercise date. At the time of writing, the NAV per Ordinary Share has nudged\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CC Japan Income & Growth Trust PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Final Results -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 above the Subscription Price set at GBP1.61, hence "in the money" whereas the Ordinary Share trails below\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 that price, still "out of the money". So, there is a small possibility of successful exercise of these entitlements at\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the last opportunity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The TSS scheme was designed by the Board potentially to raise a further GBP40 million as a "Covid-19 recovery"\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 warrant which if successfully executed would not only have increased our size and spread costs but\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 also offered significant leverage into the Ordinary Shares so that TSS holders could make some money\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 as recompense from the depressed valuations during Covid-19. Although the TSS entitlements were allocated\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 as a free bonus and the scheme cost less than 0.34p per Ordinary Share at the time of issue, I would like\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to apologise for the administrative inconvenience this has caused not least in TSS holders receiving\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 quarterly exercise notices, (a regulatory requirement of the custodian, but not the Company) when it was uneconomic\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to do so. The Scheme had the best of intentions and if validated by better markets would have\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 been a good way to grow the Company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board has invested in various marketing initiatives to raise the profile of the Company by way\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of webinars hosted by third parties and continue to update our website, which you can visit\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 at:- \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ccjapanincomeandgrowthtrust.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://ccjapanincomeandgrowthtrust.com/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Besides regular meetings with wealth managers and major Shareholders, we continue to focus on improving\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 content together with web and media distribution to raise awareness of our differentiated mandate which\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 represents a great opportunity for investors to capture total return from Japan with appreciable and stable\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 growth in portfolio income streams.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Income and Dividends\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is pleasing to see Japanese companies continuing to pursue enlightened distribution policies driven not\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 only through governance reforms but also by cash rich Japanese corporate balance sheets. Furthermore,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 despite difficult trading conditions, Japanese management teams have tended to commit to their\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dividend projections, and in some cases surprised on the upside by exceeding expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the year to 31 October 2022, the revenue return per Ordinary Share increased by 8.2% to 5.14p per Ordinary\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Share. The result demonstrates the underlying trend of Japanese dividend growth despite the vicissitudes\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility where the Yen to Sterling cross rate has been under pressure. The yen has fluctuated\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 between Y149 = GBP1 to Y172 = GBP1 during the year with an average exchange rate of Y159. Our clearly stated\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 policy is not to hedge our yen exposure. We translate dividend income into sterling on receipt. Thus, revenue\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 is potentially at risk from a strengthening of sterling although conversely, a weaker yen tends to stimulate\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese corporate earnings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board has declared a second interim dividend of 3.50p per Ordinary Share (an increase of 4.5%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 over last year's second interim dividend) making a full year distribution of 4.90p per Ordinary Share and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 representing a 3.2% improvement over last year. This will be paid on 3 March 2023 to those shareholders\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 on the register as at 3 February 2023 with an ex-date of 2 February 2023. We are maintaining our policy of\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 paying a second interim dividend in substitution for a final dividend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Board is committed to growing the dividend, it considers it prudent to continue to build the revenue\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 reserve in uncertain times, which will stand at 2.20p per Ordinary Share after the payment of the second interim\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dividend. Shareholders should also be reminded that the Company has a Special reserve of GBP64.6 million\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 available for distribution in circumstances where there is an unforeseen revenue shortfall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is the seventh year of dividend increase for the Company with the annual dividend increasing by 63%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 since launch in December 2015. We currently pay a 3% dividend yield from Japanese equities out of\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 covered income. Investors looking for equity income can continue to look to Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Board Succession & Composition\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board has a succession plan in place whereby, subject to re-election by Shareholders at this year's\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Annual General Meeting ("AGM"), I intend to step down as Chairman at the AGM in March 2024 having\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 served since launch in 2015. It is also the intention that Mr. Peter Wolton, Senior Independent Director, also\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 one of the original members of the Board at launch, will retire before the end of 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Search Consultant will be appointed during 2023 to identify a new candidate for the Board with complementary\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 skills. I should point out that the Board composition meets the recommendations of the AIC code and Hampton\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alexander review as far as gender and ethnic diversity representation is concerned. Our diversity policy can be found\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in the Annual Report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Change of Auditor\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ernst & Young LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("EY") have served as the Company's Auditors since launch but in line with the requirement to\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 conduct a tender at least every 10 years and in view of the projected increase in audit fees, the Audit and Risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Committee held a tender process to look for a more competitive solution. Consequently, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Johnston Carmichael\f28 \uc2 \f2  will be\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 appointed as the Company's new Auditors, subject to Shareholder approval at the forthcoming AGM. The Board is\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 confident that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Johnston Carmichael\f28 \uc2 \f2  will provide a high level of service at a reduced cost. We would like to thank EY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 for their service as Auditor of the Company for the past seven years. Further details can be found in the Report of the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Audit and Risk Committee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Annual General Meeting ("AGM")\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In line with the requirements of the Companies Act 2006, the Company will hold an AGM of Shareholders\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to consider the resolutions laid out in the Notice of Meeting. The Board encourages Shareholders to\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 attend and participate in the Company's forthcoming AGM on 1 March 2023 at the offices of\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stephenson Harwood LLP\f28 \uc2 \f2 , 1 Finsbury Circus, London EC2M 7SH. Our Investment Manager, Richard Aston, will\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 provide an update on the portfolio and take questions after the formal business of the meeting. The Board will\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 also be available to meet shareholders and discuss the Company. I do hope that you will join us.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We recognise it is not possible for everyone to attend the AGM and I would remind Shareholders that any\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 questions relating to the business of the AGM, can be sent by email to ukfundcosec@sannegroup.com . To the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 extent that it is appropriate to do so, the Company will respond to any questions received in a Q&A which will\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 be posted on the Company's website.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Shareholders are unable to attend the meeting in person, they are strongly encouraged to vote by proxy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 and to appoint the "Chairman of the AGM" as their proxy. Details of how to vote, either electronically, by\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 proxy form or through CREST, can be found in the Notes to the Notice of AGM. The lodging of\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 a form of proxy (or an appointment of a proxy through CREST) will not however prevent a shareholder from\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 attending the AGM and voting in person if they so wish.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outlook\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The long period of disinflation and free money is over. Collective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy led by the US Federal Reserve is firmly aimed at reducing inflation at a time when the pricing power of labour in the USA and Europe is in the ascendant. As interest rates rise on the back of a collapse in monetary aggregates in the USA, it looks as if the risks of global recession are rising. With the high levels of debt embedded in the world economy, tightening policy beyond a certain point could also potentially expose credit problems. Global market sentiment will, as ever, be driven by the perception of policy direction emanating from the US Federal Reserve.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan too, is facing similar inflationary pressures, although these are largely due to the war and hence\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 exogenous. As elsewhere, the labour market is tight, reflecting an ageing demographic and shrinking\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 workforce. Both the BOJ and Government are keen to encourage wage growth which has been static for\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 so long. Indeed, Prime Minister Kishida has urged employers to increase wages by more than 3% as part\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of his "new economic capitalism" plans with Consumer Price Inflation (CPI) running at its highest for 30 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a long period of deflation, some inflation will be good for Japan. Any increase in wages should benefit\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 domestic consumption.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Just before Christmas, the BOJ announced a relaxation of the bands for controlling the yield on the 10-year bond.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is seen as a first step to normalising monetary policy and has sparked a sharp rally in the yen. However,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the BOJ has had to absorb massive liquidation of Japanese Government Bonds (JGBs) by stepping in to\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 buy bonds to defend the new yield level. Hence, rather than tightening monetary policy, the BOJ are now forced\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to expand their balance sheet and appear chained to loose monetary policy. We can expect interest rates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 and the Yield Curve Control Band to rise further in the months ahead and the market will be looking closely for\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 pointers as to further policy direction, particularly with the appointment of a new BOJ Governor in April 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A weak yen has deterred foreign buying of Japanese securities, and a rebound in its value could help\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 stimulate foreign interest which has always provided a catalyst for buying the stock market. Equally, it should\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 be noted that TOPIX index companies now produce an income dividend yield higher than inflation, which could\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 tempt domestic savers earning no interest on deposits. Moreover, the Government has announced measures\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to try and mobilise savings by offering incentives for people to invest.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although Japan's domestic economy is steadily reopening for business, it is frustrating to see that yet\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 another Covid-19 wave in China may further delay a recovery in tourist visitor arrivals. Nevertheless, the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 overall economic outlook appears favourable. The wild card remains geopolitics and the stability of the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CC Japan Income & Growth Trust PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Final Results -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GDP forecasts show higher expectations for Japanese growth compared to other developed economies\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 while there is growing confidence in better corporate earnings guidance. Continuing commitment to corporate governance reforms should also underpin rising dividend distributions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Our focus on income will continue to be an important part of our total return which clearly differentiates our mandate from most other funds investing in Japan. Richard Aston and his colleagues are finding no shortage of investment opportunities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Harry Wells\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chairman\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 23 January 2023\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 INVESTMENT MANAGER'S REPORT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Performance Review\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The investment landscape has become increasingly complex over the last twelve months with a number\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of developments challenging the relatively favourable conditions for investors in a variety of assets in recent\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 years. This has resulted, and simplified by many, as an outperformance of 'value' over 'growth' across equity\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 markets with Japan being no exception. The rapid emergence of inflationary trends around the world,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 at levels not seen for decades, and the subsequent attempts by Central Banks worldwide to regain\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 control by raising interest rates has been a significant contributory factor to this change of market direction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Additionally, political tensions have added to the elevated levels of risk. Sentiment has been severely\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 impacted by the ongoing war in the Ukraine. The ramifications have played out in a number of ways\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 with Russia's increasing international isolation, rising energy prices and credit concerns amongst the factors\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 most relevant to Japanese companies. The direct trade between Japan and Russia is limited to less than 1% of\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's international total and for most companies any direct country exposure is a very small component of\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 their overall revenues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Furthermore, as one of the main drivers for global growth in recent years, the performance of the Chinese\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 economy has had an important bearing on international trends. A combination of political discord with the USA,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 concerns about the real estate market and a tough stance on managing Covid-19 outbreak has resulted\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in a dramatic slowdown in the country's immediate prospects and international trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent years, global 'risk on' investor sentiment has been accompanied by a strengthening of the Yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fundamental factors that may have contributed to this notably different trend are 1) the impact of higher\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 oil prices of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market (given that Japan is an importer of energy raw materials) and 2)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 confirmation verbally and via market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  intends to continue with an\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 accommodative monetary policy for the time being despite central banks in other regions indicating a\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 withdrawal of components of their respective easy monetary programmes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite these many uncertainties and challenges to the perceived orthodoxy of recent years, portfolio\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 attribution has been positive and continued its post pandemic recovery. It has been especially pleasing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to see the strong share price performance of longstanding holdings in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 ,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokio Marine Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (financials sector), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Itochu Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi Corp\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (wholesale sector), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Telegraph & Telephone\f28 \uc2 \f2  (telecommunications sector) and more recently Nippon\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Parking Development (service sector). These are all companies that faced considerable and unexpected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  challenges during the pandemic but have remained committed to their shareholder return policies\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 throughout with increases in annual dividends and opportunistic share buyback programmes underpinning\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the confidence in their business future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is this ability to compound returns through a business cycle which we believe to be the most relevant and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 differentiating factor of Japanese equities for foreign investors. It is extremely encouraging to see these\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 attributes ultimately rewarded for those willing to be patient.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The weakest performers for the year include a number of companies whose contribution had been notably positive in the first half and reflects a significant downturn in expectations for technology companies in particular as fears of a global recession have intensified. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shin-Etsu Chemical\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Murata and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electron\f28 \uc2 \f2  have all exemplified the characteristic we identify as important for long term performance and as recently as their FY21 results raised dividends to a level higher than originally expected. These companies are all industry leaders with strong balance sheets and clear shareholder return policies which we believe justifies taking a long-term investment perspective and weathering any short-term adjustments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We are somewhat disappointed in the recent share price performance of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carta Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the online advertiser,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 which had been a strong performer since initial inclusion in the portfolio. The company has embarked\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 on an investment phase which has been detrimental to earnings in the near term on the basis of higher\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 expectations for newly identified projects. There is no immediate risk to the shareholder return objective which\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 is based on a stable distribution reflecting the strength of the company's balance sheet. However, we will be\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 mindful that this investment must enhance the value of the Company over the medium term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Portfolio Positioning\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We believe that the current economic realities increase the importance of bottom-up analysis in investment decision-making as many companies now face a very different operating environment from anything experienced in recent years. Rising raw material prices and higher energy costs, tighter monetary conditions, stricter environmental regulation, digitalisation and changing customer behaviour are amongst the prevailing trends that create varying challenges and opportunities for individual companies. We remain steadfast in the belief of the investment principles that underpin the strategy as established when the Company was launched. Namely, consistent returns via dividends and share buybacks are a key component of total shareholder return that investors in Japan should now consider. We are consequently encouraged by the increased recognition for companies able to compound these returns over time. Furthermore, we believe that through initiatives such as the Japanese Corporate Governance Code and consistent shareholder engagement, there is much wider acknowledgement of its importance amongst company management. As a consequence, the number of companies seeking to offer these attractive characteristics continues to increase and the portfolio has continued to evolve throughout the year seeking to take advantage of this expanding investment universe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New holdings have been established in Zozo, Intage and Benefit One, smaller domestically focused companies\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 with leading market positions which operate in areas we believe offer good growth potential. Importantly\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 this is accompanied by a greater recognition by management of the role of stable shareholders, which\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 has led, in our view, to better corporate governance and friendlier shareholder return policies. Zozo operates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's largest online fashion websites and is benefiting from the low market penetration of e-commerce in\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan. The company has suffered in the past from the erratic corporate governance of its founder, but his\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 departure has seen a more consistent approach to both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and shareholder management consistent\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 with our own expectations. Intage provides marketing research services for a diverse range of clients and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 sees future opportunities in data analytics and international expansion. Coincidently, it is seeking to\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 improve its shareholder relations and capital efficiency through an uplift in annual dividend distribution and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 share buybacks. Benefit One provides a number of outsourced services such as childcare, healthcare and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 leisure activities to a range of corporate clients. The company targets a high level of annual distribution to its\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 shareholders based on the strength of its cashflow and balance sheet strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the first time in a number of years, we participated in an Initial Public Offering. Socionext was founded in\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2015 as a result of a merger between the SoC (Systemon-Chip) divisions of Fujitsu and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Panasonic\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and an\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 investment received from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Development Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 . It is a fabless SoC solution provider, meaning\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 they design their own chips but contract out their production, covering both software development as well\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 as semiconductor logic/physical design. In stark contrast to many companies listed in recent years, the company\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 is debt free, has valuations that were attractive relative to its growth prospects besides a clear intention to pay\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dividends to its shareholders from the start.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rising interest rate environment and falling valuations elsewhere in the market have lowered the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 attraction of Real Estate Investment Trusts and the overall weighting in these specialist investment vehicles\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 has fallen, with the disposal of holdings in Japan GLP REIT and Star Asia REIT. We also exited holdings in Japan\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exchange Group (operator of the Tokyo and Osaka Stock Exchanges), Exeo (telecommunications infrastructure) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hikari Tsushin\f28 \uc2 \f2  (small company business services) as greater risks have emerged to their business prospects and ultimately their ability to maintain a shareholder return profile consistent with previous expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outlook\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The global economic backdrop appears increasingly challenging with the recent debate over the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 sustainability of inflationary trends and prospect of a new era of rising interest rates shifting notably to rapidly\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 falling expectations for economic growth and the risk of global recession. Japan, on a relative basis, is set to offer\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 many interesting opportunities given that its economic cycle is no longer in sync with other major economies,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 primarily as a consequence of its delayed domestic recovery following the Covid-19 pandemic. There are\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CC Japan Income & Growth Trust PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Final Results -4-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 reasons to be optimistic about the expected economic rebound in Japan in 2023 given the experience of other\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 economies as consumer behaviour returned to normal. The recent Yen weakness that has accompanied these\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 developments only adds to the attraction of Japanese equities over the medium term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The investment strategy employed by the Company is not dependent on any economic outcome nor correlated\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 with a particular investment style - it is one that seeks to capture a total and compounding shareholder return\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 over time regardless of short-term economic variations. This seems a particularly relevant consideration for\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 investors in these more uncertain times and we believe that Japanese equites now offer a differentiated and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 compelling opportunity in this regard. A combination of strong balance sheets, healthy dividend cover and,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 most importantly, changing attitudes have created a new investment landscape. The benefit has been\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 demonstrated clearly over the past three years with companies in Japan offering robust levels of distribution\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to shareholders despite the sluggish trends in the domestic and global economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a result, dividends and share buybacks are set to establish new records during the current fiscal year, with\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 minimal strain on balance sheets and dividend cover ratios. A further revision to the Japanese Corporate\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Governance Code in June 2022 and the restructuring of the main Tokyo Stock Exchange indices in April\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2022 were consistent with the previously established objectives of improving capital efficiency and corporate\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 governance which have underpinned the favourable progression of shareholder returns. We believe that the\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 benefits of Prime Minister Kishida's goal of doubling asset-based income places further pressures on\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 companies to continue the trajectory of improvement and that shareholders in Japanese companies will\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 consequently continue to reap the rewards.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Richard Aston\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coupland Cardiff Asset Management LLP\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 23 January 2023\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 INVESTMENT POLICY, RESULTS AND OTHER INFORMATION\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investment policy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company intends to invest in equities listed or quoted in Japan. The Company may also invest in exchange traded funds in order to gain exposure to such equities. Investment in exchange traded funds shall be limited to not more than 20 per cent. of Gross Assets at the time of investment. The Company may also invest in listed Japanese real estate investment trusts ("J-REITs").\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company may enter into long only contracts for difference or equity swaps for gearing and efficient portfolio management purposes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 No single holding (including any derivative instrument) will represent more than 10 per cent. of Gross Assets at the time of investment and, when fully invested, the portfolio is expected to have between 30 to 40 holdings, although there is no guarantee that this will be the case and it may contain a lesser or greater number of holdings at any time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company will have the flexibility to invest up to 10 per cent. of its Gross Assets at the time of investment in unquoted or untraded companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company will not be constrained by any index benchmark in its asset allocation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Borrowing policy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company may use borrowings for settlement of transactions, to meet on-going expenses and may be geared through borrowings and/or by entering into long only contracts for difference or equity swaps that have the effect of gearing the Company's portfolio to seek\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to enhance performance. The aggregate of borrowings and long only contracts for difference and equity swap exposure will not exceed 25 per cent. of Net Asset Value at the time of drawdown of the relevant borrowings or entering into the relevant transaction, as appropriate, although the Company's normal policy will be to utilise and maintain gearing to a lower limit of 20 per cent. of Net Asset Value at the time of drawdown of the relevant borrowings or entering into the relevant transaction, as appropriate. It is expected that any borrowings entered into will principally be denominated in Yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hedging policy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company does not currently intend to enter into any arrangements to hedge its underlying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exposure to investment denominated in Yen, although the Investment Manager and the Board may review this from time to time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Results and dividend\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's revenue return after tax for the financial year amounted to GBP6,930,000 (2021: GBP6,404,000). In August 2022, the Company paid an interim dividend of 1.40p (2021: 1.40p) per Ordinary Share. On 20 January 2023, the Directors declared a second interim dividend for the year ended 31 October 2022 of 3.50p (2021: 3.35p) per Ordinary Share, which will be paid on 3 March 2023 to Shareholders on the register at 3 February 2023. Therefore, the total dividend in respect of the financial year to 31 October 2022 will be 4.90p (2021: 4.75p) per Ordinary Share.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company made a capital loss after tax of GBP19,818,000 (2021: capital gain of GBP38,673,000). The total return, including income, after tax for the year was a loss of GBP12,888,000 (2021: gain of GBP45,077,000).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's Purpose, Values and Culture\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The primary focus of the Company is to generate total returns for Shareholders by investing in equities quoted on the recognised exchanges in Japan. The Investment Manager identifies companies which are undervalued, have strong balance sheets, strong business franchises, and favourable attitudes to shareholder returns in the form of sustainable and growing dividends and share buyback policies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company aims to meet the needs of investors through the Investment Manager's dual mandate of generating income and capital growth. The Company has been investing in Japanese equities since launch in 2015. Whilst the Company does not have a benchmark, the Board measures performance against the TOPIX Total Return Index and High Yield Indices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To achieve this, the Board of Directors has engaged Coupland Cardiff Asset Management LLP, who have the appropriate capability, resources and controls in place to actively manage the Company's assets in order to meet its investment objective. The Investment Manager has a well-defined investment strategy and process which is regularly and rigorously monitored and reviewed by the Board. As the Company has no employees and acts through its service providers, its culture is represented by the values and behaviour of the Board and third parties to which it delegates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To ensure that the Company's purpose, values, strategy and culture are aligned, the Board comprises independent non-executive Directors from a diverse background, who together bring a wide range of knowledge, skills and experience. The Board members contribute to a transparent culture ensuring effective oversight, critical support and challenge to the Investment Manager, and all other third-party suppliers. For more information, please refer to the Company's section 172 statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key performance indicators ("KPIs")\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board measures the Company's success in attaining its investment objective by reference to the following KPIs: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (i)            Long-term capital growth \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board considers the Company's Net Asset Value ("NAV") total return figures to be the best indicator of performance over time and this therefore is the main indicator of performance used by the Board. The NAV cum-income total return for the year to 31 October 2022 fell by 5.9% (2021: +24.3%) but the NAV total return from the Company's inception in December 2015 to 31 October 2022 increased by 18.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chairman's Statement incorporates a review of the highlights during the year. The Investment Manager's Report gives details on investments made during the year and how performance has been achieved. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (ii)           Revenue return per Share and dividends \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 he Company's revenue return per Ordinary Share, based on the weighted average number of shares in issue during the year, was 5.14p (2021: 4.75p). The Company's proposed total dividend payable in respect of the year ended 31 October 2022, including an interim dividend of 1.40p per Ordinary Share paid on 5 August 2022 and a second interim dividend of 3.50p payable on 3 March 2023 is 4.90p (2021: 4.75p) per Ordinary Share. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (iii)          Discount/premium to NAV \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The discount/premium relative to the NAV per share represented by the share price is closely monitored by the Board. The share price closed at a 8.1% discount to the NAV as at 31 October 2022 (2021: 6.9% discount). This is addressed in the Chairman's Statement. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (iv)         Control of the level of ongoing charges \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board monitors the Company's operating costs carefully. Growing the size of the Company offers many benefits, as not all of the Company's operating costs increase in line with the Company's assets under management. Based on the Company's average net assets for the year ended 31 October 2022, the Company's ongoing charges figure calculated in accordance with the AIC methodology was 1.06% (2021: 1.05%).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other information\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Modern slavery disclosure\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company aims to act to the highest standards and is committed to integrating responsible business practices throughout its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The prevention of modern slavery is an important part of good corporate governance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As an investment trust, the Company does not offer goods or services to consumers and deals predominantly with professional advisers and service providers in the financial services industry. As such the Board considers that the Company is out of scope of the Modern Slavery Act 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greenhouse Gas Emissions and Streamlined Energy and Carbon Reporting ("SECR")\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CC Japan Income & Growth Trust PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Final Results -5-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company has no employees, physical assets, property or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of its own, does not provide goods or services and does not have its own customers. It follows that the Company has little to no direct environmental impact. In consequence, the Company has limited greenhouse gas emissions to report from its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , nor does it have responsibility for any other sources of emissions under the Companies Act 2006 (Strategic Report and Directors' Reports) Regulations 2013. As the Company has no material \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and therefore has little energy use, it falls below the threshold to produce an energy and carbon report. The Company's ESG policy is contained in the Annual Report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Employees\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company has no employees. As at 31 October 2022, the Company had five Directors, comprising three males (60%) and two females (40%). On 1 February 2022, June Aitken and Craig Cleland joined the Board, bringing with them a wealth of experience and skills. As part of the recruitment process, the Board was mindful of the Company's policy on diversity which is contained in the Corporate Governance statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anti-bribery, Corruption and Tax Evasion\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is the Company's policy to conduct all of its business in an honest and ethical manner. The Company takes a zero-tolerance approach to bribery, corruption and tax evasion and is committed to acting professionally, fairly and with integrity in all its business dealings and relationships wherever it operates. Taking account of the nature of the Company's business and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Board has adopted policies and procedures that allow it to have reasonable assurance that persons associated with the Company are prevented from engaging in bribery, corruption or tax evasion; and has adopted the same standard of zero tolerance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Viability Statement\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Directors have assessed the viability of the Company for the period until 31 October 2027 (the "'Period") taking into account the long-term nature of the Company's investment strategy and the principal risks and emerging risks. The Board has chosen a five-year period to assess the Company's viability because of the expected long-term nature of equity investment, the Investment Manager's holding period and the fact that the investment objective is unlikely to change significantly over this period. Based on this assessment, the Directors have a reasonable expectation that the Company will be able to continue in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and meet its liabilities as they fall due over the Period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In their assessment of the prospects of the Company, the Directors have considered each of the principal and emerging risks and uncertainties and the liquidity and solvency of the Company. The Directors have considered the Company's income and expenditure projections and the fact that the Company's investments comprise readily realisable securities which could, if necessary, be sold to meet the Company's funding requirements. Portfolio activity and market developments are discussed at quarterly Board meetings. The internal control framework of the Company is subject to a formal review on at least an annual basis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Directors do not expect there to be any material increase in the annual ongoing charges of the Company over the Period. The Company's income from investments and cash realisable from the sale of its investments provide substantial cover to the Company's operating expenses, and any other costs likely to be faced by the Company over the period of their assessment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chairman's Statement and Investment Manager's Report present a positive long-term investment case for Japanese equities, which also underpins the Company's viability for the Period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The continuation of the Company is subject to approval by Shareholders every three years with the next continuation vote due to be held at the AGM in 2025.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This assessment takes into account the impact that higher levels of global inflation are having on portfolio companies and the investment environment as discussed in the Chairman's Statement, the Investment Manager's Report and in the Principal and Emerging Risks section.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outlook\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The outlook for the Company is discussed in the Chairman's Statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strategic Report\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Strategic Report set out in the Annual Report was approved by the Board of Directors on 23 January 2023.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK AND RISK MANAGEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Principal and emerging risks and uncertainties\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board is responsible for the management of risks faced by the Company and delegates this role to the Audit and Risk Committee (the "Committee").\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Committee carries out, at least annually, a robust assessment of principal and emerging risks and uncertainties and monitors the risks on an ongoing basis. The Committee has a dynamic risk management register in place to help identify key risks in the business and oversee the effectiveness of internal controls and processes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The risk management register and associated risk heat map provide a visual reflection of the Company's identified principal and emerging risks. These fall into three categories: strategic and business risk, financial and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, and regulatory and compliance risk. The Committee considers both the impact and the probability of each risk occurring and ensures appropriate controls are in place to reduce risk to an acceptable level. During the year under review the Committee was particularly concerned with the increase in geopolitical risk following the outbreak of war in the Ukraine. The subsequent rise in global energy prices, inflation and rising interest rates worldwide have led to a more uncertain investment environment. The increase in exchange rate volatility has had a particular impact on the values of Japanese portfolio holdings and the dividend income received. The Japanese (and Asian) economies have also taken longer to recover from the Covid-19 pandemic than those in Europe and America. The Committee continues to review the processes in place to mitigate risk; and to ensure that these are appropriate and proportionate in the current market environment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's ability to operate as a going concern and the Company's longer-term viability can be found in the Annual Report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The principal and emerging risks, together with a summary of the processes and internal controls used to manage and mitigate risks where possible are outlined below. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Principal Risks                   Mitigation                          Movement During the \par                                                                         Year \par  Poor investment performance       The Investment Manager \par   The Company's investment          has a well-defined investment \par   performance depends on            strategy and process which \par   the Investment Manager's          is regularly and rigorously \par   ability to identify successful    reviewed by the Board. \par   investments in accordance         The Board monitors the \par   with the Company's investment     Company's investment performance \par   policy.                           against its peer group \par                                     over a range of periods. 