\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - January 20\b0\par\par\uc2 2,017 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2023\par 20:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 USD dips as Fed pause, US recession angst rises; yen gains on further YCC tweak view\par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par Market briefs\par \par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Fed's Brainard: We downshifted rate hike pace in December to absorb more data, that logic is applicable today \u8226\'20\'22 Brainard says it will take time and resolve to get high inflation down to Fed's 2% target, policy will need to be sufficiently restrictive for some time \u8226\'20\'22 Fed's Collins: Policy rate needs to rise to likely just above 5%, then we need to hold rates there for some time, seeing noticeably slower goods inflation but services inflation remains persistently high \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. labor market still tight; housing mired in recession \u8226\'20\'22 US 14 Jan, w/e Initial Jobless Clm 190k, 214k f'cast, 205k prev \u8226\'20\'22 US 14 Jan, w/e Jobless Clm 4Wk Avg 206.00k, 212.50k prev \u8226\'20\'22 US 7 Jan, w/e Cont Jobless Claims 1.647 mln, 1.660 mln f'cast, 1.634 mln prev, 1.630 mln r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 US Dec Building Permits: Number 1.330 mln, 1.370 mln f'cast, 1.351 mln prev \u8226\'20\'22 US Dec Build Permits: Change MM -1.6%, -10.6% prev \u8226\'20\'22 US Dec Housing Starts Number 1.382 mln, 1.359 mln f'cast, 1.427 mln prev, 1.401 mln r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 US Dec House Starts MM: Change -1.4%, -0.5% prev, -1.8% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2  survey shows sharp drop in producer inflation \u8226\'20\'22 US Jan Philly Fed Business Indx -8.9, -11.0 f'cast, -13.8 prev, -13.7 r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. hits debt ceiling amid standoff between Republicans and Democrats \u8226\'20\'22 CA Nov Wholesale Trade MM 0.5%, 1.9% f'cast, 2.1% prev, 1.9% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Lagarde: Will stay course with rate hikes, inflation way too high \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2  Accounts: Members, overall, assessed that the risks to the inflation outlook were primarily on the upside \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2  Accounts: A large number of members initially expressed a preference for increasing the key \uc2 ECB\uc2  interest rates by 75 basis points \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2  Accounts: The steadiness of rate hikes and the time over which interest rates remained in restrictive territory mattered more Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 20 Jan 21:30 NZ Dec Manufacturing PMI 47.4 prev \u8226\'20\'22 20 Jan 23:30 JP Dec CPI, Core Nationwide YY 4.0% f'cast, 3.7% prev \u8226\'20\'22 20 Jan 23:30 JP Dec CPI, Overall Nationwide 3.8% prev \u8226\'20\'22 20 Jan 23:30 JP Dec CPI NSA 103.9 prev \u8226\'20\'22 20 Jan 23:30 JP Dec CPI MM NSA 0.2% prev \u8226\'20\'22 21 Jan 01:15 CN Jan Loan Prime Rate 1Y 3.65% prev; 5Y 4.30% prev Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 08:00 SNB's Jordan speaks on a panel in Davos \u8226\'20\'22 10:00 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Lagarde speaks on a panel in Davos \u8226\'20\'22 10:00 \uc2 IMF\uc2 's Georgieva speaks on a panel in Davos \u8226\'20\'22 11:00 \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2 's Brende speaks in Davos \u8226\'20\'22 11:00 \uc2 Riksbank\uc2 's Breman speaks on a panel in Vasteras \u8226\'20\'22 14:00 Fed's Harker speaks before the New Jersey Bankers Economic Leadership Forum \u8226\'20\'22 15:30 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Elderson participates at European Financial Services Roundtable \u8226\'20\'22 18:00 Fed's Waller speaks on the economic outlook before a Council on Foreign Relations event in New York Macro Themes In Play \u8226\'20\'22 The dollar index was modestly lower on Thursday, led by yen and euro gains of 0.3% and 0.25% which were underpinned by worries about a not-so-soft U.S. economic landing forcing the Fed to eventually veer away from its current higher-for-longer rates guidance \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. hard-landing concerns increased after Wednesday's dreadful and disinflationary grouping of data that Thursday's mixed housing and jobless claims numbers did little to mitigate \u8226\'20\'22 Helping eurozone yields and the euro higher versus Treasuries and the dollar were hawkish comments from \uc2 ECB\uc2  President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  and \uc2 Dutch \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Knot \u8226\'20\'22 The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is now following in the footsteps of the Fed's ongoing fight against the market pricing in a lower rate ceiling and rate cuts from H2 onward, but the \uc2 ECB\uc2  remains further than the Fed from rates that are restrictive enough to force inflation toward its target within its plausible policy horizon \u8226\'20\'22 That is seen more likely now that the worst-case energy crisis scenario stemming from Russia's war in Ukraine has so far been averted \u8226\'20\'22 In any event, EUR/USD has found support above its rising 10-day moving average after its brief risk-off/haven dollar-on dip on Wednesday \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY losses were partly offset by the recovery in Treasury yields from Wednesday's dour data dive and as yen bulls were deprived of another BoJ yield cap raise at Wednesday's meeting \u8226\'20\'22 Eventual dismantling of the \uc2 BoJ\uc2 's yield curve control and negative rates policy remains expected, but still faces hurdles from Friday's Japanese CPI report, wage negotiations and Governor Kuroda's April 18 departure \u8226\'20\'22 And as with the broader dollar index, USD/JPY's downtrend needs a sustained break below the midpoint of the pandemic advance to begin another major leg lower \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling rose 0.25%, shrugging off normally bearish drops in stocks and gilts-Treasury yield spreads. Prices are near major resistance from Wednesday's 1.2435 high by December's 1.2446 trend high and the 61.8% Fibo of the 2022's downtrend at 1.2442. The vast outperformance of the FTSE 100 over the S&P 500 since November is supportive \u8226\'20\'22 High-beta \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were under pressure again, with the Australian dollar's 0.63% drop exacerbated by soft jobs data \u8226\'20\'22 Japan's CPI is Friday's top data event risk Commentary and Analysis EUR/USD-Bulls take a stand below 1.0800 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD hit 1.08385 in Europe then sank, NY opened near 1.0825, fell further \u8226\'20\'22 US rate, US$ gains weighed; USD/CNH gains, stocks also weighed \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD hit 1.0782 on EBS after slew of US data then rallied \u8226\'20\'22 US$ buys abated, oil gained; EUR/USD neared 1.0825 late, up +0.25% \u8226\'20\'22 Techs are bullish; 10-DMA supports, RSIs rise, monthly bull hammer in place \u8226\'20\'22 German December PPI is a data risk in Europe's morning \u8226\'20\'22 Comments from Fed's Harker, Waller are looming risks for Friday's session USD/JPY-Heavy on Fed-BoJ convergence risk, but above key props pre-Japan CPI \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY is still consolidating above its key 50% Fibo at 126.56 on EBS \u8226\'20\'22 BoJ delaying further YCC loosening has cushioned prices fall \u8226\'20\'22 But BoJ eventually seen working out of ultra-loose policies \u8226\'20\'22 Friday's Japan CPI is immediate event risk in that regard \u8226\'20\'22 Vast majority of inflation has been imported, weak yen worsened \u8226\'20\'22 Very poor US data Wed also dampens already trimmed Fed hike pricing \u8226\'20\'22 Fed continues to push back against that pricing to little effect \u8226\'20\'22 A close below 50% of pandemic rally at 126.56 is key to next leg lower \u8226\'20\'22 While above, the 13- and 30-day MAs have been good spots for fades Early sterling slide reversed, recent trend high at 1.2446 in focus \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/USD ending NorAm near session high +0.23% at 1.2375; NY range 1.2378-20 \u8226\'20\'22 GBP$ firm despite higher UST yields; \uc2 BoE\uc2  seen hawkish amid high UK inflation \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling bulls may charge at Fibo level again soon \u8226\'20\'22 IRPR on Eikon indicates 71% odds for BoE +50bp Feb 2, Fed seen +25bp Feb 1 \u8226\'20\'22 Shallow GBP$ dip hints at pot'l for further gains \u8226\'20\'22 Res at 1.2404's upper 30-d Bolli, 1.2435 Wed high, 1.2446 Dec 14 trend high \u8226\'20\'22 Support 1.2314 Thurs low, 1.2262 daily Tenkan line, 1.2223 10-DMA \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/GBP flat on the day, Thurs range 0.8786-23; Lagarde, ECB Accts tipped hawkish AUD/USD-Bears cannot keep the momentum going \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD traded 0.6948-0.6875 overnight, NY opened near 0.6885, drop extended \u8226\'20\'22 Pair's fall aided by US rate, US$ gains; pair fell to 0.6872 \u8226\'20\'22 USD/CNH rally toward 6.7950, equity, copper drop helped weigh \u8226\'20\'22 US$ softened & copper bounced after US data hit the wires \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD rallied toward 0.6920 while USD/CNH dipped below 6.7800 \u8226\'20\'22 Pair sat near 0.6905 late in the session, was trading down -0.56% \u8226\'20\'22 Techs are mixed; Daily RSI rising but monthly falling, up trend is intact EUR/USD bulls stand their ground against bears With interest rate and technical influences on their side, EUR/USD bulls look likely to win their fight with bears, with scope to 1.0939 and 1.1076 if they do. U.S. weekly claims and housing indicated economic strength but the Philly Fed survey's prices-paid component indicates disinflation could be taking hold. The disinflation indication may give the Fed reason to take a less hawkish stance at its January 31-February 1 meeting. German-U.S. 2-year yield spreads , which EUR/USD is positively correlated with, are tightening again and are poised to break the January 12 tight. Technicals continue to highlight upside risks. In additions to support from the 10-day moving average, the monthly RSI is rising and a monthly bull hammer candle is in place for January. The 1.0939 is a 50% retracement point of 1.2349-0.9528, with 1.1076 marking the April 2022 high. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3GR7BLK"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3CZzgsF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Sterling bulls may charge at Fibo level again soon GBP/USD looks poised to take out its Dec. 14 trend high at 1.2446, courtesy of sticky UK core inflation data and U.S. Eurodollar futures pricing a lower terminal Fed Funds rate. 1.2446 is close to a 61.8% Fibo resistance level of the 2022 fall from 1.3749 to 1.0327, which was threatened after Wednesday's hot UK inflation in services prices data. \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  Governor Andrew Bailey on Thursday noted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not push back against market expectations for interest rates to peak at 4.5% in its December policy statement. Although the \uc2 BoE\uc2 's hike path looks less steep than in early November, the pound should remain well bid versus the dollar as futures indicate the Fed will reduce rates at a faster pace than the \uc2 BoE\uc2  later this year. This could leave UK rates with a 50 basis point yield advantage in 2024, which should boost GBP/USD. Ongoing expectations for Fed rate cuts in the second half of this year come despite recent hawkish Fed member comments. Several Fed members continue to forecast U.S. rates topping above 5% in 2023 and remaining there. Chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3CVLmTK"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Chart of the Day (USD/JPY) USD/JPY might need Friday's Japanese pure core CPI to rise more than forecast to get below key support at 126.56 which it has held above despite Wednesday's dismal U.S. data following the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  policy hold, with 120.57 a major target if it does. If Japan's December core-core CPI (all food, not just fresh food) remains well below 2% after rising just 1.4% YY in November, the \uc2 BoJ\uc2  will still have an argument against letting JGB yields run much higher, even if overall CPI rises to 4.0% from 3.7% as forecast. And if Q1 wage negotiations produce lower raises than hoped, the BoJ could delay stimulus removal amid largely imported inflation, while remaining wary about allowing yen weakness that inflates import costs. USD/JPY has already tumbled 15% from October's 32-year peak courtesy of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen, a dovish shift in Federal Reserve rate expectations and the BoJ raising its 10-year JGB yield cap to 0.5% in December. Far weaker than forecast U.S. data Wednesday undermined Fed guidance for 5%+ rates and no 2023 cuts, and expectations for eventual BoJ policy normalization remain high. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3XlHgfH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3ZLhqn9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 To read our latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news and analysis services, click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-1-22T19:35:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230119ej1j03btx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
