\par\par \b \uc2 Factbox-Who are candidates to become next \uc2 BOJ\uc2  governor?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,151 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2023\par 00:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Reuters) - Japan's government is likely to present its nominees for the next \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) governor and two deputy governors on Feb. 10, sources have told Reuters, a choice that will affect how soon the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could dial back its massive stimulus.\par \par \uc2 The nominees, if approved by both houses of parliament, will succeed \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  and his two deputies, \uc2 Masayoshi Amamiya\uc2  and \uc2 Masazumi Wakatabe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kuroda's term ends on April 8, while those of Amamiya and Wakatabe expire on March 19.\par \par \uc2 Below are possible candidates for the posts:\par \par \uc2 MASAYOSHI AMAMIYA - VERY STRONG CONTENDER FOR GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 Currently deputy governor, Amamiya has spent most of his career at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  drafting monetary policy ideas and is nick-named "Mr. \uc2 BOJ\uc2 " for masterminding many of the bank's unconventional monetary easing ideas.\par \par \uc2 He played a key role in drafting Kuroda's huge asset-buying programme in 2013 and consistently called for keeping ultra-low interest rates. But he also said in July the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  must "always" think about the means of exiting ultra-loose monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 An avid fan of classical music, Amamiya is known for his deep contacts with lawmakers and bureaucrats that help him read which way the political wind is blowing in steering policy.\par \par \uc2 HIROSHI NAKASO - VERY STRONG CONTENDER FOR GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 A career central banker who served as deputy governor until 2018, Nakaso played a key role in navigating an exit from the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's first spell of quantitative easing in 2006.\par \par \uc2 With long experience overseeing the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and international affairs, Nakaso has repeatedly warned of the drawbacks of prolonged monetary easing such as the distortion its huge presence could create in bond and money markets.\par \par \uc2 Nakaso laid out his idea of an exit from ultra-easy policy in a book published in May. Under that plan, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  would first ditch its 10-year bond yield target, then raise short-term interest rates and finally move to reduce its balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 HIROHIDE YAMAGUCHI - POSSIBLE CONTENDER FOR GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 A career central banker, Yamaguchi served as one of the two deputies of former \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Governor Masaaki Shirakawa and played a key role in crafting an asset-buying scheme under which the bank first began purchasing exchange-traded funds (ETF) in 2010.\par \par \uc2 Since retiring from the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  in 2013, he has criticised Kuroda's stimulus as relying too much on the view central banks can influence public perceptions with monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Warning of the rising cost of prolonged easing, Yamaguchi has also called for ending the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's huge asset purchases and abandoning an yield cap he describes as unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 MASATSUGU ASAKAWA - POSSIBLE CONTENDER FOR GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 Like Kuroda, Asakawa formerly had the role of Japan's top financial diplomat, a job he took in 2015 and kept for four years. During that time, he battled a strong yen and engaged in tough negotiations with then U.S. President Donald Trump's administration on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 After retiring from the finance ministry, he became president of the \uc2 Asian Development Bank\uc2  in 2020 - a job Kuroda also took before becoming \uc2 BOJ\uc2  governor.\par \par \uc2 In his book, Asakawa praised Kuroda's stimulus programme as a right move to beat deflation. He also said yen declines would be harder to stop than yen rises via \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because the amount of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can sell to prop up the yen would be limited to the size of its foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 DARK HORSE CANDIDATES\par \par \uc2 Traditionally, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  and the finance ministry take turns filling the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor post, holding the post for only a single five-year term. That broke when Kuroda was appointed in 2018 to serve a second five-year term.\par \par \uc2 If history is any guide, the next \uc2 BOJ\uc2  governor would be chosen from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ranks. But there is a chance Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  opts for a dark horse candidate.\par \par \uc2 Among names floated by analysts are \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  professor Takatoshi Ito, who is close to Kuroda, and former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat \uc2 Takehiko Nakao\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Given Kishida's close ties with the finance ministry, he may hand-pick a former top bureaucrat from the institution, though there is no consensus on who could be among strong candidates.\par \par \uc2 SHINICHI UCHIDA - POSSIBLE DEPUTY GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 A career central banker who spent most of his career at the elite monetary affairs department, Uchida landed a rare reappointment for a second, four-year term as the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's executive director overseeing monetary policy. Many analysts see the reappointment as a sign he could fill one of the two deputy governor posts.\par \par \uc2 Together with Amamiya, Uchida played a key role in drafting many of Kuroda's policies including the introduction of negative interest rates and yield curve control in 2016.\par \par \uc2 People who know him say Uchida firmly believes that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  must maintain ultra-loose policy to reflate growth and tighten the job market enough so that wages rise more.\par \par \uc2 TAKEO HOSHI - POSSIBLE DEPUTY GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 An economics professor at the \uc2 University of Tokyo\uc2 , Hoshi is well-versed in Japanese monetary policy and is a regular member of government panels and the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's academic workshops.\par \par \uc2 At one of the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  workshops held in November, Hoshi explained how structural changes in Japan's labour market could push up average wages more than in the past.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  must start worrying about the possibility of inflation accelerating more than expected," he told Reuters, adding the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  may abandon its yield cap as early as this year.\par \par \uc2 FEMALE DEPUTY GOVERNOR CANDIDATES\par \par \uc2 No female has yet served as \uc2 BOJ\uc2  deputy governor or governor, a tradition Kishida may seek to change to enhance diversity.\par \par \uc2 One possible candidate is Tokiko Shimizu, who rose up the ranks in the male-dominated institution and now serves as executive director overseeing international affairs. People close to her describe Shimizu as dovish on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Other candidates include think tank head Yuri Okina, who calls for phasing out the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's yield cap, and former \uc2 BOJ\uc2  board member Sayuri Shirai, who proposes a review of the current stimulus so the bank can adjust interest rates more flexibly.\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL BUREAUCRAT DEPUTY GOVERNOR CANDIDATES\par \par \uc2 If a former \uc2 BOJ\uc2  executive becomes governor, there is a strong chance one of the deputy governor posts will be filled by top finance ministry bureaucrats such as Shigeaki Okamoto and Yasushi Kinoshita.\par \par \uc2 Kinoshita told Reuters the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  should eventually head for an exit from ultra-loose policy, and in doing so must move "cautiously and steadily" to avoid causing huge market turbulence.\par \par \uc2 Ryozo Himino, a former head of Japan's financial watchdog, is also considered a dark horse candidate. People close to him describe Himino as critical of the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's negative rates policy for the damage it is inflicting on commercial banks' profits.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-1-17T23:48:23.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230117ej1h044gh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
