\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Is the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  sheathing its bazooka?\b0\par\par\uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the bond market to keep yields anchored near the zero bound, and the market expected it to maintain its current "yield curve control" (YCC) policy.\par \par \uc2 TOKYO - Last December 20, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  decided to raise the ceiling on 10-year government bond yields from 0.25 percent to 0.5 percent. Minutes after the announcement, the yen appreciated against the U.S. dollar by 3 percent, the Nikkei 225 index fell by 2.5 percent and the ten-year bond rate jumped almost 25 basis points to approach the new ceiling.\par \par \uc2 This strong reaction was largely due to the fact that the decision took investors by surprise. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the bond market to keep yields anchored near the zero bound, and the market expected it to maintain its current "yield curve control" (YCC) policy until the end of Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 's ten-year rule in early April 2023. But, according to Kuroda, it has become necessary to allow greater flexibility in the ten-year bond market to strengthen the effectiveness of the YCC.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  had been buying increasing sums of ten-year bonds at a rate of 0.25%, often adding them to its balance sheet a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued them. Now, one can infer that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was alarmed to find itself buying bonds at an accelerated pace. After all, the yield curve recently fell into its ten-year mode, meaning that the ten-year bond rate was lower than the nine-year and 15-year bond rates.\par \par \uc2 Although the \uc2 Enel\uc2  shift in control of the yield curve is tantamount to a rise in long-term interest rates, Kuroda insists that it should not be interpreted as the first step in a broader process of monetary policy tightening. The real interest rate, he points out, has been declining over the past year as the inflation rate has been rising. By raising the cap on ten-year yields; thus, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  was effectively adjusting for inflation, rather than raising the real interest rate.\par \par \uc2 Kuroda is right on this technical point. But the YCC change could still be the first step toward normalizing monetary policy. If so, it should be heralded as a sign that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  has had some success with its decade-long ultra-loose monetary policy. Kuroda's Bazooka, as it is colloquially known, may have hit the mark.\par \par \uc2 Since Kuroda's appointment in 2013, Japan's inflation rate remained mostly above zero but well below 2%, leading some critics to argue that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's inflation targeting framework was a failure. But others, including myself, defended that policy. From the perspective of a flexible inflation targeting regime, we argued, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  was balancing below-target inflation with Japan's strong GDP growth (or unemployment rate) in its assessment.\par \par \uc2 But one point was always clear: the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  failed to anchor inflation expectations at 2%, which was supposed to be one of the great benefits of the inflation targeting framework. Now, the inflation rate (excluding fresh food) has risen sharply. A year ago, in January 2022, it was still at only 0.2%; but by April it had reached 2.1% and by September it was already 3% As in many other countries, this pickup was driven by increases in global energy and food prices and a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even so, because Japanese inflation has been well below that of the United States (7.11 %), the United Kingdom (10.7 %) and the Eurozone (10.1 %), the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  had consistently refused, at least until December 20, to raise interest rates. Shortly before, in October 2022, the members of its Policy Board projected that inflation would fall back to 1.6% this coming year.\par \par \uc2 But now there is reason to suspect that this forecast will be proven wrong. First, the just-released November data show that the inflation rate, excluding energy and food prices, is 2.8%. Thus, even if energy and food prices were to stop rising in 2023, the inflation rate could remain above 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Second, the upcoming "spring offensive" this year (annual wage negotiations) is expected to generate large wage increases, partly to offset the higher inflation rate in 2022, and partly to redistribute the higher gains from yen depreciation, in the event that substantial wage increases would replace some of the "cost-push" inflation of 2022 with "demand-pull" inflation (by increasing the relative purchasing power of many households), which tends to be relatively easier for consumers to accept.\par \par \uc2 That would be an ideal initial condition for the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  to begin to achieve its inflation target on a more sustainable basis. It is possible that this year will bring a happy ending to Japan's decade-long ultra-loose monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The author\par \par \uc2 A former Japanese vice finance minister, he is a professor at Columbia University's School of International and Public Affairs and a senior lecturer at the National Graduate Institute of Policy Studies in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Copyright: Project Syndicate, 1995 - 2023\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230112ej1c0079e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscale