SD/JPY-Edges lower with the USD, as Treasury yields fall \u8226\'20\'22 Down 0.2% in a 130.59-131.45 Asian range, with the USD a touch softer \u8226\'20\'22 Nikkei -1.4% after the long weekend - 10yr UST yields -6bp to 3.731% \u8226\'20\'22 Successful \uc2 BOJ\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to yield further yen gains \u8226\'20\'22 BOJ Kuroda sticks with easy money to meet 2% inflation target \u8226\'20\'22 Charts; neutral momentum studies, 21 day Bollinger bands head lower \u8226\'20\'22 5, 10 & 21 DMAs fall, Tenkan and Kijun lines flat line - downside bias \u8226\'20\'22 134.70 horizontal Kijun line capped in December - remains pivotal resistance \u8226\'20\'22 Tuesday's 129.51 low and 128.16, 61.8% 2022 rise are initial supports EUR/USD-Firms in Asia as risk assets bounce back \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened -1.1% at 1.0548 after USD broadly strengthened \u8226\'20\'22 After dipping to 1.0541 it tracked higher when Asian markets rallied \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD traded to 1.0569 before settling around 1.0565 into the afternoon \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at former support at the 21-day MA at 1.0607 \u8226\'20\'22 A break above 1.0610 would relieve downward pressure \u8226\'20\'22 Support is at yesterday's 1.0519 low with buyers tipped ahead of 1.0500 \u8226\'20\'22 The close below the 21-day MA suggests a short-term top is in place \u8226\'20\'22 A break below 1.0500 targets the 55-day MA at 1.0342 \u8226\'20\'22 Fed minutes later today will be the main event for markets AUD/USD-Strong bounce as risk assets rally in Asia \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened -1.03% at 0.6727 as USD broadly strengthened and risk sagged \u8226\'20\'22 After trading at 0.6717 AUD/USD tracked higher as Asian markets rallied \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD short-covering when it broke above 0.6750 and traded to 0.6776 \u8226\'20\'22 The 10 & 21-day MAs converge at 0.6745/50 and now provide support \u8226\'20\'22 Key resistance is at the 200-day MA at 0.6852 with sellers ahead of 0.6850 \u8226\'20\'22 Weak China sentiment and global growth concerns may limit upside \u8226\'20\'22 Fed minutes later today will be the key events for markets GBP/USD-Firmer with risk, can PM Sunak inspire confidence? \u8226\'20\'22 +0.15% with the USD -0.2%, in a 1.1960-1.2002 range with constant D3 flow \u8226\'20\'22 Risk appetite resurfaced in Asia, UST yields fell 10yr -7bp to 3.729% \u8226\'20\'22 UK fresh food prices surge 15% in year to December: BRC \u8226\'20\'22 Long tough winter ahead- can PM Sunak help in his speech today? \u8226\'20\'22 Charts; daily momentum studies edge lower and 21 day Bollinger bands expand \u8226\'20\'22 5, 10 & 21 day moving averages ease, setup suggests the base is weak side \u8226\'20\'22 1.1894 lower 21 day Bollinger band, and 1.2137 21 DMA likely contain \u8226\'20\'22 1.1960 Asian low and 1.2057 NY high are initial support, resistance Global growth fears to undermine AUD/USD in early 2023 Global growth concerns appear to be the dominant theme as 2023 gets underway and their negative impact on demand for key commodities is likely to keep the Australian dollar under pressure. AUD/USD rose nearly 12% between mid-October and mid-December, on broad U.S. dollar weakness and as hopes China would take the necessary steps to open its economy and spur demand underpinned sentiment. China's radical reopening - suddenly abandoning strict COVID protocols - has resulted in a massive wave of infections, according to unofficial estimates. Investors fear the disruptions to activity and demand may become critical enough to slow China's economy and impact global supply chains. The \uc2 IMF\uc2  has warned the challenges facing the global economy in 2023 will be more difficult to overcome than in 2022. Unlike previous economic downturns, central banks are not in a position to provide support, as inflation-fighting remains the top priority. The Australian dollar is vulnerable due to its close correlation to commodity prices and role as a proxy for China sentiment. The AUD/USD fell over 1% on Tuesday, trading below 0.6700 at one stage. The next level of support is at 0.6616, the 38.2 Fibonacci retracement of the October-December rise. A break below that level targets the 61.8 Fibo at 0.6446. Key resistance is at the 200-day moving average at 0.6852; shorts should place a stop-loss above that reading. Successful \uc2 BOJ\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to yield further gains The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September-October 2022, to support the weakening yen, is now looking like very profitable business. Perceptions on the outlook for Japanese monetary policy have changed due to building expectations that the next \uc2 BOJ\uc2  governor will be more hawkish on inflation. The Nikkei reported on Saturday that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  is considering raising its inflation forecasts in January to show price growth close to its 2% target in fiscal 2023 and 2024, which would open the door to a move away from ultra-loose monetary policy. Ten-year Japanese government bond yields have traded just below the new upper 0.50% limit since the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's Dec 20 band widening, and the pressure on the band will be a key driver of USD/JPY. Hawkish Federal Reserve expectations still suggest USD/JPY yield differentials will widen in Q1 2023, but a major change in Japan policy should support the yen. USD/JPY is down around 14% from its October 151.94 trend high, but still climbed 12% in 2022. Technically 5, 10 and 21-day moving averages fall and 21-day Bollinger bands head lower in a bearish setup. USD/JPY is poised above strong long-term support at the 129.65 weekly cloud top, which has supported since February 2021, so the pace of the current fall may slow, but the bias is lower while the 134.70 horizontal daily Kijun line caps, a top since November. Yuan bulls keep the faith as China prepares more stimulus Yuan investors have cause for optimism as China's finance minister said fiscal spending will be increased this year to spur the economy, soon after the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  promised more monetary policy support. The looming double-barrelled economic stimulus is much needed. The Caixin manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  published Tuesday showed a fifth straight period of contraction in November, despite slightly beating expectations. Official PMI data was more downbeat, especially in the services sector. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said on Friday it would step up implementation of "prudent" monetary policy; analysts expect more easing in 2023. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also repeated its pledge to enhance the yuan's flexibility while keeping it basically stable. The same day, the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System (CFETS) tweaked the weightings of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, in an annual adjustment to align the renminbi index with China's trade flows. The USD and EUR weightings were reduced again, which should help buffer the CFETS index from gyrations in those two major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The greenback now constitutes 19.7% of the basket, versus 26.4% before the 2017 adjustment. The overnight bounce in USD/CNH is now being sold, after an as-expected \uc2 PBOC\uc2  yuan midpoint. There is a good chance the 200-day moving average at 6.8658 will be tested soon; if that support goes, the 6.8000 psychological threshold probably won't hold for long.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-1-7T04:33:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230104ej1400f1p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
