\par\par \b \uc2 Japan's Nakao sees smoother path for Kuroda's successor with \uc2 BOJ\uc2  policy shift\b0\par\par\uc2 555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2022\par 02:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Reuters) -The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) has modified its stimulus measures to ease the transition away from an unconventional monetary policy when Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  retires in April, former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat \uc2 Takehiko Nakao\uc2  told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Prolonged monetary easing has amplified side effects such as blunting market functions, excessive yen weakening and looser fiscal discipline, at the expense of increases in real income, said the former vice finance minister for international affairs.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised markets last week by relaxing its yield tolerance for 10-year Japanese government bonds (JGBs), a move aimed at easing the cost of prolonged economic stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  has not succeeded so much in raising inflation expectations and bringing down real interest rates while side-effects became larger. I was thinking the current framework must be modified sooner or later," he said.\par \par \uc2 "I am not sure about the reason for the latest action, but it may have an effect of alleviating burden for whoever succeeds Kuroda regarding all the negative shocks stemming from adjustments.\par \par \uc2 \u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0 "It was good in a sense that it reduced the burdens of starting policy adjustment."\par \par \uc2 Altering the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's easy-money policy would cause shocks such as pushing up mortgage interest rates and JGB yields, but that needs be done at some point, Nakao said, also noting that the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's 2% inflation target - stipulated in a government accord - may be making monetary and fiscal policy inflexible.\par \par \uc2 Nakao was president of the \uc2 Asian Development Bank\uc2  from 2013 through early 2020 and is now "Chairman of the Institute" at Mizuho Research and Technologies, part of \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group Inc\uc2 , Japan's third-biggest commercial bank.\par \par \uc2 RECESSION RISK\par \par \uc2 Nakao sounded cautiously sanguine about the global economic outlook next year.\par \par \uc2 Speculation lingers about a global downturn due to the global tightening of monetary policy, but Nakao saw no need to be pessimistic, with the United States backed by solid domestic demand and a flexible job market which may lead to sustainable wage gains.\par \par \uc2 There's no need to think that a recession is inevitable, he said.\par \par \uc2 Although the strong dollar may strain emerging-market debt, Nakao brushed aside the risk of return of the Asian financial crisis due to stable monetary policies, stricter fiscal discipline and financial regulation in the region.\par \par \uc2 As the top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat, he \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to stem yen strength when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded around 75 yen in 2011 in the aftermath of an earthquake, tsunami and nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 "The yen was too strong back then, but now the yen is clearly too weak," Nakao said, declining to specify preferred levels under current circumstances.\par \par \uc2 In September, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in 24 years to back the yen which weakened against the U.S. dollar to a 32-year low of around 150 yen.\par \par \uc2 Nakao said Japan's waning economic power and its excessively expansionist policy are weighing on the yen and making Japanese assets vulnerable to takeovers by overseas investors.\par \par \uc2 "It's certain that excessive yen weakness is bad," Nakao said. "It is helpful that raising interest rates lead to some strengthening of the yen."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Christopher Cushing and Muralikumar Anantharaman) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-12-28T01:22:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221227eicr00tbl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
