\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Surges After Surprise Bank of Japan Interest Rate Tweak -- Barrons.com\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Swint \par \uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2022\par 10:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The yen surged against the dollar Tuesday morning after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  changed a key plank of its interest-rate policy.\par \par \uc2 For years Japan has lagged far behind other major central banks in keeping interest rates ultra low. Policy makers in the U.S. and Europe have tightened at the fastest pace in a generation to try to rein in inflation after a surge in energy prices caused it to surge to four-decade highs this year.\par \par \uc2 But Tuesday the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  made its surprise move and the yen rose. One dollar bought 132 yen, compared with more than 137 yen before the Tuesday announcement. That's a change of more than 3%. The Japanese yen had been weakening for much of this year as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  rapidly increased interest rates while Japan insisted on keeping them at rock bottom to bolster the economy.\par \par \uc2 Short-term interest rates have been close to zero in Japan for years, yet the stimulative policy has failed to generate inflation as intended. That led the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2016 to start doing yield-curve control. This involves buying longer-dated bonds to keep interest rates low at two years, ten years and other maturities.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  said it would change the cap for the interest-rate on the 10-year government bond from 0.25% to 0.5%. That rate quickly climbed to 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 Raising interest rates usually causes \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen because it makes the relative returns in that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive. It also tends to push down stocks because it becomes more expensive for companies to borrow money. The Nikkei slipped more than 2%.\par \par \uc2 In September, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to try to stop the yen from weakening so much against its peers.\par \par \uc2 Write to brian.swint@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 20, 2022 04:50 ET (09:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221220eick000sr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
