\par\par \b \uc2 The Bank of Japan Shocked Markets, but Not These Investors -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anna Hirtenstein and Julie Steinberg \par \uc2 865 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2022\par 19:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Betting against the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has finally begun to pay off. Some think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just getting started on a more sweeping change in policy direction.\par \par \uc2 Trades that bet that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will tighten monetary policy have been a losing proposition for so long that they are colloquially known in markets as the widowmaker. But the most recent crop of skeptics has scored a victory.\par \par \uc2 "It was becoming increasingly clear that what they were doing in Japan, it just didn't make sense," said Jonathan Gregory, head of fixed income at \uc2 UBS Asset Management\uc2 's U.K. office. He had been betting that Japanese government bonds would lose value since the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  raised the limit on 10-year government bond yields, a policy known as yield curve control, which keeps borrowing costs below a certain threshold. The move is seen as the first step toward tighter financial conditions in the country that has maintained ultra-easy monetary policy for decades.\par \par \uc2 The move inches the institution closer in line with other major economies' central banks, which have been raising rates and curtailing bond-buying programs to fight the worst bout of global inflation since the 1970s. The Bank of Japan was the outlier. It has kept monetary policy easy with negative interest rates and a massive bond-buying program. This drove the yen to the weakest level against the dollar in nearly a third of a century earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been getting burned for decades betting against the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 . Many who have placed bets over the years have come and gone. Big Wall Street investors and hedge funds that bet on macroeconomic policy changes became convinced this time was different. The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 , they figured, couldn't keep rates at their super-low levels while the rest of the world was increasing borrowing costs at their fastest pace in years.\par \par \uc2 Even for those betting for this to happen, the timing was still a surprise.\par \par \uc2 "We were positioned for it, while not expecting it to happen this early, " said John Roe, head of multiasset funds at \uc2 Legal & General Investment Management\uc2 . "We exited the JGB positions this morning."\par \par \uc2 He expects the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will have to keep tightening policy and is buying the yen in anticipation that it will keep strengthening. Other investors are also increasing bets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will let borrowing costs rise further and potentially even raise its short-term policy rate.\par \par \uc2 Caygan Capital, an investment firm that manages around $450 million from London and Singapore, began shorting Japanese government bond futures in June. Naruhisa Nakagawa, the firm's founder, said at the time he believed the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  had accomplished its goals of higher inflation and accelerating wage growth, and would abandon its yield curve control policy.\par \par \uc2 He said Tuesday that one of the firm's funds is up more than 10% this year, helped by the "meaningful" short position. Mr. Nakagawa is convinced yields will head even higher -- toward 1% -- and hasn't taken any profits yet.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the 10-year bond yield jumped to a multiyear high. It closed at 0.417%, according to Tullett Prebon data, the highest closing level since 2015. As of early afternoon in New York, the dollar had fallen more than 4% against the yen, putting the dollar on track for its biggest decline against its Japanese counterpart since March 2009.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is rising in Japan, a shift from the country's long running deflationary pressures. Consumer prices rose at an annual rate of 3.7% in October. While that is substantially below other major economies, with the most recent readings for the U.S. at 7.1% and for the eurozone at 10%, it is also well above the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's 2% target.\par \par \uc2 The weak yen has exacerbated the country's new inflation. Japan is an importer of energy, which is largely priced in dollars. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached a multidecade low of 152 yen per dollar in October. More recently, the weakening dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry\uc2  have driven a moderate recovery in the yen.\par \par \uc2 The timing, coming after the yen had strengthened without a \uc2 BOJ\uc2  move, was likely an important consideration, according to Edouard de Langlade, founder of EDL Capital.\par \par \uc2 "If they had done it when the yen was at 150, it would have shown weakness. By doing it now, they are showing they are not caving to the market," Mr. de Langlade said. "It is more from a position of strength."\par \par \uc2 Anna Raytcheva, who runs Sonya Capital Management LLC, a Palm Beach, Fla.-based global macro hedge fund, said she started betting the yen would trade higher in the second half of the year. "The trade was initially a drag on performance, but it's finally worked out," she said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anna Hirtenstein at anna.hirtenstein@wsj.com and Julie Steinberg at julie.steinberg@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 20, 2022 13:46 ET (18:46 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221220eick002ek\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
