\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: BOJ Surprises Markets With Change in Yield-Curve Control\b0\par\par\uc2 1,502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2022\par 15:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan surprised markets by altering its yield-curve control to allow 10-year Japanese government bond yields to rise to 0.5% from the previous cap of 0.25%. The move causes a sharp rise in the yen and analysts expect further gains. The impact goes beyond Japan, however, with European government-bond yields rising and stocks falling. The following is a selection of analyst comments.\par \par \uc2 European Stocks Fall After Bank of Japan Yield Move\par \par \uc2 1237 GMT - European stocks drop ahead of an expected slightly higher U.S. open following a bond yield-related move by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  that sparked speculation about a more hawkish \uc2 BOJ\uc2  stance on interest rates. The Stoxx Europe 600, CAC 40 and DAX drop 0.2% while the FTSE 100 trades flat. Brent crude rises 0.3% to $80.05 a barrel. IG futures data show the Dow opening at 32809, versus Monday's close of 32757. "Those hoping for a quiet run to Christmas were given an awakening as the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  announced a change to its yield curve control program," IG analysts say in a note. "This will allow longer-term rates to rise more, reducing the costs of monetary stimulus over time." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 European Banks Rise on Hawkish Sentiment\par \par \uc2 1207 GMT - Bank stocks trade higher on Tuesday in Europe as investors digest the prospect of further interest-rate hikes after a hawkish \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Germany's \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  is up 7.1%, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  rises 4.3%, Italy's \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  is 2.8% higher, while Spain's Santander is up 2.5%, \uc2 BBVA\uc2  climbs 2.4% and Unicaja jumps 6.1%. The surprising \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 , which raised its effective cap on 10-year government bond yields within its yield-curve-control policy, took markets by surprise. The Bank of Japan had been the last major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain ultralow interest rates.(edward.frankl@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's Move Should Snap Period of Yen Undervaluation\par \par \uc2 1137 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's change to yield-curve control to allow the 10-year bond yield to increase to 0.5% from 0.25% previously should stop this year's hefty yen selling and "record levels of undervaluation," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  analyst \uc2 Derek Halpenny\uc2  says in a note. The yen dropped sharply as the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  stuck to ultra-loose monetary policy while other central banks raised rates, but USD/JPY could now head "into the 120s sooner than we expected," Halpenny says. The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's shift suggests that "the divergence at the heart of the surge in yen selling in 2022 is going to reverse in 2023," he says. USD/JPY falls more than 3.5% to a four-month low of 131.9950, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . It began 2022 at around 115. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 UK Gilt Yields Rise After \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's Surprise Policy Move\par \par \uc2 1006 GMT - U.K. gilt yields jump after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's unexpected decision to widen the yield-curve control band to 50 basis points either side of zero from 25bps previously, which is seen as an indication of future policy tightening, say \uc2 RBC\uc2  analysts in a note. "\uc2 BOJ\uc2  governor Kuroda said that this move did not represent a rate hike. Yet, it will be hard for markets not to see this move as a precursor for tightening policy," the analysts say. The yield on the 10-year U.K. gilt is up by around 10 basis points on the day at 3.584%, while the two-year gilt yield is up around 8 basis points at 3.756% according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2 . (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 Falls in Early Trade as \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Move Spooks Markets\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT - The FTSE 100 falls 0.2% to 7346.60 as a surprise decision by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to tweak yield-curve control to allow the 10-year bond yield to increase to around 0.5% from 0.25% previously spooks markets globally, exacerbating fears about tighter monetary policy just as recession looms. "Tighter \uc2 BOJ\uc2  policy would remove one of the last global anchors that's helped to keep borrowing costs at low levels more broadly," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  analysts say in a note. Equities had already got the week off to a weak start on fears that central banks will continue raising rates in 2023, they say. Retail and property stocks fall, partly offset by rises in heavyweight mining and oil-linked companies. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's Surprise Move Could Radically Alter Yen's Outlook for 2023\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's unexpected change to its yield-curve control policy, widening the target band for the ten-year government-bond yield to 50 basis points from 25 basis points, could substantially change the yen's outlook for 2023, ING analyst \uc2 Francesco Pesole\uc2  says in a note. The yen surges, with USD/JPY dropping 3.5% to a four-month low of 132.1070, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . The decision essentially allows higher interest rates despite the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  still officially targeting 0.0% as the outright target, Pesole says. "Markets are now assessing whether today's announcement is effectively a first step towards a broader policy normalisation process in Japan, which would quite radically change the outlook for the yen in 2023," he says. (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Metals Rise Despite Caution Over Risk Assets\par \par \uc2 0829 GMT - Metals prices are inching higher despite caution in other asset classes as worries over the state of the global economy mount. Three-month copper is up 0.5% to $8,323.50 a metric ton while nickel is 0.8% higher at $27,450 a ton. Gold is also up 0.5% to $1,806.20 a troy ounce. "Markets have given back all of the gains from early December's softer PPI and CPI inflation prints," Dave Whitcomb, head of research at Peak Trading Research says in a note, adding that a hawkish \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  is a headwind for risk assets and commodity markets. Elsewhere, a shift in monetary policy from the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has lowered equity and oil markets this morning, while bond yields are rising. (yusuf.khan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Eurozone Government Bond Yields Jump as BoJ Lets Yields Rise\par \par \uc2 0729 GMT - Eurozone government bond yields are trading higher after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  adjusted the yield-curve-control policy in a surprise move. This adjustment, whereby the \uc2 BoJ\uc2  allows the 10-year Japanese government bond yield to rise to 0.5% from a previous cap of 0.25%, "is widely seen as the beginning of a potential end to their ultra-loose monetary policy," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's strategists write in a note. "It's important not to underestimate the impact this could have, because tighter BoJ policy would remove one of the last global anchors that's helped to keep borrowing costs at low levels more broadly," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's Jim Reid writes. The 10-year German Bund yield trades more than 6 basis points higher at 2.266%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2 . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY May Head to 130.00 After \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's 10-Year JGB Yield Cap Rise\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT - JPY may strengthen further against USD and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's surprise move to raise its 10-year JGB yield cap to 0.5% from 0.25% previously. "No action was expected from today's meeting - and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  once again teach us that complacency is the devil," says Matthew Simpson, market analyst at City Index, in an email. "This is arguably the biggest surprise they have handed markets since moving to negative interest rates in January 2016," he says, adding USD/JPY could now be headed for 130.00. USD/JPY is down 2.7% at 133.27 after earlier touching 133.10, its lowest intraday level since mid-August, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's Loosening of Yield Control a Surprise But Timing Is Good\par \par \uc2 0351 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's decision to loosen its grip on the yield curve comes as a surprise but at the right time, says Akira Moroga, chief market strategist at \uc2 Aozora Bank\uc2 . As the rising momentum in global bond yields has eased, \uc2 BOJ\uc2  officials must have thought domestic yields are unlikely to rise sharply even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loosens its control, Moroga says. That said, the move around the holiday season was a surprise, he says. The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  says the 10-year yield can now move freely between -0.5% and +0.5%, wider than the previous range between -0.25% to +0.25%. USD/JPY is at 133.59, down from 137.23 prior to the announcement.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 20, 2022 09:52 ET (14:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221220eick001qy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
