\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-U.S.  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - November 22\b0\par\par\uc2 1,110 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2022\par 12:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dollar relaxes despite China COVID curbs\par \par \uc2 Market Brief\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD 0.46%, USD/JPY -0.67%, GBP/USD 0.46%, EUR/GBP -0.05% \u8226\'20\'22 DXY -0.49%, DAX 0.3%, FTSE 0.7%, Brent 0.77%, Gold 0.56% \u8226\'20\'22 Beijing shuts parks, museums as China's COVID cases rise \u8226\'20\'22 Europe to be hit hardest in global slowdown -\uc2 OECD\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 POLL-\uc2 ECB\uc2  set to raise deposit rate 50 bps as euro zone enters recession -economists \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Holzmann says leaning towards a 0.75% rate hike as things stand \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Rehn says inflation, economy will decide pace of rate hikes \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Nagel opens door to smaller hikes but sees long way to go \u8226\'20\'22 EZ Sep Current Account NSA, EUR 3.81 bln, -20.21 bln prev; SA, EUR -8.060 bln, -26.320 bln prev \u8226\'20\'22 Almost one in five Germans dipping into savings to get by - poll \u8226\'20\'22 German 2022 industry output to rise slightly, 2023 looks gloomy - BDI \u8226\'20\'22 German manufacturing companies reaching gas savings limit \u8211\'20\'13 Ifo \u8226\'20\'22 German finance minister: must talk about fiscal stimulus for 2024 budget \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrows less than expected in October, but worse to come \u8226\'20\'22 China's key money rate hits near 2-year low, but softening seen fleeting \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's price gauge hits record high in sign of broadening inflation\par \par \uc2 Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT)\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 13:30 CA Oct New Housing Price Index -0.1% prev \u8226\'20\'22 13:30 CA Sep Retail Sales MM -0.5% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 0.7% prev \u8226\'20\'22 13:55 US 19 Nov, w/e Redbook YY 6.8% prev\par \par \uc2 Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT)\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 13:30 \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2  issues Nonmanufacturing Business Outlook Survey for November \u8226\'20\'22 15:00 Spain's economy minister \uc2 Nadia Calvino\uc2  speaks in upper house \u8226\'20\'22 16:00 Fed\u8217\'20\'19s Mester speaks at virtual event, "Cleveland Fed Conversations on Central Banking: Wages and Inflation" in Cleveland \u8226\'20\'22 17:00 BoC\u8217\'20\'19s Rogers does a Fireside Chat on Assessment of risks to the stability of the Canadian financial system in Ottawa \u8226\'20\'22 19:45 Fed\u8217\'20\'19s Bullard, and \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s George participate in a panel, St. Louis\par \par \uc2 Commentary and Analysis\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD may enter a consolidation which will favour sellers\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD rally stalled 1.0481 before Monday's drop to 1.0222 EBS \u8226\'20\'22 Pair's sell-off stalled at 0.9528 following drop from 1.2266 \u8226\'20\'22 Target for minimum tech correction 1.2266-0.9528 drop is 1.0574 \u8226\'20\'22 Target for minimum tech correction 0.9528-1.0481 rise is 1.0117 \u8226\'20\'22 Consolidation within ranges near 1.00-1.05 or 1.01-1.06 may evolve \u8226\'20\'22 Quieter periods favour those whose positions align with interest rates \u8226\'20\'22 Thee is a substantial carry in favour USD - benefits top pickers\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY down, flow data shows spot sold in waves this week\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Japan shares end up; caution ahead of Fed minutes limit gains \u8226\'20\'22 USD needs to break key supply to keep rebound alive \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders sell USD/JPY in waves since start of the week EBS flow data shows \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY suffered a setback from 142.25 in Asia, to 141.17 in Ldn on the EBS \u8226\'20\'22 Thick daily cloud spans the 140.57-145.87 region, seems to limit the upside \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY chart outlook is mixed \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY, EUR/JPY 30-day log correlation above +0.50 = positive relationship\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD-Strong demand for UK assets does little for pound\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Orders for UK's 2073 index-linked gilt exceed GBP 13 billion \u8226\'20\'22 FTSE 100 hits two-month peak as oil stocks rebound \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrows less than expected in October \u8226\'20\'22 Positive backdrop has limited impact for pound \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/USD up 0.4% while EUR/GBP flat and GBP/JPY down 0.3%\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD trying to recover but facing headwinds\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Technically, the AUD remains pressured: Mon cloud break bearish \u8226\'20\'22 Cloud top, 0.6653, now stalling a modest rebound from 0.6585 Mon low \u8226\'20\'22 The 100 day moving average at 0.6691 also a problem for AUD bulls \u8226\'20\'22 A 38.2% Fibo taken off the recent 0.6170-0.6797 climb is at 0.6557 \u8226\'20\'22 Converged 21-30DMAs at 0.6540 further support tight below \u8226\'20\'22 Market likely tight ahead of Wed's FOMC minutes \u8226\'20\'22 China's stop-start COVID curb-easing will frustrate AUD bulls \u8226\'20\'22 For now the Aussie is finding support above 0.6600: 0.6602 the low\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is definitely one to avoid\par \par \uc2 In October the yen dropped to its lowest level since 1970 - a record low according to available data - and with Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  determined to stick to policies driving the decline, the yen is likely going to fall much further. There is a possibility that yen's extreme weakness triggers a flight from Japanese assets that further undermines the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and could lead to disorderly market conditions that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  may struggle to contain. This risk has grown due to the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curtail a USD/JPY rise supported by its own policy as well as monetary policy in the United States. By \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forcing traders out of their required positions, leaving them less prepared for the decline driven by Japan's monetary policy. Unless Japan changes policy - which is cause to hedge the risk of a significant movement in bond markets - the yen will likely extend its 30% drop seen in the last 29 months. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3AEiqyr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3XrMUh8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 USD needs to break key supply to keep rebound alive\par \par \uc2 The dollar's recovery is entering a critical phase, as major technical supply needs to be overcome to keep the greenback's rebound alive. Overcoming this supply is key to the direction of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming sessions. The dollar retreated on Tuesday following Monday's rally, which saw investors flocking to the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to worries over China's COVID-19 flare ups, although cautious risk sentiment kept the greenback supported. The USD index, which tracks the dollar against a basket of six other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , registered a crucial close above the 107.537 Fibo on Monday, a 23.6% retrace of the 114.78 to 105.30 (September to November) drop. Now a daily USD index close above the tenkan line at 108.145 - the midpoint of the last 9 trading sessions - is needed to catapult gains to the 108.921 Fibo, a 38.2% retrace of the same 114.78 to 105.30 fall. USD's fate is pinned to the movement of the biggest components of the index: the euro, yen and pound. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3hZ589k"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 T