\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Expected to Strengthened Through 2023 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2022\par 17:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1104 ET - The yen is expected to strengthen to 130 per dollar next year, while USDJPY is unlikely to weaken beyond the recent level of 152, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  ' analysts say in a research note. "\b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  cap the topside volatility, but USDJPY's peak will ultimately depend on the extent of divergence between US and Japanese monetary policy," they write. The analysts estimate that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  can keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without selling its securities until 2Q or 3Q. By then, they say, the Fed's hiking cycle would have ended, "thereby reducing upward pressure on the USD." The USDJPY cross rises 0.4% to 140.43. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1100 ET - Mounting pressure on domestic prices and wages means that the odds of a soft landing for the New Zealand economy are getting slimmer, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  's acting Chief Economist \uc2 Michael Gordon\uc2  says. Persistently high inflation means that the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  's official cash rate is expected to rise further to a peak of 5% next year. While the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  began tightening early, the scale of the inflation problem means that it has a lot of work to do, he adds. Many borrowers have yet to feel the impact of higher interest rates. That will change a great deal over the next six to 12 months as they come due for refixing. It is more likely that \uc2 Westpac\uc2  would be forecasting an outright recession were it not for a recovery in international tourism, he says. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1025 ET - \uc2 Redrow\uc2  faces more risks as U.K. housing-market demand falters, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says, downgrading the house-builder to neutral from buy. Whilst a modest stabilization in mortgage rates should reduce the risk of a steep house-price decline, rising interest rates still are likely to reduce demand significantly, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. In addition, more lackluster recent trading points to a higher risk of cancellations and incentives across regional markets, which may have benefited most from strong market dynamics since the pandemic and are a core part of the group's exposure, the bank says. "We downgrade our rating on the stock to neutral as we believe the risk/reward now looks more balanced," \uc2 Citi\uc2  analyst Ami Galla says in a note. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1005 ET - Treasury yields recover some of last week's steep losses triggered by lower-than-expected inflation. The two-year is at 4.399% and the 10-year at 3.860%, both off recent highs. Fed Governor \uc2 Christopher Waller\uc2  warned Sunday that the battle against inflation will require higher interest rates. "The Fed is going to slow, not stop," AmeriVet's Gregory Faranello says in an analysis report. "But market moves following the CPI report [highlight] the sensitivity to inflation and the desire for reprieve from 2022." Economists surveyed by WSJ expect tomorrow's October PPI to have increased 0.4% on a monthly basis, the same as in September. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0929 ET - \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2  enters into exchange agreements with several institutional holders of its 2024, 2034 and 2044 notes. The note holders collectively exchanged roughly $123M aggregate principal amount of notes in exchange for 1.7 million shares of common stock. Following the transaction, which is expected to close around Nov. 16, the notes will be cancelled. \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2  has been trying to exchange debt for equity to shore up its financial position as it struggles to turn around its business. (suzanne.kapner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0913 ET - The unexpected strength of eurozone industrial production in September was largely due to the rebound in vehicles production and distortions affecting Ireland's data, Capital Economics' chief Europe economist Andrew Kenningham says in a note. "While vehicle output may continue to recover in the next few months, we doubt that it will compensate for a decline in energy-intensive sectors," the economist says. Eurozone production is likely to decline in the fourth quarter, according to \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . The manufacturing output purchasing managers index fell to just 43.8 in October and is consistent with eurozone industrial production falling by more than 5% year-on-year, the economist says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0851 ET - ABN Amro raises its forecast for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's deposit rate to 2.50% by the end of the first quarter of 2023, compared with its previous base case of a peak deposit rate of 2.0% in December 2022 followed by a pause, its analysts say. The deposit rate is currently 1.50%. ABN Amro now expects the \uc2 ECB\uc2  to start cutting interest rates again in the fourth quarter of 2023, Nick Kounis, head of financial markets and sustainability research, and Aline Schuiling, senior eurozone economist, write in a note. Behind the forecast revision, the analysts point to the "clear hawkish tilt" to the language of several \uc2 ECB\uc2  officials since the October policy meeting and stronger-than-expected economic data after last month's meeting. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0840 ET - Increased mortgage rates in the U.K. are likely to lead to a fall in discretionary spending, say \uc2 Barclays\uc2  analysts in a note. U.K. mortgage rates rose in late September after high market volatility due to government's debt-funded tax cuts which were reversed on October 17. "U.K. households are facing a double-whammy: rising costs for their mortgages and other loans, along with big rises in utility bills," the analysts say. "The refinancing risk for home loans seems likely to result in falls in discretionary spending and an erosion of savings." (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0814 ET - Oatly tumbles 10% premarket after the company reports weaker-than-expected earnings and cuts its sales outlook for the year. The Swedish oat milk maker says it is cutting overhead and head count tied to some of its corporate functions and overseas \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as taking other steps to simplify its organizational structure and supply chain strategy. The moves come as Oatly posted a bigger-than-expected 3Q loss and revenue that missed analyst estimates, which the company attributed to Covid-19 restrictions in Asia, production problems in the Americas, and ongoing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  challenges. Oatly now expects 2022 sales of between $700M and $720M, down from a prior forecast of between $800M and $830M. (jesse.newman@wsj.com; @jessenewman13)\par \par \uc2 0754 ET - European stocks rise after mixed Asia trading and ahead of an expected slightly lower U.S. open. The Stoxx Europe 600 gains 0.3%, the FTSE 100 and CAC 40 advance 0.4% and the DAX climbs 0.5%. Brent crude drops 1.1% to $94.92 a barrel. IG futures data show the Dow opening at 33701, versus Friday's close of 33747. Australian stocks closed flat and mainland Chinese and Japanese markets fell, though Hong Kong shares rose. Asian markets were mixed overnight as one \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  member, \uc2 Christopher Waller\uc2  , warned about putting too much weight on a single CPI number, IG says. "However, he did say the Fed might now think about a slower pace, something that may well give buyers fresh hope," IG analysts write. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 ET - The easing of supply bottlenecks boosted eurozone output in the capital goods sector, offsetting declines in energy-intensive industries, Oxford Economics' senior economist Paolo Grignani says in a note. Eurozone industrial production grew 0.9% on month in September, a touch higher than national data suggested, the economist says. Monday's data, together with an upwardly revised August print, brings output in the third quarter 0.5% above the previous quarter, according to Oxford Economics. However, the economic research firm expects eurozone industry to contract over winter. Oxford Economics' forecasts point to a eurozone recession between 4Q of this year and 1Q of 2023, led by a sizeable contraction of industrial output. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0720 ET - Brazil economists raised their estimates for the country's inflation in 2022 for the third consecutive week, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weekly survey. The median forecast for consumer price increased this year by 144 economists rose to 5.82% from 5.63% a week earlier. The forecast for next year was unchanged at 4.94%. Last week Brazil's statistics agency reported consumer prices rose 6.47% in October from a year earlier, down from 7.17% in the 12 months through September. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key lending rate to 13.75% earlier this year to try to slow inflation, and the survey shows economists expect the rate to stay there through the end of this year.(jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 14, 2022 11:04 ET (16:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221114eibe004uz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
