\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Firms as Markets Weigh Midterms -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par 19:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1309 ET - The dollar strengthens today as investors digest midterm elections. "Red wave is more muted than expected, and the market is disappointed," \uc2 Louis Navellier\uc2  says in a note. Meanwhile, Japan is selling short-term Treasury securities in an effort to prop up the yen, the chairman of Navellier & Associates says. Japan's one the biggest buyers of Treasury securities and Japanese selling pressure has helped to invert the yield curve, making it more difficult for the Fed to sell and reduce its balance sheet, he says. "The Fed never fights market rates, so if Treasury yields rise, then the Fed has to raise its key interest rates to get in synch with market rates." WSJ Dollar Index gains 0.5% and the dollar firms 0.4% against the euro and 0.5% against the yen. (jonathan.vuocolo@wsj.com; @jonvuocolo)\par \par \uc2 1240 ET - North American freight railroad traffic slips 0.7% for the week ended Saturday, with a 4.1% drop in the volume of intermodal units more than offsetting a 2.9% rise in carloads on 12 reporting US, Canadian and Mexican railroads, data from the \uc2 Association of American Railroads\uc2  shows. The weekly tally continues a choppy year for railroads, and with only eight weeks to go in the year, "we're still waiting for broad-based acceleration to end 2022 on a strong note," \uc2 Evercore\uc2  analysts Jonathan Chappell and Alex Johnson say. North American rail traffic is down 2% for the first 44 weeks of the year, \uc2 AAR\uc2  says. (colin.kellaher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1222 ET - A draft \uc2 European Parliament\uc2  report calls for an EU-wide temporary ban on the use of spyware such as Pegasus, made by the Israeli company NSO Group, unless a member country is transparent about how it is used and discloses the specific crimes it will deploy the technology to investigate. Sophie in 't Veld, a Dutch member of the \uc2 European Parliament\uc2  and chair of an investigatory committee into the use of spyware, says the use of such surveillance software violates EU laws. Reports about surveillance have caused uproar in Greece, Spain and other countries over the last year. (catherine.stupp@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1218 ET - Sterling's outlook remains downbeat despite talk of a potential breakthrough in the UK-EU dispute over Northern Ireland following the UK's exit from the EU, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. The \uc2 EU\uc2  has expressed optimism that an agreement over the controversial Northern Ireland protocol--effectively the regulatory framework for post-Brexit trade between the UK and NI--could be reached within weeks, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. Still, while time pressure to resolve issues surrounding the protocol is rising, those issues are only likely to have a clear effect on sterling as any related deadlines approach, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. "We're yet to be persuaded to alter our bearish view on the pound and maintain a three-month forecast of EUR/GBP 0.89," \uc2 Rabobank\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2  writes. EUR/GBP rises 0.9% to 0.8810. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1143 ET - The dollar could rise if data on Thursday show U.S. core inflation remained elevated in October as it will support expectations for further interest rate rises by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  , ING says. "An outcome in line with the consensus estimate of a 0.5% month-on-month rise in core inflation would likely keep expectations of Fed funds at 5% next year on track and keep the dollar supported," ING analyst \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2  says in a note. The Fed funds rate is currently in a 3.7%-4% range. The DXY dollar index could trade in a 109.50-110.50 range ahead of the data, Turner says. The DXY rises 0.4% to 110.050. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1100 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry\uc2  cannot continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange market to shore up the yen forever but it may not need to as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could recover further against the dollar, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. USD/JPY has recently eased, driven by a weaker dollar, and that trend may continue, \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Ulrich Leuchtmann\uc2  says in a note. If USD/JPY falls further, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be "incredibly lucky" that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will no longer be necessary before \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves become finite, he says. Otherwise, traders are likely to bet against the MOF once it becomes evident that reserves are coming to end. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1044 ET - The weather seems to be caught between two seasons as the National Weather Service issues a blizzard warning for parts of both Dakotas, while simultaneously issuing a hurricane warning for most of Florida's East Coast. "Heavy mountain snow today for the Sierra Nevada, Intermountain West, and northern Rockies," says the government agency's Weather Prediction Center, adding that down south "Tropical cyclone Nicole continues to approach the Florida peninsula and is forecast to strengthen to a hurricane before making landfall along the Florida east coast." The six-month-long Atlantic hurricane season officially ends Nov. 30. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1039 ET - Emerging markets are at risk of suffering a sudden stop of capital flows as developed world central banks aggressively raise interest rates, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  's Claudio Irigoyen and Christian Gonzales Rojas say in a report. They also list the Russia-Ukraine war and China's zero-Covid policy as potential causes for a "sudden stop," something they say is recurrent among EMs. "These episodes tend to last 8 months on average, but they have become less protracted in recent times," the authors write. "They are frequently accompanied by sharp depreciations in the exchange rate and reductions in economic activity." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1033 ET - The \uc2 Czech National Bank\uc2  's strategy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the koruna entails significant risks, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "\uc2 CNB\uc2  's still sizeable reserves affords it decent credibility, but only for as long as the market will find the medium-term inflation projections (to which \uc2 CNB\uc2  's policy response is calibrated) believable," \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Tatha Ghose says in a note. Last Thursday, the \uc2 CNB\uc2  left interest rates unchanged but said it would continue with \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent "excessive fluctuations" of the koruna. EUR/CZK rises 0.2% to 24.3800. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 ET - Germany's economic outlook is recently less pessimistic as natural gas prices are falling and the government has announced sweeping fiscal support, \uc2 Citi\uc2  's economist \uc2 Christian Schulz\uc2  says in a note. "Germany is less exposed than other euro area members to the new headwinds from financial volatility," Schulz says. However, \uc2 Citi\uc2  forecasts the German economy will contract 0.8% in 2023, following a 1.7% expansion in 2022. The energy price cap for households and businesses may cost between 2% and 3% of GDP worth of borrowing, but will reduce the peak-to-trough recession from 2.5% to 1.7% and add to growth in the subsequent upswing as well, Schulz says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 ET - Fixed-income investments again offer income opportunities to investors, but a successful strategy will require flexible switch between asset classes, while short duration is advised, Alex Wise, fixed income investment specialist at \uc2 UBS Asset Management\uc2  , says in a note. Global perspective is crucial, taking into account the changing response strategy of central banks, structural inflation and the impact on labor markets as global issues, he says. "In recent years, the fixed income market has been viewed as 'boring'; that has changed," he says. The good news is that risk premiums have become very attractive, while the bad news is that existing assets have been dragged down by monetary policy changes," he says. Duration is a measure of the sensitivity of bonds to interest rate changes. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0857 ET - Treasury yields rise amid prospects of a political gridlock. The two-year is at 4.668% and the 10-year at 4.145%. The Senate remains a toss-up, but "a narrow Republican victory in the House would spell the end for President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's domestic agenda, and the potential for further big shifts in fiscal policy," Capital Economics' Andrew Hunter says in a report. Hunter says pro-Trump candidates have disappointed, paving the way to the rise of a Republican candidate with higher chances to beat Biden in 2024, "potentially heralding the return of market-friendly tax cutting policies." Investors look ahead to Thursday's October CPI data. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 09, 2022 13:09 ET (18:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221109eib90042u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
