\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  to Lift Yen May No Longer be Necessary -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par 17:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1100 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry\uc2  cannot continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange market to shore up the yen forever but it may not need to as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could recover further against the dollar, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. USD/JPY has recently eased, driven by a weaker dollar, and that trend may continue, \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Ulrich Leuchtmann\uc2  says in a note. If USD/JPY falls further, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be "incredibly lucky" that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will no longer be necessary before \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves become finite, he says. Otherwise, traders are likely to bet against the MOF once it becomes evident that reserves are coming to end. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1044 ET - The weather seems to be caught between two seasons as the National Weather Service issues a blizzard warning for parts of both Dakotas, while simultaneously issuing a hurricane warning for most of Florida's East Coast. "Heavy mountain snow today for the Sierra Nevada, Intermountain West, and northern Rockies," says the government agency's Weather Prediction Center, adding that down south "Tropical cyclone Nicole continues to approach the Florida peninsula and is forecast to strengthen to a hurricane before making landfall along the Florida east coast." The six-month-long Atlantic hurricane season officially ends Nov. 30. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1039 ET - Emerging markets are at risk of suffering a sudden stop of capital flows as developed world central banks aggressively raise interest rates, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  's Claudio Irigoyen and Christian Gonzales Rojas say in a report. They also list the Russia-Ukraine war and China's zero-Covid policy as potential causes for a "sudden stop," something they say is recurrent among EMs. "These episodes tend to last 8 months on average, but they have become less protracted in recent times," the authors write. "They are frequently accompanied by sharp depreciations in the exchange rate and reductions in economic activity." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1033 ET - The \uc2 Czech National Bank\uc2  's strategy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the koruna entails significant risks, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "\uc2 CNB\uc2  's still sizeable reserves affords it decent credibility, but only for as long as the market will find the medium-term inflation projections (to which \uc2 CNB\uc2  's policy response is calibrated) believable," \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Tatha Ghose says in a note. Last Thursday, the \uc2 CNB\uc2  left interest rates unchanged but said it would continue with \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent "excessive fluctuations" of the koruna. EUR/CZK rises 0.2% to 24.3800. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 ET - Germany's economic outlook is recently less pessimistic as natural gas prices are falling and the government has announced sweeping fiscal support, \uc2 Citi\uc2  's economist \uc2 Christian Schulz\uc2  says in a note. "Germany is less exposed than other euro area members to the new headwinds from financial volatility," Schulz says. However, \uc2 Citi\uc2  forecasts the German economy will contract 0.8% in 2023, following a 1.7% expansion in 2022. The energy price cap for households and businesses may cost between 2% and 3% of GDP worth of borrowing, but will reduce the peak-to-trough recession from 2.5% to 1.7% and add to growth in the subsequent upswing as well, Schulz says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 ET - Fixed-income investments again offer income opportunities to investors, but a successful strategy will require flexible switch between asset classes, while short duration is advised, Alex Wise, fixed income investment specialist at \uc2 UBS Asset Management\uc2  , says in a note. Global perspective is crucial, taking into account the changing response strategy of central banks, structural inflation and the impact on labor markets as global issues, he says. "In recent years, the fixed income market has been viewed as 'boring'; that has changed," he says. The good news is that risk premiums have become very attractive, while the bad news is that existing assets have been dragged down by monetary policy changes," he says. Duration is a measure of the sensitivity of bonds to interest rate changes. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0857 ET - Treasury yields rise amid prospects of a political gridlock. The two-year is at 4.668% and the 10-year at 4.145%. The Senate remains a toss-up, but "a narrow Republican victory in the House would spell the end for President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's domestic agenda, and the potential for further big shifts in fiscal policy," Capital Economics' Andrew Hunter says in a report. Hunter says pro-Trump candidates have disappointed, paving the way to the rise of a Republican candidate with higher chances to beat Biden in 2024, "potentially heralding the return of market-friendly tax cutting policies." Investors look ahead to Thursday's October CPI data. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0853 ET - \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2  says it entered into a privately negotiated exchange agreement with a holder of some of its debt. The holder owns $9.5M of its 2023 notes and $22M of its 2044 notes. \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2  will issue roughly 2.8 million shares of common stock to the holder. Of that amount, 1.8 million shares are in exchange for the notes; 100,000 shares for unpaid interest and 900,000 shares for a cash payment of $3.5M. The proceeds will be used for general corporate purposes. \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2  has been struggling with falling sales and a cash crunch as it works to turn around its business. (suzanne.kapner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0848 ET - Cryptocurrencies fall amid concerns over the near collapse of crypto exchange FTX. FTX on Tuesday agreed a rescue deal with rival \uc2 Binance\uc2  after a surge in customer withdrawals sparked a liquidity crisis. Bitcoin drops 9.8% to $17,790, having reached a 24-month low of $17,266 earlier, according to CoinDesk. Ethereum slumps 18.5% to $1,211 after hitting a near four-month low of $1,139 earlier. "We will see a couple of days of high volatility and selloff, but the contagion will likely remain limited, and the survivors will carry on," \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2  analyst Ipek Ozkardeskaya writes. "Yet, investors would be, once again warned, that they are operating in a mostly non-regulated industry, and problems could pop up anytime." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0822 ET - Foreign investors in gilts require greater credibility surrounding the U.K's spending and taxation plans, say \uc2 MUFG\uc2  analysts in a note. Foreign investors are the second largest holders of gilts in the market, with 29.3%, behind the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  at 34.3%, and therefore have a significant influence on the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  's borrowing costs. "The U.K. is running a current account deficit of historic proportions and in order for that deficit to be financed at a lower rate of interest, greater credibility in fiscal policy is required" the analysts say. (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0804 ET - The eurozone economy remains on track for a recession amid high inflation, despite full gas storages and fiscal support, \uc2 Citi\uc2  's economist say in a note. Eurozone real GDP is expected to contract by 0.3% in 2023, after a 3.2% growth in 2022. Leading indicators continue to deteriorate, heralding a significant winter recession, they say. However, with gas storage more than 90% full, downside risks from the energy crisis have receded and gas prices are down, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. Significant new fiscal support for 2023 further reduces downside risks, \uc2 Citi\uc2  notes. On the other hand, rising borrowing costs due to the hawkish \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  complicate the outlook. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0748 ET - U.K. real GDP is expected to contract by 1.6% in 2023 and by 0.5% in 2024 as households are set to face a severe cost-of-living squeeze in the next two years with higher interest rates and limited fiscal support, \uc2 Citi\uc2  economist Benjamin Nabarro says in a note. Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  is expected to implement a significantly tighter fiscal stance than previously expected, while the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  will likely continue to tighten monetary policy aggressively in the near-term partly to compensate for the lingering damage to the country's credibility, Nabarro says. "Policy disruption in recent weeks now leave the U.K. facing a worse trade-off between growth, inflation and rates," he says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 09, 2022 11:00 ET (16:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221109eib9003im\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
