he Ukraine war's impact on the euro zone economy and increased U.S.-EZ yield differentials. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expectations have recently begun to shift, with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  now discussing smaller rate rises, though that may result in eventually higher yields. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was slower to respond to inflationary pressures than the Fed, but is now focussed on the fight against rising prices. \uc2 France's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief believes the \uc2 ECB\uc2  must not stop rate hikes before core inflation peaks. Thus yield differentials may support EUR/USD. The European economy is expected to fall into recession this winter as energy prices surge, but there are glimmers of hope as investor morale improved and German industrial output beat expectations on Monday. Technically, EUR/USD faces major resistance just above parity, with the 21-week moving average at 1.0044 and the October high at 1.0093. Weekly charts show a bullish cross of the 5-WMA through the 10. Daily charts show 5, 10 and 21-day moving averages, plus Bollinger bands all heading higher. A close above 1.0100 would open the door to a test of the 1.0457 falling 200-DMA, which has capped since June 2021. Yuan bears are right but must reckon with reopening risks Yuan bears are probably correct in betting that China's economy, thereby the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will continue to struggle into 2023. But Friday's USD/CNH plunge on China reopening chatter ignored dollar-positive factors, and might shake their conviction. The offshore yuan has recovered 1.9% against the USD from a 7.3746 record low on Oct 25. While it's still down 12.1% year-to-date, the speed of the USD selloff likely spooked some. Positioning may be to blame, but it also reflects a stubborn desire to bet on China's reopening happening sooner rather than later. An unexpected drop in October